      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>ALCOHOL AND LIBERTY.</head>
        <p>ALCOHOL AND LIBERTY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln a leiter in your issue of January 10, Mr. Benjamin T. Parkes says that in order to protect women ,and helpless children from drunken and improvident, husbands and fathers we should exert our influence towards the aboli- tion of the trade in alcoholic drinks.</p>
        <p>The belief that drunkenness can be suppressed by prohibiting or abolishing the drink traffic has been thoroughly falsified by experience. The so-called " Prouibition States" in America afford clear proof of this.. The law is either broken or evaded; and there are few things more injurious to a nation, from a moral point of view, than that the law of the country should be regarded with contempt.</p>
        <p>Drunkenness is undoubtedly a great evil, but there are others quite as bad, or even worse. If it were possible to put down drunkenness by force, it does not follow that the man or the State would be any better. Indeed, no mere negative reform is of much moral value, s&amp; sobriety could have no moral value unless it was the free choice of the individuals them- selves, not something imposed upon them from without. "Better England free than England sober."</p>
        <p>Temperance, after all, is only a part, a neces- sary part, Of character, and if we aim at pro- ducing character, instead of imposing unreason- able restrictions upon freedom, men will realise that the liberty to please themselves is always subject to the duty they owe to their wives, their helpless children, and the State generally. EDW. TUCKER. January 14th, 1914.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Parkes asks me what value I place on the figures from New Zealand, which tells us that under "No licences" drinking does decrease. My reply is that I place the same value that I do on the returns from several cities in Maine, 'U.S.A., where they have had, not only "No licence," but the absolute pro- hibition of the sale of alcoholic liquor for 65 years, and I find that the arrests for drunken- ness are 25 per 1,000 of the inhabitants, and no public-houses. Mark the extraordinary fact, no salogns—the abolition of which our good friend Mr. Parkes imagines will cleanse human nature of all its vices—at least that is what the prohibitionist will tell you, and yet there still remains, after half a century and more, cruelty, crime, and, what is worse, an amount of hypocrisy that cannot be equalled anywhere. He says that I fall into the "rut of personal liberty?' Is that his opinion of the spirit and feeling that has made the Anglo-Saxon race, with all its faults, :what it is? True, we are losing some of our self-respect and independ- ence. We are more inclined to be "spoon-fed", as Lord Rosebery once said, but there is an awakening to the fact that the State has no right to interfere with the individual, beyond a certain point, and that it is good for the State that the individual should demand a respect for his personal liberty; for only on the self-respect that springs from liberty can you rear a race of men and women that will be upright, strong and sober—not the hypocritical sobriety of com- pulsion—but the true sobriety that comes from freewill and choice.</p>
        <p>G. E. HOLME.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>ARMENIAN CHRISTIANS: POSITION MPROVING, BUT HELP NEEDED.</head>
        <p>ARMENIAN CHRISTIANS: POSITION MPROVING, BUT HELP NEEDED.</p>
        <p>' ' ,"7</p>
        <p>,</p>
        <p>T</p>
        <p>Sir,—May we ask your permission once more to bring the needs of the Armenian Christians to the notice of your readers, who have so kindly assisted •us in our efforts on their behalf?</p>
        <p>So many of the breadwinners are gone—taxa- tion is abnormally heavy—the winter cold is severe and work very hard to obtain—illness, the</p>
        <p>result of hunger an privation, is prevalent, for the people are in a chronic, state of abject poverty.' Miss Davies, the faithful head nurse at the Adana International Hospital writes, late in December: "Operation cases have literally poured iti; now that the cold weather has come poor meh who have come to the city to seek work fall victims to void and exposure The warm garments you sent out some time ago are most useful."</p>
        <p>From trfa we learn that : "Peace has given a little business confidence and commerce is somewhat better, but there are still Many men unemployed, who are quite without food, having nothing with which to purchase it." The 111is- aionaries there are doling out the relief funds as economically as possible, but those available fall far short of what is needed, and they plead with us for at least ..e.50 this winter with which to help the worst cases.</p>
        <p>And until these people become sufficiently educated in industrial ways, and the Govern- ment is able to afford them sufficient protection to give them opportunity to support themselves, these calls for .aid must come-, and it must be ours to answer them. And a similar cry is heard from all the stations where the good missionaries are fighting evil and wrong and suffering., Their reward is in the wonderful development of the boys and girls they have taken under theit wings--development spiritual, moral, mental and physical. These boys and girls come from the most miserable surround- ings, but blossom like flowers under the train- ing of love and care bestowed on them, and will prove, in God's good time, the leaven of their country, which " will leaven the whole lump." Is it not well worth while to help to this end? These people, too, "must needs pass througl- Samaria" before they reach the sweet shores of Galilee, whither their weary feet are journey- ing. The way must necessarily be rough and hard, but shall we not try to create for tllem some restful little oases in this desert, where they may gather fresh strength for the toilsome journey? We, too, each one of us, have our Samaria to tread, and how thankfully we grasp the hands of friends stretched out to give us help and sympathy. The friends of these poor Armenian Christians are comparatively few, and it there- fore behoves those who realise their brotherhood to come generously to their succour. have sat so long in "darkness and in the shag° .v of death." Let Us help them into the light. (Mrs.) MARY HICKSON, Hon. See.</p>
        <p>47, Victoria-street, Westminster, S.W.</p>
        <p>Donations should he forwarded to the Hon. Treasurer, Mr. E. Wright Brooks, and cheques should be crossed "London County and West- minster Bank."</p>
      </div>
