      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>DISESTABLISH MENT AND DISENDOW-</head>
        <p>DISESTABLISH MENT AND DISENDOW-</p>
        <p>MENT.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l must protest very strongly against the mis-representation by Mr. Stidston-Broadbent, in his letter of the 14th inst., of portions of my last letter to you. Mr. Stidston-Broadbent is much too eager to claim that I acknowledged a mistake in claiming Lord Halifax as a supporter of Disestablishment. I did nothing of the kind, but I did point out that Mr. Broadbent, in his letter of December 30th, stated quite clearly that the reason Lord Halifax was not in favour of the present Bill was because Clause 3 re- stricted the Disestablishment of the Church of England in Wales May I point out to your readers the very considerable difference between Lord Halifax not being in favour of the present Bill for the Disestablishment of the Church of England in Wales, and his not being in favour of Disestablishment? The word "Disestab- lishment" covers a larger field than merely the Church of England in Wales, and because Lord Halifax does not favour the Present Bill for Wales it does not follow that Ire would not sup- port a more drastic Bill in the future, either for Wales or England. This quarrel with the pre- sent Bill is simply that it does not disestablish the Church completely, but still leaves a certain amount of State control, as Mr. Broadbent kindly mentioned in his letter of December 30th. I think I am entitled to at least having my letters properly read. Mr. Broadbent is quite at liberty to doubt my abilities, but his mere assertion that Henry VIII. did not establish a Church, or usurp the Papal Authority, is worth nothing. I was challenged in the first instance by this person to produce my authorities, and I believe I have gone the full length of courtesy in doing so, and laying before your readers the plain historical facts, not one of which has Mr. Broadbent been able to shake, nor has he quoted a single Church authority. His letters are merely a string of assertions, made apparently on his own initiative. Your readers must judge for themselves who has stated his facts in the most convincing manner, and I would suggest they check them for themselves.</p>
        <p>The really silly talk whether I am a Roman Catholic and a Liberal is scarcely worth notice, and would not have received my attention but that I believe it to be a thinly veiled Party attempt to embroil me with my own party. Mr. Stidston-Broadbent argues in his letter of December 30th that I am a Low Churchman, in his letter of January 14th that I am a High Churchman, and, finally, in his last letter, asserts that I am a Roman Catholie and not a Liberal—really an excellent example of what Mr. Asquith called "incurable sloppiness of argument." Mr. Broadbent, in the last paragraph of his letter, insists in thrusting upon me the honour of speaking for the Liberal party in 'Lymm, in spite of my explanation, and also in spite of the fact that he was not present at the meeting when I spoke, and cannot, therefore, be aware of what the Chairman's remarks were before call- ing upon me. Really controversialists ought to be careful to verify their facts! The offer of a conversation (not a controversy) is most kind, but my experience of Tory conversations is that they consist chiefly of Toy assertions, to which the listener is expected to hearken in silent wonder at the marvels exposed to his under- standing. I would like to point out that run- ning all through Mr. Broadbent's letters is that spirit of—may I say it ?—smug satisfaction that whatever a Diocesan Church organiser says it must be correct. Whatever may be said on the past history, there is not the slightest doubt that at present, and at all elections for many years, there has been, and still is, a persistent and sustained demand by the Welsh nation for the Disestablishment of the Church of England in Wales.</p>
        <p>REGINALD BARKER.</p>
        <p>Oak Lea, Statham, January 21st, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>INCREASING RENTS.</head>
        <p>INCREASING RENTS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Rathbone's flattering statements are likely to make me conceited. He says: "his powers of perception are remarkable, his imagination great." I am "naive," a modern "Don Quixote," of "tilting and slashing" fame. In the melodrama I am indeed wonderful. I am pictured in the backwoods of America in full feather and war paint, building up straw men and tomahawking them in most valiant style, all my own, and knocking them down to my own satisfaction and glorification. ,Add to these the statements made by "Fair Rent," namely, "The wily correspondent is no respecter of name or nom-de-plume, consequently it requires all the abilities of a Scotland Yard expert, rather than a rating expert, to draw him out," and a modi- fied pen-picture of my abilities lies before you. Now, I suggest that it would be a waste of real talent to accept that post to run works night and ,clay and thus dodge the rate collector. I think it is but reasonable that I should have a word in the choice of my own situation, and affirm that a man possessing the afore-men- tioned abilities is a good comedian and a born nolifieian, and on the basis of present politics would be a fine asset to any political party or Government, and I here and now offer myself, seeing that the Chancellor might resign, as a candidate for the possible vacancy at £4,500 a year less than the price now paid for talents little above my own, if my opponents are to be believed. Mr. Rathbone says: "He has been arguing that he is entitled to a rent of 6s. 4d. a week on a house which has only been returning him 55., on the ground that he has to pay ..t4 log. 10d. in rates, plus, of course, 18s. for paving. Now, does he really pa Y ..t4 10s. 10d, in rates on this house which has been rented at 58. per week, even though he charges 6s. 4d. rent, or does he only pay £2 9s. 3d, until the next assessment in Nfareli, 1915ra My answer to this is that the owners, so far as I know, are now aware that some time must elapse before the new valuation takes place, and would who, by nbythreaiassisnugmthe rents, would lose the to sg. have raised their rents frbm ss. 3(1, expect the ten taon tenants vweh their are rneonwpt: pro aanyi si thatendg 01;5. s4v. da9.lduo na the hao twh I not take place for some time. Therefore date the new valuation takes place, unless they agitate for all rents below 6s. to come under the scale of composition. The property owners have been told that this cannot be done without pre- seating Probably this m whn but h , t vent. seßialltinwgill abeßiplul to shed f the people is raised high enough, voice o before.e etneo The advance of rents means an increase of funds for the Corporation, increase or cause a reductionand will prevent an rates. Here-, again, fi m in the general can well afford t manufacturer gains, and tage. o pay something for the advan- , Mr. Rathbone says: "And builders in ug" " carg ington , g are there not hi n a ss. 9d. per week, in streets h rent of w exactly the same kind on the where hm :houses of. side are being let at SS. 4d., the latter returning quite a satisfactory profit to the landlord?" I have searched Warrington for the streets where houses on the one side- are rented at sg. Pd. and owned by a speculative; builder, and where</p>
        <p>the houses opposite are rented at ss. 4d. and owned by an apparent philanthropist ; houses, that is, which are supposed to be of equal value. I have now secured the necessary in- formation, which I place before the readers of this paper. The ss. 4d. houses were erected by Warrington builders, now out of business, and cost ,about £145 each house. The builders had borrowed ..t.130 by way of mortgage on each uouse. When the laud valuation took place, owing to the low rent, the houses were valued at a considerable sum below the mortgage. The mortgagees took fright, went into possession, and ultimately sold the property at less than their own mortgage. I found a second and third and many other streets where the,same condi- tions prevailed. The builders and past owners had lost their hard-earned money. Surely this is a discredit to England, and the -politicians who so generously framed the People's Budget, which was to in- crease the builders' trade, and cheapen the artisans' dwellings. Each reader can see for himself why some owners can, for the prNentf let their houses at less- than the speculative builder, and still 'have the same return on their capital. The builder .is determined to save his property from the hand of the exploiters, and the only way is to increase the value of his security, to the average earning capacity of like securities.</p>
        <p>Who ought to receive the benefit? I say the man who has run the gauntlet of the Budget. Who ought to help him to do it? I affirm the people who have unwittingly caused the dis- aster. . .</p>
        <p>The Chancellor has promised relief; let him give it, and do it now, without loss of time Mr. Rathbone asks me to make a stand on behalf of the tenants. I am doing a greater work for them. I am showing them the neces- sity for doing something for themselves, for, after all, only the voice of the multitude carries weight in these democratic days. GEORGE SMITE!. The Limes, Orford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>31- IN</head>
        <p>31- IN</p>
        <p>CHINA SOAP TRADE.</p>
        <p>Incorrect Version of Our Latest Enterprise In the East.</p>
        <p>" TILE FACTS."</p>
        <p>We recently published a report from Shanghai dealing with a scheme for a huge com- bine and large works in China for the manu- facture of soap. That account, as we reported, w'as denied by several of the firms named in connection with the scheme.</p>
        <p>A recent message from Shanghai says : According to cabled advices received here from London, an incorrect version of the latest Brit- ish enterprise in China has been published in England. The facts are as follow :7. ,</p>
        <p>Negotiations have just been completed for a combination, of several large firms for the pur- pose of manufacturing soap and allied products in China. The negotiations have been con- ducted here by Sir William Lever, head of the firm of Lever Bros., Mr. Fred Brock, of the firm of Brunner, Mond and Co., and Mr. E. S. Little, general manager of the latter firm in• Canada. The other companies interested ars those of Messrs. Gossage, Messrs. trosfield, and the Eras- mic Company, together With all the associated companies of the above. The scheme involves the largest combination of British capital on record. The necessary new capital will be put up by the firms men- tioned, no new shares being issued publicly. The combine will erect a factory at Zangtzepoo (a suburb of Shanghai, where there are already many cotton mills), a site having already been purchased at a cost of about £llO,OOO, near the municipal electric power station. The ground is on the banks of the Whangpoo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WIDNES WATER EXTENSION SCHEME.</head>
        <p>WIDNES WATER EXTENSION SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Mr. Oppenheim, Town Clerk of Widnes, has received a communication from the Local Gov- ernment Board stating that they are prepared to give their sanction for the full amount of £19,170 for the extension of the Corporation's Waterworks undertaking at Netherley. It will be remembered that the Widnes Corporation 'applied for sanction to borrow this sum, but owing to Mr. Pierce's opposition on behalf of the Liverpool Corporation, Mr. Hetherington, the inspector, said he would like to submit the case of the Widnes Corporation and the Liverpool opposition to his Board. The Board's decision still holds the Liverpool agreement good, and this also affects St. Helens and Warrington, who have sitnilar powers under the Vyrnwy Act.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>AFTER LONG SUFFERING</head>
