      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>WASTED BRAINS.</head>
        <p>WASTED BRAINS.</p>
        <p>A Scheme Proposed to Provide University Scholarships.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>INITIAL STEPS TAKEN.</head>
        <p>THE INITIAL STEPS TAKEN.</p>
        <p>The wasted brains of promising Warrington children are likely to be encouraged towards development in the future by the provision of University scholarships. Hitherto Warrington lagged behind other towns in this respect, and it was not realised until the matter was first pointed out by "The Examiner" a few months ago. : Since then the subject has been under the consideration of the School Management Sub- committee, who, at the meeting of the Educa- tion Committee on Monday, recommended 1— "That a sub-committee, consisting of Mr. Alfred J. Jolley, Councillor Henry Roberts, Mr. John Upson, the Rev. Neville Wilson and the Chair- man of the Committee (the Rev. Canon Steven- son), be appointed to consider the question of the provision of scholarships to Universities."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>ANOTHER LINK* IN THE CHAIN.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER LINK* IN THE CHAIN.</p>
        <p>Canon MORLEY STEVENSON said another link in the educational chain in Warrington was in course of forging by the scheme now proposed. He thought all would feel it was overdue, but would be glad it was now coming. The scheme, in greater detail, would come before the committee at a future meeting. The conception of the scheme was very largely due to the interest Councillor Roberts had taken in the matter, and if anything further was to be said that night he would rather it be said by Councillor Roberts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>FINDING BRAIN QUALITY.</head>
        <p>FINDING BRAIN QUALITY.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS said I not intend at that stage to go into any details, because the sub-committee, appointed to prepare the scheme, would submit their proposals to the School Management Sub-committee, and, if approved, these would be submitted to the Education Com- mittee, and afterwards to the Education Authority. He believed very strongly that the quality of brain to be found amongst the children of the working class in any borough or , district was quite as good as the brain quality of any other class. (Hear, hear.) He believed also that until they set out to find such brain quality they would not run across it, and he thought hither- to lack of • opportunity Perhaps had been the only difficulty which had 'prevented them from bringing that brain quality to he front and making the most of it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>For the Poor Children.</head>
        <p>For the Poor Children.</p>
        <p>_ He thought the time had come when they should make it possible for the hest brains tq enter the Universities and obtain the benefit of the culture and education Oily to be obtained, such conditions. He therefore hoped the scheme would be developed in ,such a way that the scholarships—whatever the number may. finally be agreed upon—would be bestowed only ,upon those children, ,who, having qualified, of course, by competition, whose parents were quite unable to assist them to a University career.</p>
        <p>The Material to Develop. The object I have in view, he said, is to make it possible for the poorest boy—or girl as well— in this town, by this method, to qualify for the highest position, professional or otherwise, which education and brain power entitles them to obtain.</p>
        <p>He believed it would stimulate generally the cause of education, because up to now they had the material, but now the effect of the educa? tional course given in the Secondary School, and the transference from the elementary schools, by scholarships, to the Secondary School and the Grammar School of some of the brightest chil- dren had given an amount of material to draw upon when they came to hold the first test exam- ination for the scholarahip if the Education Committee and Council decide upon such a scheme.</p>
        <p>He commended the scheme as a matter of sound policy to the committee's favourable con- sideration, and he hoped, as he felt certain would be the fact, they would he practically unanimous on the scheme even if it might be necessary to slightly alter the details. Vim INTO THE SCHEME. ' The lima associated himself very cordially with the scheme. They had all recognised for some time the serious difficulty which their poorer people had to contend with in attempting to climb to the top of the educational ladder, so what he wished to impress upon the commit- tee was to put some vim into their consideration of the question so that it might, if the scheme was suitable to the committee, come into opera- tion at the earliest opportunitY, because the loss of,every .year, in his opinion, was a serious matter.</p>
        <p>that the educational scheme may be brought to</p>
        <p>Alderman ABEN He had always said there was a weakness in L ONCETATLapproved of the proposal. WEAKNESS. their local education, in that they had not been able to complete the educational ladder right up to the University. There were scores of lads in Warrington who had brains quite equal to the highest and brains drabeinsst in the land. It seemed to him, from the tniai uet they brainsoan,thiaoa dal n achlpointan nsne chance, and m view, it sini t inexhaustiblet not gold mine. The educational aetses wise to give these complete until it made the way clear for the humblest child to go Education forward t o Committee mitte ew University.oud lHe was sure the welcome any pro- posal with open arms that would lead to such a course.</p>
        <p>, Many of their great men had sprung from a humble origin, but by sheer ability and per- severance had forged ahead and he wanted to make it easier for others to follow in their foot- steps. There were many children who had brain power, but had no o pportunity. What was wanted was to give them the opportunity. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>OTHER MEMBERS' VIEWS. Mr. HoLmAN KixorioN said he cordially asso- ciated himself with what had been said on the scholarship question. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY sold he did not know what tapping a gold mine was, or what a gold mine was like, but he appreciated the sentiment in Alderman Bennett's simile. He thought the scheme would be appreciated. Councillor PEMBERTON, while not objecting to the scheme, thought it could not be compared to tapping a gold mine. There was no certainty that if they sent boys to a University they would do anything marvellous. They must not give the impression that immediately a youth had passed through a University foftune was at his feet. There were many University men not getting more than ..t2 or £3 per week. Councillor Roasars said he was personally obliged to the Mayor coming to support the scheme for University scholarships, and he was anxious the first scholarship should be awarded during Councillor Peacock's mayoralty. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>The Committee's proposals were adopted un. animously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>PITIFUL SIGHT WITH ECZEMA.</head>
        <p>PITIFUL SIGHT WITH ECZEMA.</p>
        <p>Evendons-lane, East Heath, wokingham, Derks., ,Eng.—" When my son was two weeks old his little head started with a sort Of soapy eruption on top. This gradually became worse. They told me it was a very bad form of eczema. For three weeks we never knew what it was to go to bed with him. llis poor little head was like a ball of fire. I then prayed for him to die, as it seemed wicked to see him suffer. .For three months it was like this. His head was one mass of running sores from the back right over to his eyebrows. It was a pitiful sight to see the poor little chap rub his head on the pillow till he rubbed the cover in littes. "Then I decided to try Cutictira Soap and Ointment. After the first application he fell off to sleep, a thing he had not had for .many a long hour. His head was not so hot and did I not irritate so much, so I bought more Cuticura Soap and Ointment, and still kept on using them and now •he is completely cured.", (Signed) Mr.. E. Spratley, July 22, 1913. .Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post-free. Ad- dress: P. Newbery and Sons, 27, Charterhouse- square. London. or Potter Drug and Chem. Corp.,ol3oston, U.S.A.</p>
        <p>THE BISHOP OF ST. DAVID'S: I do not counsel violence, my lord, but couldn't we manage to hang it up for a bit? [Suggested by the Bishop of St. David's letter to the ,` Times" on the Welsh Church Disestablishment Bill, Friday, January 9, 1914—a letter in which he hints that the Parliament Act does not prevent the peers from postponing till the very end of the Session any consideration of the Welsh Church Bill.]</p>
        <p>By permission of the" Westminster Gazette."</p>
        <p>mont-street, Willow Park, Sowerby Bridge, a dyer's labourer, was charged with loitering with intent to commit a felony. Sergt. Holland stated that at 7,15 p.m. on January 12 he was 'in Sankey-street, when he saw the prisoner act- ing in a suspicious manner near Messrs. Dut- ton's shop, where there were a number of ladies Iwith satchels. Prisoner followed one of these ladies to Boots' chemists shop, and thence to I Hallet's and Messrs. Hodgkinson's, and at each 'place he acted suspiciously. Later he went into Winwick-street and loitered round Mr. Moun- field's shop, where there were two ladies, one with a satchel. When prisoner was arrested there was no money in is possession—Prisoner stated that he had no intention of stealing, and after previous convictions had been proved, prisoner was sent to prison for 14 days.</p>
        <p>A;VVOMAN'S OFFENCE.—Mary Jane Latham, 73, Bostock-street, was charged with. stealing bed- ding and clothing articles, valued 65., the pro- perty •of Mrs. William Toogood.— Prisoner pleaded not -guilty.—The evidence showed that when the prosecutrix was ill in bed she sent washing to the prisoner, who did not return all the articles, but sold them for 2s. When prisoner was charged she replied "I will have to plead guilty, that is all."—She was fined 305., with the alternative of one month's imprison- ment. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>RAID ON THE MARKET. Petty Pilferers Discovered.</p>
        <p>For the past twelve months several of the Market stallholders have suffered from petty pilfering by children. The police have made several arrests, and the sequel was heard at the Police Court on Wednesday, vihen five young persons were charged in connection with the robberies.</p>
        <p>In the -first case Thomas and Albert Edward Greenwood, aged 15 and 12 respectively, of 51, Fairclough's-avenue, were charged with steal- ing during the past ten months three pairs of boots and a number of articles of clothing, to the value of 19s. 2d., the property of Bernard Abrahamson, 44, Great Ducee-street, Manches- ter- The boy William was also charged with stealing boots and articles of clothing from the the stalls of Jack Shiphird and others. The mother of the boys, Sarah 4ane Greenwood, was charged with receiving the goods knowing them to have been stolen. Th 4 Chief Constable said it would appear that for the past twelve Months the boys had been systematically stealing from the Market and taking the things , home. The mother had dis- posed of them in pledge. She' was a regular customer at the shop of Messrs. jackson, pawn- brokers, and the manager was not suspicious until Wednesday, of last week, and as a result the police were acquainted. The woman was interviewed by the police as to how she came into possession of the boots, when she replied that she had bought them for 4s. 11d, from a shop at Market Gate. The charges represented only a small portion of the property. The two boys had been systematically stealing for some months. In reply to the Mayor, the elder boy said he stole the boots to bring them homes for the children.</p>
        <p>The father of the boys said he was very sorry, for what had happened. He was innocent of the whole affair.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: Have you treated your wife as you ought to have done. It is all very well for you to come here and say you know nothing about it.</p>
        <p>The Pather : I am not always in the posiEibn to be with them.</p>
        <p>- - A sister of the woman, who had journeyed from Plymouth, said that if the children had had a proper father they would have never turned out like that.</p>
        <p>The Mayor, addressing the mother: I think you are to blame for your children being brought into this position. We are sorry we can't deal with your husband as well. You will be fined £2, or one month. The boy Albert would receive four strokes with the birch, and William would be fined 108. including costs. Elizabeth Ann Cousins, aged 13, of 24, King- street, was charged with stealing between 24th December and 16th January 18 pairs of boots, valued ..t2 2s. 3d., from the stall of Bernard Abrahamson. Laura Ann Cousins, mother of the girl, was charged with receiving the pro- perty. The Chief Constable said that on Saturday morning last P.C..Turner was making inquiries at the pawnshop of Messrs. Stubbs, when the manager called his attention to a pair of boots, apparently new, which had had the soles partly dirtied. It appeared that the girl had been stealing the boots and her mother had been dispoising of them at second-hand shops and pawnbrokers. When arrested by the officer the girl made a statement to the effect that she had told her mother that she had taken them from a stall in the Market, and that her mother had sent her to get others. P.C. Turner stated that when he arrested the mother she was drinking beer in the house. A daughter of the prisOner said she thought her mother's downfall was due to poverty. She had been left for seven years without any his- band, with eight children, and only two were able to work for her. The Chief Constable said the woman had been once previously before the Court on a charge of stealing, but was discharged. The Mayor said the magistrates had no sera_ pathy with the mother • she had had her chance and had abused it. She would have to go to gaol for one month with hard labour. The girl would be placed under probation for twelve months. Margaret Forster (12), 14, Foundry street and Polly Taylor (14), 3, Buckley's_ Yaru, were charged with stealing seven pairs of boots, valued 10s., the property of Bernard Abraham- son. The girl Taylor said she pledged the boots after Forster had stolen them. They admitted spending the money on toffee. Abrahamson said that there was a small hole in the stall sufficiently large ,to allow a child's band to get in, and the boots had been stolen in that way. The hole had been there since ine, hilt was now fastened up. The Mayor: It serves you right for allowing the stall to remain in that condition so f long. The girls were placed under probation or twelve months.</p>
        <p>The magistrates ordered the retention of the pledges until the full amount was paid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. Before Mr. A. J...Jolley_(in the chair) and</p>
        <p>Mr. W. T. Williams. WOREHOESE PORTER ASSAULTED.—WiIIiam Cot- ton, no fixed address, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Guardian-street, and also with assaulting Thomas Boothman4porter at the Workhouse.—lt was alleged that pris- oner presented himself for admission to the casual ward. fie was in a drunken condition, and ,when told to come again he kicked Booth- man on the leg. P.C. Chrimes took Cotton, who was very drunk, into custody.—Prisoner, who made his fiftieth appearance, was sent to prison for three months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before "Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes (in the chair) and T. Pemberton, and Col. Edelsten. Wiuns's LITTLE GAME.—Willie Green, 3, Bel-</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Messrs. H. Thornton, C. Jackson Holmes and W. T. Williams.</p>
        <p>A STORY OF CoAL.—Mary Langley, 6, Tolley's- yard, was remanaed for a week on a charge of stealing i-cwt.. of coal, valued 9d., from the Arpley Sidings, the property of the L. and N.. W. Railway Company. The Chief Constable said the prisoner, when arrested, gave a wrong name and address, and it was necessary that the police should make inquiries as to her pre- vious character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. W. Roberts (in the chair), A. J. Jolley, W. T. Williams and Dr. Naden.</p>
        <p>A BOY AND THE CANDLES.—WiIIiam Trigger, giving an address at 19, Barbauld-street, was charged with stealing three candles, valued 20., the property of James Drinkwater.—The evi- dence showed that on Saturday afternoon prose- cutor placed three candles inside a carriage, and later found that they had disappeared. He reported the matter to the police, and prisoner was arrested. Prisoner at first denied all know- ledge of the candles, but subsequently admitted it.—The boy's father stated that his son had been living at a common lodging-house since Christmas, but he' was willing to take his son home.—Prisoner agreed to go, and he was bound over for 12 months.</p>
        <p>FRIDAY. Before Messrs. S. Wright (in the chair), j. Jolley, W. Furness, T. Locker and G. Pilling, and Aldermen Smethurst, Bolton and Burton. iodging-honse in THE COPPER BOlLER.—Fred.—Fred Moore, a stranger to the town, who lived at a Ship-yard, was remanded until next Wednes- day on a charge of stealing a copper boiler, eon., valued 285., the property of Mr. John Lunt, tractor, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Hiilli) (I) . 07..."-,,:..,::i...:....n'ten,</head>
        <p>Hiilli) (I) . 07..."-,,:..,::i...:....n'ten,</p>
        <p>CHINESE'. CHILDREN. -</p>
        <p>Their Delight in National and Ragions</p>
        <p>Festivals,</p>
        <p>As in other lands, the various national and religions festivals are a source of never-ending delight to the children of China. On New rear's Eve red paper pasted on the lintels of the door and . sweeping and washing remove the memories of the old year's dirt. In the morning there are fireworks everywhere; new clothes are donned, a handful of small coins jingles in the pocket of the bright new coat, shops are closed, gambling goes with a swing, cake men hawk all kinds of brilliantly coloured • cakes and sweetmeats, and the day ends with more ieasting, fun and crackers. But such a festival as this, and that of the Feast of Lanterns, when the streetk of the towns and villages present an extraorctinary sight with their multitude of quaint and artistic lamps, are for all, both young and old. There are, however, in some cities special festival@ for the children about the beginning of the summer. At this time the youngsters are taken to a temple and solemnly dedicated to the presiding lady. For the payment of a small sum to the nuns in charge of the tem.. pie, the little ores are adopted by the god- dess. Once adapted it is the duty of the child to attend every year till the age of six- teta when the payment of another sum of money frees him from the duty, pleasant or otherwise, and also releases the goddess of any responsibility for the crowds she has ac- cepted as her special charges. Dresses of the brightest hues are worn on these occa- sions and if the parents of the child can af- ford it the journey to the temple is made on f horseback. Parent and child promenade the temple bowing to tie altar, burning incense sticks and offering candles. One or two of the festivals mig benefit of ht be intr. duced into this country for the the underpaid echoolmasters whose duty it i in war of right and part of the to bring up the young duty. I refer to those days 'when ceremony consists in making the teacher a present of money. On the a e fifth day offering. but fifth moon the teacher g but as he has to give a fan in return, perhaps! lii the eighth month s similar gift and has to ac- knowledge it by Paying back in moon-cakes. We have a suspicion that the teacher gener- ally manages to get the best of the bar. Tilere are Unfortunately many black spots h., the lives of some of the young Celestials. Little girls have been placed where tigers could devour them and others have been droWned like kittens at their birth. When drought comes, there are long periods with little or no food; when too much rain . falls grain is spoilt and houses swept away. There are village fights when fathers are killed and poverty seizes the home. There is hard.werk at an early age in the fields, and small girls are often sold as elaves. if the father smokes opium the revenues dry up, and poverty and wretchedness become the sad lot of the young &lt;msg.</p>
        <p>-4V '-44` 4</p>
