        <p>MILK-FED CHICKENS.</p>
        <p>A Market Demand Which Might Be Better Catered For.</p>
        <p>ART OF EARLY HATCHING,</p>
        <p>211„ete XS now a fairly good demand .for milk -",_`eaens, that is, well fattened, tiny birds, `Teat 6°z. each, nicely plucked and sent to high- :TlBB Poulterers in attractive style. Prices for "vie vary from is. 6d. to 28. each; but, so far, InllY a few of the best elms poulterers seem able to dispose of them. I hope the trade may ltriprove rapidly; it is just a business many MePie ran manage, and if present Prices are realised then there should be money in it. Those who cannot sell their chickens at six weeks can run them on until they get about 31b. weight. I have seen them sell at 9s. per couple in March II . cross-breds are being reared, and anything Like • these prices can be obtained, the pullets should be cleared off along with the male birds; always take a decent profit when offered. Many bold out, but it is not a business-like policy.</p>
        <p>AIDS TO PRODUCTIVENESS. Suburban poultry-keepers should place all waste table scraps in a large iron pan, and before warming up add butchers' scraps, which Can be obtained for a few pence, and make a desirable addition to the stockpot. The liquid Should be seasoned with a little salt and a tea- spoonful of poultry mustard added for every Six birds. The meals or cereals used are im- portant. One part each of bran, Indian meal, and middlings or sharps, is excellent, and may he varied by using pea meal and Sussex ground ' oats with the bran and sharps. Barley meal in moderation makes a nice change, hat care must be, exercised, and directly the birds appear too heavY the bran should be increased and cereals reduced. For the evening feed the best sound grains will always be found the cheapest, good, heavy. Plump oats, sound wheat, and good, taw barley forming the staple food. A pinch of illinkyro salts once a week is beneficial, and may be added to the soft mash. 1:)"glas mixture will be found the best of all tonics, and is easily made. To 111 b. of sulphate ' c)f iron and lot. of sulphuric acid add one gal- of water. This mixture should always be •-ePt in an earthenware bottle, well corked. As 'tonic, add one teaspoonful of the mixture to va4s 11 Pint of drinking water. Green bone is a •43;0"eful form of giving the birds something dlictiaukhing Nature whereby to help egg-pro-</p>
        <p>EARLY HATCHING• early r4e ru- rriMse who go in for hatching "Farm and and March, says a writer in in -trolue," will find that the young stock grow . hetter, while the pullets. if kept on, through jaYing by November Ist. and should go rig will star tt lje°Pie do not undertake to have chicks in good gh Winter. It is surprising that more time, and until this is winter a general rule s o, 'figs will be scarce. Wyandottes, on rpiagt Faverolles b , and Langshans should all e hatched not later than March to. give a good account of themselves. Non-sitting breeds do aat take as long to mature. If hatched in April, about the middle of the month, they do Well, while occasionally May chieks take some beating as winter laS-ers. Carnpines and An- eonas 'mature the soonest of any variety; the first week in May is about right for hatching. It may seem surprising to some that chickens hatched out in the coldest months grow into the largest fowls. Very seldom do April chicks gat as large as January ones, and so on. To make big profits out Of chicken raising great care is necessary. Little things can hap- Pen that will entirely upset all calculations. An attendant may turn the lamp of the foster- bother too high, or may even allow the light to go out and the chicks to get starved to death. Until the chicks are six weeks old personal auPervision is essential for success. Some breeds are hardier than others; the Itock, and .Faver- olles aro excellent varictie4 .in this 'respect, i_vhile the Orpington and.the.Dorking are not 11_1.1. behind.' In crosses the Dorking-Oame, llondan-Orpington, and tho Priverolles-prping- ton are all well worth a trial, and can be raised Illy in the bleakect district&amp;</p>
        <p>o,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>DAIRY INSTITUTE FOR CHESHIRE. The ,Cheshire Education Committee, at their ieeting at Crewe last week, decided to pur- hase lienhull Hall Farm, near •Nantwich, from Henry Tollemache, for the purpose of the moliosed farm (dairy) institute,. The farm con- 3iSts of 212 acres, and the agreed price is £65 an acre, total, £13,780, the seller indemnifying the County' Council against all claims by the tenant, who, by agreement, is to receive £1,030 for' permanent improvements. ffenhull Hall Farm is one of the most noted dairy farms in cneshirn. The Board of Agriculture have promised to pay from 71 to 75 per cent, of the capital outlay by the county upon the farm ,land and buildings), also to provide largo autintenance grants. j Possession will be abtained in 12 months' time. Staffordshire and Shropshire will not join in the scheme "for the present."</p>
        <p>MILK AND TUBERCULOSIS. Comparative Family Histories.</p>
        <p>In the course of a letter to the Editor. of " The Times on the sterilisation of milk questiod, Dr. Samuel West says : • "If a series, of cases can be shown in which infants. fed on boiled milk appear to have suffered in some way, it must be remembered that an other and much larger series can be placed :tgainsts this in which the infants, similarly fed, ffourfshed exceedingly. I am/ intimately ac- quainted with families in which unboiled milk was prohibited in the house and in which the children had been fed most scrupulously on 'ioded milk from the earliest days. They suf- fered as infants and children singularly little from digestive disturbances of any kind. Their Autritiort Was perfect. No instances of tuber- cular affection of any kind occurred, and the children grew up. into • fine healthy men and women.</p>
        <p>"On the other hand, I know of other families in which the parents steadily refused to boil the milk, though often advised to do so. They were absolutely healthy themselves and had a clean family record. The children were brought up under the most favourable conditions in town and in the country. But tpbercular affec- tions appeared—enlarged cervical glands requir- ing operation in some, enlarged mediastinal glands in one. /Disease of the spine occurred in one, of the hip in another, and one child died of tubercular meningitis. It seemed in all these cases as if the infection must have come by the milk, and as if those troubles might have been avoided if only the milk had been boiled." . Cheshire Critics of Tuberculosis Order. At the annual meeting of the Cheshire Cham- ber of Agriculture held at Crewe, Mr. A. M. R. Legh, of Adlington Hall, presided. Reference was made in the report, which was adopted, to the Tuberculosis Order, which came into force in May of last year, and had been responsible already up to September 30 for the slaughter in Cheshire of some 200 cw S. Council had been carefully watchinge operations of the Order, and had come to the conclusTiohne that the fears which they reseed had been expressed men m theemeboemr s of the association had been called . fully justified by experience, heavy sacrifices in the public interest,upo3n to make pensation being entirely ivinhadioehquate, while the Provew e r et to be bceh very awn dpe mor were n ithes large r a otoi contributors, tyexpensesrates, ,Ivto /monvrld ' il the farmers heavy indeed. The Council Iver opinion that the time had comefe,r therefore, of co eight fur- ther representations to the Board of Agriculture based based on the experien of the last and it was hoped that they would on this occa- sion be more successful.</p>
        <p>MINIMUM WAGE "IMPOSSIBLE" IN CHESHIRE. At the annual meting of the Cheshire Cham- ber of Agriculture, Mr. S. Jackson, referring to the suggested minimum wage for the agricul- tural labourer, said that to fix such a minimum would be totally impossible. The whole busi- ness of agriculture would have to be re-organ- ised from beginning to end. They could not interfere between farmers and their workmen, or between farmers and their landlords, lie would not be in favour of further legislation. They had had so much legislation that they did not know where they were. (near, hear.) It would be a grand thing, added Mr. Jackson, if the Government were to be disbanded for a few years. (Hear, hear, and laughter.)</p>
        <p>FARMING AND CLIMATE. The average winter tests the versatility of the farmer in adapting his plans to its sudden changes, says "The Times." Last week was a repetition of its predecessor, frost and snow prevailing at the beginning, and giving way to- wards the end to thaw and rain, and work has varied as prescribed by the governing condi- tions. The frost, though not prolonged, has checked the yoting wheat for the time being, and given an opportunity for the application of farmyard manure. LIVE STOCK PRIDES.</p>
        <p>Stock of all kinds are selling well. Trade and prices imply deficiency all round—in cattle, sheep and pigs, both fat and lean. The fine autumn no doubt was an important factor in making for this result, as it made it practic- able to hold the roots in reserve for the spring, but in spite of this the sufficiency of keep re- ported from most districts is difficult to recon- cile with a 'full complement of cattle and sheep. The amount of keep is estimated in re- lation to requirements, and judged by this standard, correspondents no doubt speak cor- rectly when they say that they have enough roots as well as dry fodder for normal contin- gencies. Occasionally, however, a remark is made which implies scarcity of live stock. It has taken longer than was expected to restore the breeding flocks 'and herds to the level reached before the drought of 1911, probably on selvo,scseei ailosnufonantrtsimm. oefer Thatthe v am' r i tdt efros r twu inl el s rightin s u cuciee me d there e neverr e needf slow b7e t 0 00 perceivefear, fororthio utilise an opportunitye or improving his posi- tion, and if circumstances warrant he will in- crease his breeding resources in cattle sheep or pigs, with expedition and skill</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>rn C" si</p>
