      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>, The present is a good time to plant hedges of beech, privet and hawthorn. After planting tread the soil firmly about the roots.</p>
        <p>_ Lily of the valley may be lifted and re-planted now in soil deeply dug and well-manured. Plant in a moist and slady position. Walls may be hidden by planting quick-grow- ing creepers against them. The ground must, however, be broken-up to at least two feet, and if the soil is poor incorporate some dung and fibrous loam. -</p>
        <p>The splendid condition of the soil at this time of the year will enable planting and digging to be proceeded with, and make work all the easier later on. Rose trees may be purchased and got in as opportunity permits. Cut off with a sharp knife all bruised portions of root, and soak the roots in water for a few hours before planting. Plants will probably be found to have become loosened by the wind, causing a collar of soil round the stems. The soil must be brought close to the roots and the plants made firm to prevent water lodging round the stems. Put stakes to tallish roses.</p>
        <p>LATEST SWEET PEA LIST. The National Sweet Pea Society, as the result of last year's test, recommend the following varieties of sweet peas:— Bicolor.—Arthur Unwin. Mrs. Andrew Ire- land, Afterglow—waved; Jeannie Gordon—not waved.</p>
        <p>Blue.—Flora Norton Spencer, Princess Mary, Mrs. G. Charles, Blue Jacket—waved; Lord Nel- son—not waved.</p>
        <p>Blush.—Mrs. Ilardcastle Sykes, Princess Vic- [oria—wayed. Carmine.—John Ingman—waved. Cerise.—Cherry Ripe, Decorator, Chrissie</p>
        <p>Continued in next column</p>
