      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>TIM EXAMINER, JANUARY 24, 1914. MUSICAL SOCIETY CONCERT%</head>
        <p>TIM EXAMINER, JANUARY 24, 1914. MUSICAL SOCIETY CONCERT%</p>
        <p>Notes on Some Interesting itects in Next</p>
        <p>Wednesday's Programme.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL TO "THE EXAMINER."</p>
        <p>Ely HEN</p>
        <p>It will be seen from an announcement on the front page of this issue that the Warrington Musical Society's concert in Parr Hall on Wednesday next is of a miscellaneous character, and that two of the three artistes taking prin- cipal Parts are local people. Miss Nellie_ Anderton and Miss Clarice Newall are already well and favourably known to Warrington con- i cert-frequenters in their capacities of vocalift and piano executant respectively, and the public ' of the town, or, at any rate, the musical part of it, will, we trust, show their appreciation of tltese talented townspeople in the practical way of attending next' Wednesday s concert in goodly numbers. •</p>
        <p>As the music in which both ladies will be engaged on Wednesday is, like some other of the items in the programme, of considerab7.) intrinsic interest, it is thought that the fol:cw- ing notes, in regard to these outstanding num- bers and their composers, may be found usefin in preparing the minds of those who may be privileged to listen to the music and of assisting them in intelligently following its performance.</p>
        <p>TSCHAIKOWSKY. Valse from " Eugen Oniegin." . Peter Ilich Tschaikowsky was born at gamsko:Votinsk, in Viatka, in May, 1840, and died in 1893, and it is safe to say that few com- posers of our time have captured the public ear so completely as has this prolific and versatile musician during the 20 years that have elapsed since his death. The operatic valse which opens the concert on Wednesday next is a character- istic example of Tschaikowsky's style in works of this kind. A somewhat extended introduc- tion or prelude rising through a crescendo to a vehement and curiously syncopated climax, leads to a waltz theme of great melodic beauty. Its predominant quality—and the remark ap- plies equally to most of the other melodies Occurring in course of the movement—is extreme vivaciousness as distinguished from the dreamy iangourousness which characterises many highly esteemed waltzes of the modern school. The opera "Eugen Oniegin," from which the valse is taken, is one of two by Tiichaikowsky which have so far achieved success. Written shortly before the year 1880, when the composer may be said to have been in the Prime of life, "Eugen Oniegin" was first performed in that year at Moscow by some students of the Conservatoire in that city. That the opera met with only a cool reception is ascribed to the fact that only a poor interpretation of it was given. Happily, however, the circumstance of its comparative failure on this occasion does not appear to have discouraged Tschaikowsky, who lived to see it grow in favour until it became one of the most popular of his work-s. COLERIDGE-TAYLOR. 48 Kiibla Khan," a Rhapsody for Contralto, . :Chorus and Orchestra.</p>
        <p>It is a little confusing to have to speak, in the same connection, of Samuel Taylor-Coleridge, poet and philosopher, and of Samuel Coleridge- Taylor, musician and composer. Naturally it will occur to many that there is some closer connection between these two names than the accident of the bearers of them being eon„rood in this cantata. So far as can be ascertained,' such, however, is not the, case, and the coinci- dence of the later man's name being a trans- position of the earlier one's, does not appear to stand for more than a record of some whim onl alas! become addicted to the part of Coleridge-Taylor's parents. Begin- ning, then, with the author of " Kubla Khan," life, when a‘y_hreenmhined hinatde,rested readers that Coleridge for the first time to dis- cern 1772, died 1834) wrote this poem in middle' opium. It will therefore surprise no one who reads " Kubla Khan" the most fervid imag- ination. , therein poetic licence carried to extreme' lengths. Here flights of fancy show themselves , in luxurious oriental imagery so superb tit 1 magnificence as to satisfY composed from two to three That ~Kutia Khan " is rightly called the fragment now exist- ing dream poem is atteeted by the circumstances upon awaking. lie was interrupted attending its creation. Coleridge himself tells us that he conll3o lor, the composer of hundred lines of it during a sleep of three hours, and that he wrote down in August, 1875, and that binyga(sv4isliitneers)anud the remainder of the poem van- ished from his mind! accounts for his negro-like Of Samuel Coleridge*TaY and his mother was an 1 the music of " Kubla Shan,'" we may recall that hilee dwasiedbsoorni in recentlyL ondoans n wFricnii, he attained some profi- ciency, father was a Doctor. of Medicine, and a native of Sierra 1 Leone—nala afapcpteawrhanicche_ Ib9:2in. g at first a student Stanford (now Sir Charles), and conductor of the Handel Society. composition, passed to the study of , ueoE ininf Englishwoman. dnlgt 09hle :oyes I violin,:w. he oe mbecame Villiersa n e' which he is credited with 'laving brought to a state of greatly enhanced fg i e—e-eneY. It was as a Handel commission from the Society that " Kla Kabsa, n in' was composed, and by that society first Produced, under the composer's direction.., A conspicuous feature of Coleridge-I'4Yl ub it w may, 1906, or s art has always been the free use of orchestral colouring, and it is not surprising, therefore, that, cast in a rhapsodicalhan " "Kubla K a very iin a matter of fact, the orchestra plays Portant part throughout the comiet i cmould,hrichly oe coed A position, a long instrumental introduction. built upon the chief themes, skil- fully handled and laid out amongst the various sectionsttentn of as engaging the io s his setting of The chorus has some graceful passages to sing, and many oppor- tunities for really effective ensemble singing present themselves. It will be noticed, indeed, athteheorwch work opens. ean t s. once that everything depends at first on the chorus carrying on, musically, the fascinating narrative of the opening ten lines of the Poem, commenc- ing: "In Xanadu did Kubla Khan . A stately pleasure-dome decree; Where Alph, the sacred river, ran Through caverns measureless to man, Down to a sunless sea."</p>
        <p>As the contralto soloist enters at the line: " But ch.! that deep romantic chasm," the choral voices become more or less subjugated to it, and the music itself gradually takes on a deeper emotional quality, while to the lot of the solo voice, after the "damsel with a dulcimer" comes upon the scene, falls some onerous and telling passages, capable of quite dramatic expression.</p>
        <p>"Kubla Khan," the poem, is at once delight- glY climactic. Upon the atmosphere of its adequately realised, and (f theiriel layin questionse- ltileerevaalg uamen, nhdee stsshte.ir sro n tlio full possibilities of its climax are attained, there is by no meaps unanimity amongst the quid nuncs. Let each and all therefore judge for themselves by taking the opportunity which presents itself, on Wednesday next, of listening to this remarkable work.</p>
        <p>SIBELIUS. Symphonic Tone Poem, " Finlandia." Jean Sibelius, the Finnish composer, has attained to a position of high esteem in this country during the past decade, and it can hardly have escaped notice that items of hie music sre now constantly in the programmes of all important concerts. Nor can it be said that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>RY BOND.</head>
        <p>RY BOND.</p>
        <p>Sibelius has yet attained Ile. pian3cle of his popularity here, for a deeper alcquaintance es thethinFii Icahis musical appetitel. sneudtresi dsei lfoianoonf 01 should thel:di n6el the case follow tcorj ia) eutbl s. obeewwil musiconaicfgsßt hmwaos ahretai Born at wasest attvhnoeniCnronTs trtp peculiar enaietarsdllros lemltyl di:, z!suaaei stt ,cett iihhoitonhani roes: discov- ered, he received his early training in music under Wegelius at Helsingfors, and, later, under Becker at Berlin, and Goldmark at Vienna. Sibelius has almost continuously been under the strohg influence of the Finnish mythology, and his compositions, while often therefore saturated with legend, are also more or less permeated with the folk-music of his country. The beingrk outcomes h mus music. Finlandia" tone-poem, which will bei heard next week; is quite a typical example of these a dire struggle against an unfavourable environ- Iflenant sib• kceu tl ihaer environ- ment, the proper understanding of this fight, and esc ho fa icst e;,o of the triumph of life in such a life and death conflict, is necessary to make. Sibelius's music intelligible. In his music, Sibelius, therefore, stands almost entirely alone, for so strong are the racial characteristics which distinguish his compositions, as they do all Finnish music, that it is impossible to find anything allied with it in that of adjacent nationalities. In the Finnish folk-song the 5-4 rhythm largely prevails, while another marked feature is the rapid repetition of some one note or notes many times. This latter peculiarity is , strongly in evidence in " Finlandia." The work is, moreover, unques- tionably founded upon the folk-music of the country of its origin. Great masses of sonorous harmony are here flung upon the listener, and mingled with it is the 'wild chanting of racial tunes, numerous flights of notes in the strings being answered by that continued reiteration of groups of other notes in the wind instruthents, to which allusion has been made. That Sibelius's nature is curiously varied shows up in his music in alternations of languor bordering on mystic- ism, with moments of great vigour and extreme self-assertiveness. As an instance let readers recall the ethereal qualities of the Valse Triste, heard at the last Musical Society's concert, and compare it with the work about which we are now writing.</p>
        <p>SCHUMANN. The A Minor Pianoforte Concerto.</p>
        <p>Robert Alexander Schumann, bOrn at Zwickau, in Saxony, in 1810, the youngest son of a book- seller and litterateur, had in him almost as much of the poet as the musician. Indeed, the poetic faculty would seem to have been the first to assert itself in Schumann's character, and, being from early years largely under the thrall of Jean Paul, it is scarcely surprising that he not only showed the possession of considerable poetic talent, but his life was to a great extent spent in alternations of literary labour and work as a piano-executant and composer. For quite a 'long period his attention was distracted be- tween contributing and subsequently editing a musical periodical, and the study of music itself. A more or less rigorously kept resolution by Schumann when at the age of 20 to devote him- self to music, marks the beginning of his inti- mate connection with his pianoforte master, Friedrich Wieck, of Liepsic, whose daughter, Clara Josephine, he eventually married. She is, of course, known even to many Of the present generation as the gifted and renowned Clara Schumann, who achieved numerous musical triumphs in the later years of her widowed life. The works of Bach were a considerable factor in the shaping of Schumann's faculty for composi- tion, and for a long period he wrote scareely anything but pianoforte music. A marriage, marked by the deepest devotion to each ether of husband and wife, signalises that part of Schumann's life from which his larger and more important works date. The pianoforte concerto, the first movement of which is to be performed on Wednesday, is one of the first fruits of this more luminous period. The first movement, viritten in 1811, was intended to form an inde- pendent piece, with the title of " Fantasie," and only after a four years' interval did Schumann write the two further movements which 'com- plete the concerto. This is how it happens that the excerpt under consideration is a work so self- contained and complete that only seldom is the whole concerto performed. In its entirety it is one of Schumann's most beautiful and mature works, one, indeed, in which he has left for all time a record of his genius. Glowing with the fire of his personality it embodies the qualities of brilliancy allied with striking and broadly moulded subject-matter. The general plan of the first movemtnt may be roughly outlined thus:</p>
        <p>After a few animated bars by way of prelude, and serving the purpose of asserting the key of A minor, the broad Principal subject is announced by the wood-wind and brought to a half close. This subject, cast, of course, in the key of A minor, and bearing well-marked features, is then taken up by the solo instrument, in masses of combined chords and Melody engaging both hands in a way peculiarly Sc.huniannesque, and by it brought to a full close. Incidentally ample material is provided for the development section that follows—a section which is built principally upon the opening phrase of this lead- ing theme. As it proceeds there will be heard further and subordinated subjects which though contrasted in character with the main theme are yet scarcely less engaging. These and some episodic material of a highly melodic kind are utilised in leading np to a further presentment of the principal subject by the soloist. This time it is, however, recast in the key of the relative major, the new conditions imparting to it a guise of quite transcendent beauty. The middle section is imperceptibly led into by treatment which at first promised to culminate in a vehement close in the key just mentioned. Instead, it quietly merges into a 6-8 slow rhythm in the new key of A flat, which now affords welcome and grateful relief from the briskness of all that precedes it, without entirely breaking continuity, inasmuch as its subject. matter consists in new and modified forms of ideas already familiar. The intervention of an animated and agitated phrase reiterated in the orchestra and generating a feeling of high expec- tancy, leads in a striking manner to a resump- tion of a more animated section in the open key,* and, later on, to the re-entry of the principal subject under exactly the same conditions as occur at its first hearing. Similar developments follow, and again this leading theme is presented by the pianoforte in its trans- figured and most beautiful aspect in the major key, but this time that of the tonic major, in which key the movement continues from this point. Becoming ever more animated, it cul- minates in an extended and brilliant cadenza, which leads into a lively coda in the original key of A minor, bringing the work to a dashing and finely climactic conclusion.</p>
        <p>01 "Bits."</p>
        <p>"How jaded your horse looks, cabman," re- marked an, old lady fare. "Is not the hit uncomfortably large for his mouth?" "It ain't the big bit in his mouth, mum, it's the small bit in his stomach that's troubling him, and "-- looking at the tendered. shilling—"it doesn't seem as if it'll get much larger."</p>
