      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>IN CHARM'S NAME.</head>
        <p>IN CHARM'S NAME.</p>
        <p>A Brilliant Company Dance for 'Funds for the Infirmary.</p>
        <p>A PRETTY SPECTACLE.</p>
        <p>It was a fashionable party that gathered in the Parr Hall on Monday evening to partici- pate in the annual ball, organised to augment the income of the Warrington Infirmary and Dispensary. There were about 150 guests pre- ent, and the scene in the hall during the danc- ing was very pleasing. The ladies were ar- rayed in the latest ballroom fashions, and as they pursued their way across the floor one was reminded of a peep into a fashion book—so neat were the dresses.</p>
        <p>The building had been in the hands of skil- fu) decorators, and showed up exceedingly well under their treatment. The body of the ball had been treated in A simple yet most effective manner. At the far end of the large room a Inage mirror had been fixed and on either side was a decorative scheme cpmposed of white and green. muslin' arranged in' perpendicular stripes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>NOOKS AND ALCOVES.</head>
        <p>NOOKS AND ALCOVES.</p>
        <p>The lialcony was hid from view by Oriental tapestry, to which „a frill was provided by fes- toous 'of 'greenery. Tile scheme was finished oj- the introduction of hanging flower baskets, so. arranged as to fill in the void between the curves of the festoons. The gallery was trans- formed into a number of nooks and alcoves, all aighly draped, but commanding an excellent view of the dancing on the glass-like parquetry floor.</p>
        <p>The programme was composed of twenty dances, including valses, one-steps and tango's, executed to the tunes of Mr. W. P. Bayley's band.</p>
        <p>One of the crush rooms had been converted into the supper room, and here again a very effective scheme had been carried out, in which white and green muslin was to the fore.</p>
        <p>THE LIST OF GUESTS.</p>
        <p>Those present included:—</p>
        <p>Miss Adams.</p>
