      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Was It Based Upon a Corrupt Form of Christianity?</head>
        <p>Was It Based Upon a Corrupt Form of Christianity?</p>
        <p>PROF. CURRIE MARTIN'S VIEWS.</p>
        <p>The third lecture of the series on the great religions of the world was given in the Stock. too heath Adult School on ..uonday evening by Professor George Currie-Martin, his subject being "The Religion of Islam." The lecturer mentioned by way of introduc- tion that Islam or Mohammedanism was con- , tinually before the British public in the con- stantly recurring problems of the Near East, Incidentally, the speaker said that King George was ruler over more Mohammedans than any other monarch. Another interesting thing about Islam is the fact that it is the only great historic religion that has arisen in the world since Christianity. The Islam literally means "subject to the Will' of God, or slaves of [leaven." That was the great dominant note in all the Mohammedan's life and affairs. Mahomet himself was born in 571 A.D. and died in 632 A.D. But it was only in his later years, beginning in 622 A.D., that the religion 'he founded began to assume any influence. It was In the great lone deserts that his inspirations and revelations came to him. The desert had in the past had a great influence upon other re- ligions. Moses went through his great spiritual experiences and received his great revelation in the desert. Paul, after his conversion and the great upheaval in his religions experience, went into the desert to think things over and find his spiritual bearings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>Corrupt Form of Christianity.</head>
        <p>Corrupt Form of Christianity.</p>
        <p>Mohammedanism was derived from three sources: (1) The old Arabic Animism; (2) Old Testament stories and traditions, and (3) a cor- rupt form of Christianity. The Koran—the sacred book of Islam—was full of Old Testa- ment history and incidents, but mixed up with the events as recorded in the Old Testament were all sorts of legends and traditions. All the great patriarchs and prophets of Israel were mentioned in the Koran, and even Jesus was spoken of in very high terms as a very great— perhaps the greatest—prophet. The Virgin lfary was defended valiantly against the many slanders against her person which were current in the East. Mahomet himself unfortunately never came into contact with a high type of Christianity. The Christianity ,which he saw was of a corrupt and debased type, and Mehemet was perhaps justified in coming to the conclusion that it did not meet his needs. Had he experienced the purer and nobler forms of Christianity it might have made a revolution- ary difference in the religion which he founded.</p>
        <p>God as a Tyrant. The Islam idea of God could not be said to be a very high one. It was "God merciful," not the "God loving" of Christianity. Rather the idea of God as a tyrant, who must be appeased, than a God who dearly loves all His creatures and showers blessings on their heads. The four great rituals of Islam were all in keeping with this idea. Prayer, fasting, pilgrimages and alms. The Mohammedan was a very devout and reverent person indeed, and performs his devo- tions anywhere and at all times, as his spirit moved him to do so. Modern Islam was divided into three sects. (1) Surma; (2) Shia, and (3) Bab. The Sunna consist of the literalists, who were the unbend- ing, uncompromising, fanatical Mohammedans, found in North and West Africa chiefly. The Shia, who were found in Persia, had elements of a much softer, more sympathetic, and ten- derer quality in their creed and outlook. They were the mystics of Islam. The followers of Bab. a modern Mohammedan, approached in some respects very near to a high type of Chris- tianity. It was possible that this latter sect ' might yet be the means of leavening the fiercer and more cruel elements of fanaticism out of Islam altogether, and when that was accom- plished Christianity and Islam would not be very widely separated.</p>
        <p>"THE LAND" BOOK.</p>
        <p>Reproduction of the cover of the book, which is• being eagerly read and discussed in every part of the kingdom. Mr. Lloyd George speaks thus of "The Land," the book published locally at "The Examiner" Office, in which appears the ' evidence of the committee that will be the foundation of the land campaign upon which he has embarked:— _ _ _</p>
        <p>"It is the biggest piece of work. I think, of the kind done in my day in Great Britain. Look out for the book. It is worth stud3'ing, but it swill take some time." "The Land," price 1/..</p>
