      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>COMPLETE EXAMINER STORY'</head>
        <p>COMPLETE EXAMINER STORY'</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS Itinzaireo.,</p>
        <p>THE SOUL OF A SHIP:</p>
        <p>BEATRICE HERON-MAXWELL (Author of "A Wornan's Love," "The Queen Regent," etc.).</p>
        <p>CHAPTER I. THE Two CHRISTAN.</p>
        <p>The photograph of the "Christine," in a carved wood frame made from a fragment of the fore-mast, hangs IKfore me as I write, and shews a brig of 750 tons, trim and clean from stem to stern, lying in the harbour of Mobile, ready to make her maiden voyage with the break of day. Below is another picture—the portrait of a girl, whose beauty lies, not so much in fea- tures and colouring, though both are full of charm, as in a certain spirituality of expres- sion that characterises it; with the raised letters of her name, Christine, on a gilt oval mount. The wonderful eyes, gdlden brown, with velvet depths in them, lik3 pansies -glowing in the sun, look into space as though they saw, and, seeing, took command of, a shad- owy world of infinite ,possibilities. Billows of hnir, with ruddy gleams in their warm brownness, wave over a broad fore- head; and eyebrows, fine and -dark, arch up- wards like delicate wings, losing themselves in the s'n-idow of soft love-locks on either. side. Ther\ is a hint of sadness in the wistful droop of her mouth, but a little cleft in the rounded chin betrays two capacities—those tof mirth and passion, and one can fancy gaiety and tenderness and the tumult of love, all, in turn, quickening into vivacity the close-folded sweetness of those lips. But at the moment of the portrait an absorbed se- renity is the dominating note of face and attitude. And the story of the two Christines, twin namesalses and sisters as they were, both be- ing the offspring. of Christopher Hansen, Canadian merchant of Mobile, is told in the human documents enclosed in a locked case beneath them.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER, 11. PAGES FROM CHRISTINE'S DIARY.</p>
        <p>To-day has been the strangest and the I happiest and the saddest of my life! For I have gained and lost what I have never had before—a- companion, a sister, a being whose life belongs to me, who shares with me my father's love, his hopes, his ambitions, his very heart-strings, and whom I am to lose with the dawn of to-morrow, the second day ei her independent separate existence! Ever since I was quite a little child my father has always said: "When the Chris- tine is built," just as other people say: "When my ship comes home:" and when he asked me what should be my present f-om him on my twenty-first birthday I said quickly: "Give me the Christine—build her for me." Father smiled and said: "It is less than five months to your birthday, and we have the winter to face, which is not good for such work; but a woman-child only wants the possible when it is impossible, and if the Christine could but spring into being at this moment you would no longer desire her." I clasped my arms round his neck and I said: "Try me." But he only smoothed my cheek and put me gently by, and presently went away to his office and his work. , - Yet from that day I felt that something was coming to me from the Unknown, some gift of Fate or Fortune, some heritage of Chance or Destiny, that drew nearer with each passing hour, and must inevitably ar- rive. This morning when I awoke and realised that it was my birthday. I felt that the pre- lude was over, and that what I waited for was close at hand.</p>
        <p>• There were two voices in the morning-room when I came 'down to breakfast, my father's and another, which I recognised as that of Captain Brunswick of the Merchant servioe. We have known him for some time. They were talking earnestly, but they stopped . when I came in, and, smilingly, drew asun- der; an/ then I saw what they were looking at, and forgot everything in the sudden glad- ness of the sight. For on the tab:e was &amp; perfect model of a ship with my name painted round her bows, and by its side a card inscribed: "To be christened and launched to-day at one o'clock by the owner, Christine Hansen." I took it in my hands and pressekmy lips to it, and then I turned to father and said: The impossible made possible! Father you have given me my heart's desire." I knew that the tears were in my eyes, and wishesi Captssin Brunswick had not been there to gee them; but father, patting my ahoulder, made me look , at him, for he said: " The Captain has consented to take your ship to Queenstown, Christine, with a load of pitcn pine, and, if all goes well, they start to-morrow. had to :shake hands with the Captain, and listen to his congratulations and compli- ments, impatient as I was, and always have been, of them. And then he went away, and father to7d me what they had been talking of before I came. It seems that Captain Brunswick wants to marry me, and that father has set his heart on my doing so. I am to wait to give my an- avver, ifsl wish, until he returns from Queens- town. The thought would have distressed me— since I do not love Captain Brunswick nor wish to marry anyone—but that there was no room in mg mind for anything but my ship, the realisation of say Dreams. 'When I had put on my white cloth dress and white furs, we drove, father and I, to the dock; and there in her cradle Was nay Dew-born siater, my Christine. How beautiful she wag! how pure, and spotless, and delicate and strong! like a sea- bird whose wings could spread and bear her swiftly against wind or water to the haven where she would be. As the time drew near for the christening, father led me to the litt:e platform with its crimson rail that hung over the edge of the dock, and shewed ins the lever that would draw the cork of the champagne to baptise her, and the handle I should turn to release her from her bonds, and give her the power to live and more and have her being. Father himself stood on any right, and on my left was Captain Brunswick, and beyond again, the crew of the Christine, and then a heat of people—many friends and many strangers. But one face only stood out from the throng; a dark, strong face, grave and keen, with eyes that seemed to leap to mine and hold them captive. We stood, gazing at one another, this dark tall young stranger and I, until my father, railowine the direction of my eyes with his</p>
