      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>CHAPTER M.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER M.</p>
        <p>EXTRACTS PROM ROGER_ IsELIN'S JOURNAL. I begin to distrust Brunswick, and for a reason that I cannot define. He is scrupulously polite, but he watches' me, and cnce when he came suddenly into my cabin and surprised me looking at Christine's photograph I saw his hand clench itsea quickly, and he said something •under his breath that sounded queer. let he regained his self-control so quicily that I almost per- suaded myself it was a trick' of my imagina- tion.</p>
        <p>A. fortnight to-day since we left Mobile. There are signs of bad weather, and I think we are in for a gale. The skipper seems bent on taking us into the teeth of it—unneces- sarily, since our more direct course would keep us well to the windward. I ventured a suggestion, but he cut me short and gave me an order that took me below. I do not under- stand him.</p>
        <p>Some of the men have spoken to me about Brunswick. We are having nasty weather, and we are out of our course; they are very discontented, and want me to remonstrate with him. It is as much as my billet is worth in the mood he is in now. I think Brunswick is a rittle wrong in the head. He goes about 'silent and moody, or else has sudden paroxysms of anger easily roused. The look out is as bad as it can be; we are in for a eyclone, and God knows if we shall weather it. The worst has happened. The men mutinied co-day without the slightest warning. I heard a sudden tumult and ran on deck. They had taken Brunswick unawares, and I found them in a ring with him helpless and lashed to the mast in their midst. "This is your doing," he said. " You have set them on to this." For answer I cut the rope round his arms and took my stand beside him. The men were like devils let loose. " He's sailing us to our death," they shout- ed. "We'll down you both and take the brig Queenstown ourselves." We had a sharp set-to. They wanted me to take their part, put the Captain in irons, and step into his place. He fought more like a savage animal than a man, and I am afraid he is more than half a madman.</p>
        <p>We cowed the men at last, and drove them back to their work. Then I went down to Brunswick and spoke to him, as carefully as possible. I to:d him we were taking unnQces- Glary risks, and that if he wanted to keep the men obedient he had better take the safe course. He flared up at once, called me an insubordinate dog, and repeated that I had started the mutiny. Then he suddenly flung out of the cabin, and bolted me in. Here I am, shut up, a useless prisoner in his cabin, and the hurricane has burst fight over • our heads. _ .</p>
        <p>The noise is tremendous, and I shall be surprised if the Christine lives through such a sea. It is horrible—maddening—to be here] alone, powerless, when I am longing to be at my work. I would sooner throw myself into the sea and light for my life than stay like this with idle hands; a rat in a trap, cut off from everyone, with the knowledge that the Christine is being hurried to her doom. This was .13runswick's meaning; I see it now clearly. He deliberately vane mistakes and worked the men up to rebellion; he knew they would turn to me because I am popular with them, and he is not. He calculated on my doing as I have done, and so putting it in his power to pen me up down here; and ,now—God only knows what he means to do with the ship and me. I have been looking at Christine's picture; drinking in—for the last time I expect—the perfection of her face. co woman has ever been anything to me before in my life. Christine is my. friend, my sweetheart, my all. Somehow her eyes seem to command hope and courage; her lips seem to say: "My heart and soul are with you, and will bring you safely to me once more., God! what is that ? Are we struck What is happening ? They are letting down the boat—they are going to desert the ship. The cowards! I never thought it would jnd like this. I would like to have fought with the winds and wavco for the Christine, and if I couldn't have saved her, to have gone down with her in the open—not shut in here. Good-bye, Christine—my only love—good- bye. You will never read this, but it calms me to write it down—it keeps me sane. I don't want to dio mad—l want to keep my mind clear, and go down in your ship with Christine. your "me on my lips.' Christine ray</p>
        <p>CHAPTER IV. CHRISTINE'S Duna CownNerly.</p>
        <p>Three whole mouths to-day since my birth- day. have Sounded the depths of hell on earth and learnt what it can be sine., then. We said to each other: " They are ail lost to- getiier, the Christine add her crew."' Ar,it e•° myself I said: " I have lost my sister and my lover—and for me life holds nothing fur. ther."</p>
        <p>There was a night when I dreamed that Roger was in some deadly peril from which I could not gave him. There was a waking moment of torture so greaf that it nearly unhinged my brain, while the dream stayed with mend I believed it true.</p>
        <p>But after that came a -merciful numbness in which my suffering seemed deadened into apathy.</p>
        <p>say that joy never kills. That may be, but it goes very near it sometimes. 'When father brought the cable, and I knew that Roger was safe. I seemed to go over the bor- derland, and for many days they thought that I would die. Then came Roger 'himself, and his voice and hand seimed to draw zzie</p>
