      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>IR, SATURDAY, JANUARY 24, 1914.</head>
        <p>IR, SATURDAY, JANUARY 24, 1914.</p>
        <p>nick again to consciousness and full under- standing, and at last to that blest hour when he told me. all. _ .</p>
        <p>Holding me in his arms with my face buried in his shoulder, he whispered to me of that awful time when he was shut in, as helpless as the Christine was herself in the grip of the storm. It was at the last moment that the cabin door was unbo!ted and flung open, and Cap- tain Brunswick staggered in. He seized Ro- ger's 'arm, and pushed him out of the cabin. "Go up," he shputed "get to the boat. The ship can't live in this—we must leave her." . . _</p>
        <p>"But you ar coming too, sir," Roger callus pack, for he was lingering. "I won't leave you here and go by myself.' He was holding my photograph still in his hand; the Captain c.utched it from him. "She saved you," he cried. "She wouldn't let me do as 1 meant to do—send you down with the ship. Go on up—l'll follow—there's tr.) time to lose." Roger ran up, and could just distinguish Use boat and hear the men shouting ior him to come. , He looked round for Captain Brunswick— there was no sign of him. Thep as he dashed back, they met face to face. , "Come on, sir," Roger cried; and seized the Captain in his arms, trying to drag him towards the boat. They wrestled together, and the Captain Brunswick flung him off. " Obey my orders," he called out, "or I'll shoot you like a'dog." IA pulled out his revolver and fired at, Roger, who .fell wounded on the deck. One of the sailors gripped Roger and pull„ed him into the boat, and the men let her go, and in a moment she was carried away on the waves.</p>
        <p>Roger was insensible for many hours, and knew nothing more until after they had been picked up and rescued by the "Titania," the following morning. He was badly wounded and was ill for many days; but recovered while the Titania was Competing her voyage</p>
        <p>to Australia. . Then the Titania took him back to Eng- land, and he and all the crew of the Chris- tine were landed at Liverpool, and made . .. ._ , .</p>
        <p>their way here by tho next- bait. So that Captain Brunswick must have re- manic(' on tne ilcisilue atm tacro wait ca tor his death.</p>
        <p>**</p>
        <p>The last chapter of the Christine'd life is written, and 1 am going to closo and put away for ever that part of my lie. • NN, o have had news from Queenstown that the .wreck of my ship has drilled into har- bour there—a hundred and sixteen days al:-</p>
        <p>ter she left Mobile !, Little was standing beside the stump of the foremast, but when they searched below, they found what had once been Captain Brunswick, shut into his cabin. They have put up a eross, made from the ship, to his memory at Queenstown, and they speak of him there as the gal:ant cap tam n who stuck to his ship until .the end. Poor fellow. Poor, poor fellow I May he rest in peace. But though we take our happiness--Roger and I—with reverent trembling hands, since it has come to us' through such a sea of sor- row and despair, we take it fearlessly, know- ing that strength of love and singleness of heart and will, can weather any storm that life may' bring. It is our marriage day, and my father's present to us is a new Christine in which we are to go for our wedding journey. We are going to Roger's old home in England, and he has promised me 'to yield to his mother's last wish and take up the old title that was his father's and his fore- father's before him. Once More there will he a Sir Roger Iselin at Lin Castle where all hie childhood was spent. So, while I say farewell to the old Chris- tine, I stretch out, my hands in welcome to a new one and feel how near Life is to Death and Death to Life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>FUNERAL OF LORD CROSS.</head>
        <p>FUNERAL OF LORD CROSS.</p>
        <p>Tho following account of the funeral of Lord Cross was, unfortunatelY, crowded out of last week's "Examincr,,, along with several other</p>
        <p>The funeral of Viscount Cross took place at the parish church at Broughton-in-Furness on Saturday, the Bishop of Carlisle, Canon Mon- nington, and many other clergy of the diocese being present. The chief mourners were Mr. Richard Asslieton Cross (the heir), the Hon. Mary Cross (daughter), the Hon. J. E. and liars: Cross (son and daughter- in-law). the Rev. the Hon, C. F. Cross (son), Mr. and the Hon. Mrs. Edward Bowyer and Major and the Hon. Mrs. Henry Dawson (daughters and sons-in-law). Miss Dorothy Cross, miss Marjorie Cross, and Miss Ellinor Cross, of Ash House; Mr. Richard W. Bowyer, Miss Bowyer, Miss Madge Cross. and Miss Elsie Cross (grandchildren), Mr. Wil- liam Cross, of Read Soar, and Vice-Admiral C. W. Cross (nephews). Others present included Mr. J. A. Grant, M.P., , Mr. A. Aslett (general manager of the Furness Railway), Mr. 0. S. Holt (secretary of the Great Central Railway). and Mr. E. A. Beasley (director). Preaching at the Temple Church on Sunday. the Master said that a good many of them had had in their thoughts the close of an excep- tionally long mici heneured life, which, if not of Commanding eminence, had accomplished a great deal of solid, useful and unobtrusive work. Viscount Cross, for many year's Master of the Bench of the Honourable Society of the Inner Temple, was not perhaps a great statesman, but a highly successful Min- ister. He would probably be best remembered 1 for his work at the Home Office, to which he brought the indispensable qualifications of sound law and hard common sense. The legislation which he initiated there had stood the test of time, and subsequent reforms had followed on the lines he had laid down. The sterling in- tegrity of his character had been widely recog- nised, and had made him a trusted adviser. The last occasion on which he had been a wor- shipper at that church was not so very long ago, and, notwithstanding his great age, he had been able to stay, for the second service. Dying in his 91st year, he had outlived most, though not all, of his contemporaries, but those who knew him in the later years of his active life would think of him with much esteem and friendliness.</p>
        <p>"Only the actions of the just Smell sweet and blossom in their dust."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>"LITTLE JACK HORNER."</head>
        <p>"LITTLE JACK HORNER."</p>
        <p>"Little Jack Horner," the familiar nursery rhyme, had its origin in a real tragedy. During the Reformation, the head of Glastonbury Abbey, in the West of England, resolved to mak4 his peace with Henry VIII., and, in token thereof, sent certain title-deeds of abbey pro- perty lo the King at Whitehall. For security's sake, the, abbot placed the documents in a pie dish, and covered thorn with a crust. The dish he gave to a rustic lout; named Jack Horner, and he told him to carry it by the high road to the King in London. On the road, Jack Horner became hungry, and camo to tho conclusion that it would be foolish to starve while he had a pie in his hands. so he broke the crust and put in his thumb and pulled out a roll of parchment. The disgusted and disappointed fellow threw both pie and parchment into a near-by brook. When the deeds did not appear, the King charged the abbot with contumacy, and commanded that the unfortunate cleric should be hanged.</p>
        <p>Spongy Brains., , There are people—and they pass as intellec- tuals 'often ,enough—whose skulls contain sponges instead of brains, and they drip quota- tions, the soluble ideas of others which they have sucked up as a sponge will drip the water it has absorbed.—" The Tatter."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>WOMEN'S QUESTIONS.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>Food More ; Dress Less ; Cost up in</p>
        <p>Each Case.</p>
        <p>THE COMING SEASON.</p>
        <p>Should nothing happen of an untoward nature the London season this year is expected to begin early, and to be very brilliant. The first Court will be held three days after the State oPening of Parliament, on February 10th, and there will be two State balls at Buckingham Palace. Princess Arthur of Connaught will take her place in the Royal circle as one of the brides of the season. and many interesting debutantes will be seen in society this spring. It will be a dancing year, and one of the most brilliant balls will be given by the Duke and Duchess, of Devonshire at their town house, in honour' of their debutante daughter, Lady Maud Caven- dish. The latter is the god-daughter of the Queen of Norway, and of the Princess Royal. Another debutante of distinction is Lady Maud Hamilton, who is to make her formal and first appearance in. society at a big dance given especially for her. • Rifle Shooting for Women.</p>
        <p>Rifle shooting as a sport for women is very popular throughout the United Kingdom. The ancient city of Salisbury has a very strong ladies' club, and in the recent' competitions two members tied with a possible hundred each. Some men's clubs set aside certain afternoons for ladies' practice. Beverly, also an ancient city, gives three afternoons a ifeek, and the ladies, on these occasions, arrange inter-club, competitions. Earl Roberts has expressed his great admiration for the example women are setting their menfolk in this connection. He thinks it quite possible that in our next great war, men who volunteer, but who cannot use the rifle, will be rejected and sent back from the fighting line, to be instructed by their female relatives in this, the first duty of a citizen. Why Food is Dearer.</p>
        <p>We know now what has done it! A professor of chemistry at one of the leading universities in America has discovered that, as a result of the mode for scanty attire, women eat much more than they used to. They must keep warmth in themselves somehow, and wearing fewer and thinner clothes,, they have to eat more of the heat-giving foods to keep up a normal temperature or the body. Thus there is a greater consumption of food, which the pro- fessor says, has Caused an increase of ten per cent. in the cost of living. This, however, is only a one-sided explanation of the rise in prices. No doubt women do eat more than they used to. The more active life they lead in this twentieth century would account for that, but whilst provisions have gone up, one does not notice that the less demand for clothing has reduced its prices. For some time past the woollen and textile trades have been complain- ing of loss of business, owing to.. the vogue for slimness. .What has been poison for them has been meat for the provision dealers, but the result to the public seemS to have been much the same in both cases. The American profes- sor's theory may appear to him to be a plausible one, but it will not acconnt for the increased cost of clothirig as well as of food.</p>
        <p>The Winter Bargain Carnivai. The winter sales, that have come to he re- garded as one of the most important events of the New Year, are at their busiest during the third week in January. Then thousands of country cousins pour into the Metropolis from the four points of the compass. They Pain the London main body of shoppers, forming an army that fills the West End shopping centres to overflowing, and crowd the pavements to such an extent that walking becomes a slow and 'difficult process. The greatest bargains this year are to be found in the fur departments of the various stores and shops, and as the most fragile fabrics will be trimmed with narrow bands of peltry till late in the coming spring, if not during the early summer, any of these will pay to buf at the prices marked. ' Fashionable Scarves and Sets. Material sets, consisting of scarves, or stoles, and mtffs, trimmed with bands of fur, will be even more favoured in the spring than they have been this winter. Those made for wearing</p>
      </div>
