      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>MILES'</head>
        <p>MILES'</p>
        <p>on the Riviera are most charming confections of brocade, chiffon, ninon, mousseline, de soie, or the many other filmy fabrics that hive taken the popular fancy this season, and bands of fur. The woman, or girl, with a taste for the mak- ing of the various accessories of modern attire, could easily provide herself with several of these dainty and delightful sets, and at very little expense. A length of silk, or other suit- able material may be bought now for the pro- verbial song, and the fur would be a trifling matter also; nor does ..the making present any difficulties, whilst these sets add much, variety to a small wardrobe.</p>
        <p>34,36,38,40,42.44;46;4i§. LONDON RP. /2/41.ROYAI ‘QPOOL 17./Rh'/51///f6,1/Yfiff</p>
        <p>NATURE IS AN ALCOHOL FIEND.</p>
        <p>Nature apparently seems anxious to produce alcohol on the slightest provocation. With jam, as every housewife knows, if the least bit of air enters a fermentation sets up and alcohol is formed, Then there is the harmless glass of grape juice, which, if left for a little while, will turn into wine. Even a dish of sugared fruit will greet one in a short time with a tingling flavour of alcohol, and a s'imple bottle of milk, if given a touch of yeast, will turn into a purely alcoholic beverage called koumyss. It does appear from many indications that all nature longs to grow intoxicated, but that man is always interfering.</p>
        <p>SHOULD DYSPEPTILS DIET?</p>
        <p>LIVE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Dress of Two Materials.</head>
        <p>The Dress of Two Materials.</p>
        <p>Now is the time to take advantage of the mode that permits the bodice, or coatee and skirt of a toilette, to be made of two different materifkls. The sales afford opportunities for acquiring short lengths of good stuffs, and the touch of fur which is now so necessary. The coat-bodice may be of brocade, the skirt of any soft silk, satin, fine cashmere, or cloth, of ap- proximately the same colour. Stripes and plain materials combine excellently in this style. Taffetas is a fabric that will be much used next spring, and short lengths should be Tim-chased at sale prices. Checks and shepherds' plaids will also be in the running, in combination with plain materials, and these could be selected from among the bargains. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Crossed Scarf.</head>
        <p>The Crossed Scarf.</p>
        <p>The immense. fur collars on cloaks and gowns are somewhat taking the place of stoles and scarves. The latter are often made of a com- bination of ermine, skunk, racoon or polinsky, mounted on velvet, and are .croseed and re- crossed on the wearer's figure, and then thrown over one shoulder. When artistically arranged the effect is graceftl in, the extreme. Muffs are almost bewildering in their variety, both in shape and fur. 'The prophets assert that the mammoth construction has had its day, and that the reign of the small muff is at hand. And there appears to be some reason for the assertion, since the newest designs are compara- tively quite small. Among these- are the mauclion—called the aeroplane, although it bears no visible resemblance to that most modern of inveutions-7and there is also the small round shape something like a cushion, and a pouch-shaped muff, with pointed ends.</p>
        <p>Marriage Superstitions. There are many superstitions connected with marriage. Among the Burmese an idea pre- vails that it is unlucky for two people to marry who were born on the same day of the week. In order to prevent this disaster, every girl is given a name beginning with the letter of the day of the week on which she was born. The Burmese women enjoy far more privileges and more freedom than those of any other Oriental country, and young people are free to marry the man or woman of their choice, with this one exception.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>DRINK NOT A DEFENCE.</head>
        <p>DRINK NOT A DEFENCE.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens Thomas White, a labourer, was sentenced to three months' imprisonment for stealing a roll of towelling valued at 10s. ed., from the shop of Mrs. Hilton, Duke-street. When arrested he said he was in drink ftnd did not know what he was doing. The Chairman said that prisoner was becoming a nuisance by begging along the street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>SPECIALIST'S VALUABLE ADVICE.</head>
        <p>A SPECIALIST'S VALUABLE ADVICE.</p>
        <p>"Dyspepsia, indigestion, flatulence and prac- tically all' farms of stomach trouble," writes a leading specialist, are in nearly every instance. the direct result of food fermentation and the consequent production in the stomach of acid. and gas which irritate 'and distend the stomach - and make normal digestion impossible. The gas- by distending the stomach and pressing upon the surrounding vital organs seriously interferes with their work, and frequently causes palpi- tation of the heart; IRA the acid is much more dangerous, because it irritates, then inflames,. and evert ulcerates the delicate lining of the stomach, producing dangerous stomach ulcers and even cancer. Food fermentation being; primarily responsible for all the trouble, it was long the custom of physicians to permit the use only of such foods as -not ferment, but un- fortunately unfermentable foods were soon found to he so lacking- in nutrition as to result in partial starvation and rapid loss of strength and vitality. In view of this fact, physicians nowadays seldom, if ever, recommend' dieting, but adopt instead the more logical plan of per- mitting the eating of' any food that may be desired, and then preventing fermentation by neutralising the acidity. This they do by pre- scribing half a teaspoonful of bisnrated mag- nesia in a littlp warm water immediately after meals. This simple antacid is obtainable from any c'nomist. and.so remarkable are its peculiar Properties that dyspeptics and stomach sufferers can eat practically anything they want so long as a little bisureted magnesia is taken imnie- dnfely afterwards." Dieting for dyspentics is seldom necessary, and' will soon be a thing of the past. no sure to get the same as what the doctors nreScribe. It is hisurated magnesia. spelled b-i-s-n-r-a..-t-o-d. Other forms hearing- somewhat similar chemical names are lacking. in its -peculiarly valuable properties.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>BROAD HINT.</head>
        <p>A BROAD HINT.</p>
        <p>Two gentlemen who were playing cards at a, club recently were very much annoyed by other members who stood behind their chairs and; interested themselves in the game. Finally one of the players asked a spectator to play the hand for him until he returned. The spectator took the cards, whereupon- the IThst player left the room. Pretty soon the. second player followed the etaiiiPle of the ileSt The two substitutes played for some time. when one of them asked the waiter where the twi) original players were. the next room "They are playing cards in sir," was the waiter's replY•</p>
      </div>
