      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 24, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 24, 1914.</p>
        <p>They are exciting passions in Ulster, where no respectable politician ought to try and excite passions. If there is a civil war in Ulster it will not be begun by us. The first step will rest with Sir Edward Carson and his Ulstermen. We are not going to fire against anyone. (Hear, hear.) If they fire against anyone, who are they goinf to fire covii ' +aFia men? Tlid Dirty pretenli to civil war in order to force a General Election is guilty of one of the grossest crimes that has over stained our political history. (Hear, hear.) If we can help it, there will not be a civil war, but if there is the beginning of one, I ask you to remember those Protestants and Roman Catholics who have signed the New Covenant, headed by a man who has done as much for the King and country as ever Sir Edward Carson has done. ("More.") I ask you to stand by them who say they are going to stick to demo- cracy, and we will stand by them whatever it costs. (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>LADY GREENALL'S PICNIC TO</head>
        <p>LADY GREENALL'S PICNIC TO</p>
        <p>IRELAND.</p>
        <p>How to Settle a Centtiries'-Old Problem in Sever' bayg.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>THAT WAS DOOMED TO FAILURE.</head>
        <p>THAT WAS DOOMED TO FAILURE.</p>
        <p>day; on Friday they sent nicely balanced tele- grams for publication in %Terrine-on, -which were as follows:</p>
        <p>Humanity demands we should oppose Home Rule,BstuAx (C).</p>
        <p>So far every opportunity has been given to each member of the deputation to make a thorough investigation from his own point of view.---Wsuass (L).</p>
        <p>Am determined to oppose Home Rule.-"---E. WILLIAMS (L).</p>
        <p>United we stand• divided we fall. Will fighte—licDronn. (o). All trade unionist leaders here unite in condemning Home Rule.—RsveNscsorr (L). Home Rule Bill a blunder, and to force same upon Ulster would not only be an out- rage, but a crime.—ENGLAND (L). Nationalists' mismanagement awful. Ulster prosperous and contented. No bluff; every- body in grim earnest.—Brxssyr (C). Ulster ready to fight. They will not have Home Rule.--Rsems (C).</p>
        <p>--- England dare not attempt to for Home Rule upon loyal Ulster.—Ar:Les (C). From appearance workmen in Belfast will not have Home Rule.—Wlarrum (L). Convinced Home Rule would mean disaster</p>
        <p>SIC TRANSIT</p>
        <p>present hanging like a black cloud over the country; and that to force the present measure into law would not only be a blunder but a crime.</p>
        <p>This document was signed on the boat on the return journey, and it will be noted that it con- tains nothing beyond the ordinary stock-in-trade arguments that have been common on our plat- forms for a quarter of a century. It was drawn up and presented for signature; who the o riter was we are left uninformed, but we notice that Mr. England's original sentence about "hlunder," "outrage," and "crime" was utilised as the parting shot in the report. We have asked several of the Liberals who signed it what the document means, and we have been invariably informed that they are not against Home Rule in principle, and the report ohly protests against the "present 'measure." One gentleman defied us to say wherein the report was against Home Rule.</p>
        <p>work against the Home Rule campaign, foe vote against it when the election came round? Is it a fact that one 'member kicked against this part of the arranged programme, and that he could not be induced to sign until the boat had reached New Brighton on its way home? What induced him to sign!, Yiras it a fact ,that this member caused the most anxiety to the Tory party, and that he had to be continually watched, owing to the reason that he was having too great influence over another member of the party, who was considered a "soft thing" for the Tories? Was it a fact that, as far as possible, it was deemed necessary by the Tory section that no two "Liberals" should be allowed together too long? Was this because only the Tories could speak or understand the Irish language? Was it a fact that the Tories very kindly offered to post all letters to the loved ones in England? And that the "Liberals" very innocently allowed them to do•so, and that as soon as the Tories and the letters were out of sight Carson's photograph, with the words "We will not have Home Rule," was stuck on each letter? Was it a fact that one or two of the wives of the "Liberals" kept these letters, and upon the return of the wanderers displayed their re- sentment in very strong terms? • ask the above, questions with a view to arriving at a conclusion as to the value of sending a few men over on a holiday like this upon such a que,tion. , I have always in my mind a previous trip to Germany upon the Tariff Reform subject, and although the trippers on that occasion definitely stated that Tariff Reform must come, we find it now has to take a back seat, by order of the seme party who have worked up this nice little holiday to one or two villages in Ireland. I am very much afraid that the opinions of the members of a deputation like this, consist- ing of the " cream" of three parties in War- rington—Liberal, Tory, and Mr. England's party—will not make the members of the pres- ent Government give Carson or Smith a walk- over.—Faithf ally yours. SERIOUS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>LAST SCENE OF ALL.</head>
        <p>THE LAST SCENE OF ALL.</p>
        <p>The inevitable interview with Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., took place as soon 58 the report was out. He spoke of the, effect the unprejudiced statements would have in opening the eyes of the country. Unprejudiced means not pre- judged, so he had evidently overlooked the fact that Alderman Bennett told his friends on arrival that he decided the matter twenty years</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>"THE TWELVE APOSTLES"</head>
        <p>"THE TWELVE APOSTLES"</p>
        <p>Mr. P. M. Oliver Shows How Futile Was Their Journey. A DEMOCRATIC DEMAND.</p>
        <p>1,1 r. P. M. ()Livia, speaking at the Junior Liberal meeting in Warrington on Monday, said: Now, in regard to Home Rale, Mr. Cros- held, according to Mr. Harold Smith, knows I am not going nothing about Ireland. to speak for Mr. Croslield, but if Mr. Harold smith willlreland.inaketb l hae been i 8 admivs-slobnonn my part: ilik dosr, :lt l than nI in Ireland more y every part of Ireland et I don't understand Ireland, I don't know Ireland, or very little don't haveon once, viasnit d visited, pyracticall about it. If he is willing to have that admis- sion, I make a further statement. Mr. Smith— and, from his name, or ought he is obviousl to be an Enilishman—does not know Ireland, does not Understand it, and can know little about it. (Hear, hear.) I don't say that with any disrespect to Mr. Smith, but I say no Eng- lishman knows and understands Ireland, and that is the sole and whole argument for a measure of Home Rule. They are a different people from us, with different ideals and aspira- tions, and different waye of looking at life, and Mr. that C r oissfi eolnde konf owe nothing about Ireland, the most potent arguments in favour of Home Rule. (Applause.) I make a further statement. I don't think the Twelve Apostles from Warrington, excel- lent people though they may be—six of them sound Conservatives and six of them may be— itfhe Mn (A Voice: "Thy went last week," and laugh- ter)—well, are said to be Liberals. Even they, those twelve apostles, don't know Ireland, don't understand it, and know very little about it. (Hear, hear.) I believe they have come back. (Laughter.) DID THEY HEAR OF THE NEW COVENANT ?</p>
        <p>„ We I should like to-interview them and go up to them, saying. you got to Dublin?” won't ask them what they did before that, it might have been uncomfortable. „Did you see the rtwneYilid3l.:rye? .and they might in ono of the most Roman I should say, "When you got to Dublin, w‘bat did you see?' Ile is the Vicar of Mitchels- tßB( town, e' wyvCn. , Carson Canonrivw" ibntehl we) Chbooeumlr I ha r e had a lot of correspon- Protestant and Roman but for the dceentcbee,lileivpianrgt edeofw inrein people remember Mitchelstewn, not for its riots yet they live on perfectiltderamilsdoi amity, ;Ili n's; good feeling Catholic. There are many other such people wofhrchorex3ittys whom they might have called upon. There is eabrsetwageeon, Mr. Hogg, who won the seat of Londonderry for us. This is one of the centres of Ulster Union- ism. I visited it a few months before the 12 apostles, and saw a great motto, '` We will have Home Rule:, (Applause.) I wonder if they called on Captain White, the son of Sir George White, the hero of Ladysmith? Captain White was not only the son of a hero, but was a hero himself, and went .through the South African War with great distinction. -ri —e was the first man to read and sign in Ulster the New Covenant. What is the New Covenant? Do these apostles know? rl '-' thy on anyone who has signed tidhe N call ewe Coven- ant? It is a covenant drawn uP and signed by Ulster Protestants in favour of Home Rule, and against Carsonism, and pledging themselves to stand for democracy and to refuse to be bound by the decrees of any Provisional Statutory body i which may be set up n Ulster or elsewhere, and if these 12 apostles don't know about this New Covenant they know little about Ulster. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>"THE CIVIL WAR" CRIME. It seems to me, and it probably seems to most Liberals, that this cry on the part of our oppon- ents on the bogey of civil war is simply a form of blackmail to bring a General Election. What- ever happens, nothing will overcome the oppo- sition to Home Rule. If we won the election we are still faced with the opposition to Home Rule, and if we lost, which I don't believe we should, the Conservatives are faced with the opposition of the rest of Ireland. We have this cry for a General Election, which can do noth- ing,o backed up by cries of civil war. A man once asked Mr. Spurgeon, " D you think a man who plays a trombone can go to Heaven?" and Mr. Spurgeon said, "The man who plays a trombone may go to Heaven, but I don't think his next-door neighbour ever will." (Laughter.) Our friends, the Conservatives, are playing the trombone, and they hope by that means to get on the Treasury Bench, which is their Heaven, but whilst they are playing they may be driving Ulster into the Hell of civil war. (Hear, hear.!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>DEVICE</head>
        <p>A DEVICE</p>
        <p>The town has been convulsed—with. laughter, I;:ourse—over the latest Tory appliefition of the linielight to themselves. There has been a trip to Ulster. For the sake of euphony it was called a "deputation" to Ireland, and we may let the point pass; though the idea of a depute-, tion is that those forming its members have been chosen by some body to represent it in its tollective form. The deputation was the . funniest combination surely that was ever col- lected together in the small area ,provided by the deck of a Holyhead boat. Nominally, there were six Conservatives and six Liberals in the Party, which, on the face of it, seemed an equit- able arrangement for the nominal object of the trip, which was " to study the Home Rule ques- tion on the spot." But added to this apparently nicely poised deputation were Alderman rennett, who had been over the same ground efore, and, being a strong Unionist, might be "Pected to do his best in the interests of the to whom he stood in the relationship of 11"1% philosopher and friend. Then, to still further guard against the possibility of any- thing Missing fire, Mr. Herbert Hayes, the brganising secretary of the Conservative party. IV". added, and we see that the 'depUtation of 4iX Conservatives and six Liberals becomes a ofdeputation W eight Conservatives and six Where were the corresponding Liberal representatives to Alderman Bennett and Mr. Hayes? Where was the corresponding Liberal representative to Mr. Smith, the secre- tary of the Conservative Association? That' is a minor matter compared with the manner in which these men were selected to do the impor- tant work they were set to do.</p>
        <p>WHOM DID THEY REPRESENT? We have nothing to say regarding the selection of the eight Unionists. If Unionists wish to "ga on trips 0 Ireland, with all expenses paid, WO cannot object; but we do protest against them grafting upon themselves a tow promiscu- ously selected companions and calling them 'Liberals for spectacular effect. Let us glance st the personnel of the party :</p>
        <p>Conservatives. Liberals. George H. Smith. E. England. • W. H. Bellian. • J. Et Warren. R. Done. J. H. Whitham. J. H. Allen. W. Healey. 1/4 Albert Regan. • G. Ravenscroft. John McDermid. E. Williams. Arthur Bennett, Chairman; Herbert Hayes, Secretary for the Tour. We lave nothing, we repeat, to say about the representatives of the Conservatives; but we r°otest most veheniently against its being made i,•l4PP°Or that the other section represent the • toiu)eral party. The Liberal party were never all that in the matter, and we hope that. is hare Ltt need be said to disillusion those who Ten, ""n misled into the belief that they are bt`7,sentative men. They .were merely asked to .who arranged it, to go on a free trip 100 Ireland, during which they would be well ; —"-t after, and they went. As to their Poli- tical convictions we have. little Concern. Men • Z6,free to hold any political belief they please, ."6 08 they are free to go to Ireland or any- where else, and so long as they do not pose in wr°og light in a manner calculated to mis- . °ad careless observers, we can have nothing to 411Y* We have been assured by several of the Party since their return that they went out Liberals and have returned Liberals, and that t•lie znessages and report that have been issued In their names do not altogether represent their opinions. Although, as we say, it is not our province to PrY into the bona-fides of men wild call them- "lves Liberal, we are entitled to take advan- "kge of facts that are public property. Mr. England, who is classed as a 'Liberal," is a declared Tariff " Reformer," even now, and, • ws Tariff "Reform," hail• been banned for the s Present by the Tories, we may say that Mr. England out-Tories the Tories themselves. •</p>
        <p>THE SEMI-CIRCULAR TOUR. , The fourteen trippers and certain auxiliaries left Warrington on Saturday, January 10th, and returned Saturday, January 17th. They went by Holyhead and Dublin, and came home by Belfast and Fleetwood. In the seven days lying between, they visited Dublin, the West of Ire- land, and " Ulster," arriving at Belfast on Thursday night to be in readiness for the •grand finale, the review of Carson's Volunteers on Saturday. How they were able to make up their minds and report adversely upon the Home 'Ride Bill in so short a time is explained by the fact that their minds wero made up, or were made up for them before they started their tour. "oh, that mine enemy would write a 1°010" They have not written a book yet, hut they made speeches and had them reported. On the evening of the Sunday on which they Arrived in Dublin, before the effects of a wintry aea Upon their landsmen's stomachs cdouldnehravae vanished, vanished, they were entertained to .n • • the Hotel Metropole. Glorious plelne • At., the end of the refection Alderman Bennett said he believed, as 20 years ago, that home Rulef read be disastrous to the best interests o ZAnd. At the same gathering Mr. George d o''nted out that the captains of Irish 11-IonTrY.D, who were present, argued that under 4, ad 1,24:u1e social reforms would be checked "It -11eiftl credit endangered, that tit erest h WOlald be payable on loans, and that "urdens and incidence of taxation Would ore heavily on the Irish corre .2e89 people than the , sPonding classes in Great Britain. It utmost sounds like "Iron is going," "Silk is going," all for the wan "Cotton is want of f tOn "All n Reform in England; or, as the Warringn; Dunip Exhibition showed us, that coffins ad e I If all these things were already Prolong the agony and go by the long of f th way e West of Ireland to Belfast? But at Srg ,heY learned beg that would be' " the in • Home Rule won Assure , fling of the end of the British Empire," And at Enniskillen 'they saw some Yeomanry turn out who had promised to join the resist a - 11. ce• Mr. Joynson-llicks lately told a War- 'llligton audience that Mr. Asquith could "fire, wri.d be damned !'"lhe deputation, we think, '4l,6,holght have told these particular Yeomanry, ~o presumably are wearing the- wil- -1 11:11. that they might, if they fired, be hanged.</p>
        <p>JUST BEFORE THE BATTLE; MOTHER It is a remarkable fact that the "deputation" was not allowed to see the review finale before they gave the result of their "studies on the APot." They arrived in Belfast late on Thum-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>"CAMTHOL"</head>
        <p>"CAMTHOL"</p>
        <p>Srrup O f Camphor Cough Linctus (Dr. Matthias's) For COUGHS, COLDS, BRONCHITIS, &amp;c. Te.stirnonials as to the efficacy of this remedy re- at!tyed from Mdme. Adeline Patti, Lord Bishop of q't. Alban's, Lord Sherborne, Canon Whittington, and others. Prices, 1/11, 2/3. and 4/•. Also post free by HOOPER Si CO., CREMIBTS, 43. KING WILLIAM-ST, LONDON BRIDGE, E.C.</p>
