      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>MR WYNNE JONES' STRICTURES.</head>
        <p>MR WYNNE JONES' STRICTURES.</p>
        <p>Opposition That Will Have No Adverse Effect.</p>
        <p>E. WiNirs Josss, speaking at:tito Junior Libera.l meeting on lionday. said: . .</p>
        <p>We have had interesting time* in Warrington just lately. Mr. Harold Smith ha. introdwed his girl.. (Laughter.) lie has also gone out M his way to tell us she is English. Wel4, she will be when she becomes Mrs. Smith, althouth there is some doubt about the name at the pre- sent time. (Laughter.) We wish them well, and hope they will live long, die happy, and when the die we hope they will go to the place ' where Liberals cease from troubling, and even Tories rest in peace.</p>
        <p>AN EMPIRE OF PARLIAMENTS. Just a word about this deputation to Ireland. (Laughter.) They have sent telegrams back, which no doubt were dispatched immediately after dinner, judging by the statements made. (Laughter.) They tell me that in the churches the regular parsons get the people on the line of conversion and then some great revivalist pulls them over. The Tories do a similar thing. When there is anyone reaching the stage of con- version they send them on a trip to Germany, or, as in this case, they send them on a trip to Ireland. (Hear, hear.) There is Mr. Ebenezer England. None of them are official Liberals. They did not approach the Liberal party to send representatives. They might have sent my friend, Mr. C. E. Parker, for instance—(hear, hear)—but I am afraid he would not put in the telegram what they wanted him to do. Mr. England has been converted once before. He once appeared on the platform at a Tariff Reform meeting in the Parr Hall, by the side of that other Liberal, who became converted after a trip to Germany—Mr. W. Furness. Mr. Ebenezer England has gone back to the Liberal fold, and they thought this would be a gran,i chance to convert him again, and they sent him on a trip to Ireland. But about this deputa- tion and these telegrams. One man makes a statement that "the whole of the trade union- ists here are against Home Rule." (A Voice: "lie's a liar !") I did not want to say that, but lam glad you have put it that way. I say that statement cannot be justified. Then another of them sends a telegram to the effect that if Home Rule is passed it will not only be a sorry 3 -ty for Britain, it will be a sorry day for the British Empire. ("Oh !") Could that man realise for a single minute that in the British Empire you have 28 separate and distinct Par- liaments, and yet if you are going to give Ireland Home Rule and make it 29 it is going to be a sorry day for the British Empire? (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>CONTEMPTIBLE AGITATION:</head>
        <p>A CONTEMPTIBLE AGITATION:</p>
        <p>I quite agree with Mr. Oliver that these people are engaged in one of the meanest and most contemptible agitations ever made. There is nothing meaner than to try and foster reli- gious strife, hatred and animosity. Mr. Swift Mac Neill, in the Parr Hall, said that he was a Protestant and the son of a Protestant clergy- man, and yet he represented in the British House of Commons one of the most Catholic constituencies in Ireland. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>I have no doubt these men have enjoyed themselves—(laughter)—and I have no doubt that the good lady who has paid the expenses— Lady Greenall, we understand, has found the money to send this deputation to Ireland—will be satisfied with the reports, for no doubt they will be able to make the reports to satisfy her. But after all this and other things, nothing is going to stop the passing of the Home Rule Bill. The time has come to reap the harvest of all the work rendered in the interest of this measure in the past, and now the Tory party, do what they may, are not going to stop the passing' of Home Rule. The only object of the Tory opposition is to try and lead our minds away from those great social measures we have in hand. We say the time has ,!ome %hen these people must be put in their places. They are no longer going to hinder Progressive r:.form. (Hear, hear, and applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>SUGGESTIoN. Proposal to Bend a Properly Appointed Deputation</head>
        <p>A SUGGESTIoN. Proposal to Bend a Properly Appointed Deputation</p>
        <p>"OP REAL VALUE."</p>
        <p>To the Editor of " The Examiner."</p>
        <p>_ _ ...... Sir,—As Lady Greenall's enterprise has caused so much comment and amusement in Warrington and in the Press, it has occurred to me that a proper deputation could have been of real value to the electors, if it had been judiciously arranged by both political parties being represented. I venture to suggest that another deputation should go to Ireland, equal number of Liberals and Unionists, and to be conducted by two gentlemen, one on each side of the question. If such a deputation can be arranged I will start the subscription list with £5 and will provide a cinema operator to go with the depu- tation and film all the places and incidents pos- sible on the journey. • On the return of the deputation I will place the Theatre Royal at the disposal of • the partiee, and exhibit, free of charge, the film, and at the same time the depu- tation can make their report. I suggest Mr. H. Smith, M.P., and Mr. A H. Crosfield be invited to attend, and each speak on the subject for, say, thirty minutes. Admis- sion to the theatre could be arranged, and each party have 500 tickets to dispose of at a reason- able price, and the whole of the proceeds be handed over to the Infirmary Fund. If we want the truth, go to business at once, and nail this deputation down and let us have a genuine opinion of the chosen men of each party.—Yours sincerely, 11. W. GRIFFIN. The Central Picture Palace (Warrington), Limited, 26, Sankey-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>WHAT OF THE WOMEN AND – CHILDREN? To the Editor of "The Examiner."</head>
        <p>WHAT OF THE WOMEN AND - CHILDREN? To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Did the " Twelve Apostles "—six Tory, six alleged Liberals—really go to Ulster to study Home Rule? Was there not another object behind it -</p>
        <p>. We have been told at Walton Ilall and else- where that women and children from Ulster are to be brought to Warrington when the dummy guns begin to fire and " the bullets to whistle round." Now, were the twelve sent there really to select presentable women and children to parade in Warrington and not leave the selection to pot luck? I notice one member always keeps the insthetic to the front, and was his selection and dispatch on the stalking expedition really to see that the , other eleven apostles chose the most distin- guished and pretty women and children for Warrington? As there are hundreds of us who believe this was the real mission, we are anxiously awaiting I a report. Will photographs of the expected Ulster guests be supplied to us in advance? A WALTON TORY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
