      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <p>not only to Ireland, but to whole British Empire.—R. Dorm. (C). Convinced Home Rule would destroy pros- perity of Ireland, and that Ulster will fight if Bill forced upon them.--G. H. Siam (C). I have been greatly impressed by what I have seen, and Ireland will not have Home RIIII3.—IIEALEY (L).</p>
        <p>They had seen all Belfast in a day, and decided promptly what Belfast would and would not have. But as Sir Edward Carson had asked some of them, more tian twelve months before, in Parr Hall, Warrington, to exclaim, with their hands on their hearts, "We will not have Home Rule," and as they had complied, the journey and expense appear to have been made in vain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE REPORT.</p>
        <p>One wondered, after these preliminary denun- ciations in miniature, what would happen after the great-review had been seen, and the ex- penses finally settled. The result is quite mild compared with our anticipations. 'The trippers came to the following conclusions, or, at all events, they signed the following conclusions :- I.—That in every town we visited where the Nationalists had the control the rates were' high' and the conditions unsatisfactory. The slums in Dublin are the worst we have seen in any town in the United Kingdom, and the filth in the streets a menace to the general health of the community.</p>
        <p>2.—That in several parts of Ireland the people, at the instigation of the Nationalist organisations, are still indulging in intimida- tions, boycotting and social ostracism. 3.—That in the agricultural districts the Land Acts were gradually effecting a peaceful revolution; that the houses of the peasantry were greatly improved; and that the Home Rule Bill, if passed, would create a state of unrest and disturbance, and would seriously retard the progress of the country. 4.—That in Ulster there was every sign of increasing industry, comfort and prosperity. The rates were comparatively low; the town clean; the slums not existing to anything like the same extent as in Dublin; and municipal affairs appeared to be well and efficiently managed, the contrast in this respect between the Ulster towns and the others being most striking. S.—That the vast majority of the people of North-East Ulster are convinced that Home Rule would ruin the industrial development of the province, and be of no practical benefit to the rest of Ireland, and with that convic- tion we are in agreement. Further, they dis- trust the capacity of the Nationalists for managing the affairs of Ireland so utterly that in every part of the province thousands of men of every class were drilling several nights a week in preparation for resistance to the Bill. The party had opportunities of witnessing this in Enniskillen, Lisbellaw and Belfast, and were deeply impressed by the evidences of the men's sincerity and grim determination, many of them assuring us that they were prepared to lay down their lives sooner than submit to the Home Rule Bill. This was particularly noticeable amongst the working men of Belfast, with whom we had special and unrestricted opportunities of con- versing in several of the largest/works. We are convinced that the Ulster men are deter- mined to resist the Bill at all costs. 6.—That in the interests of humanity, good government, democracy, and Imperial safety alike, it is urgently necessary that the states- men of the United Kingdom should devise some means to obviate the tragedy which is at</p>
        <p>ago. Mr. Smith also spoke of the statements being supported by the " Liberal section." He also spoke of the. justification the report gave to his scheme for quartering Orange refugees upon Warrington, his comments on this aspect of the question taking up by far the greater share of the interview.</p>
        <p>THE " EXES." The expenses of the trip were borne by Lady Greenall. Those who "lost time" were paid their week's wages in addition. Appropriately enough, a cinematograph operator was on the scene; in fact, we are told the thing was thor • °uglily well done throughout:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>SOME QUESTIONS THAT NEED AN ANSWER.</head>
        <p>SOME QUESTIONS THAT NEED AN ANSWER.</p>
        <p>Who, hat, Where and How?</p>
        <p>To the Editor of " The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Will you kindly allow me space to ask a few questions with reference to the above trip? Who picked the so-called representatives of the Liberal party? Are they all Liberals? Were the Liberal leaders consulted in the matter?</p>
        <p>Mr. England, who seems ever ready for trips of this description, said he represented,a section of the English people who had been somewhat apathetic, &amp;c., &amp;c. Whom does he represent? Is he a member of the Liberal party? If not, what is he at all in politics? How many times has he changed his politics? Has he not been on the Tariff Reform plat- form, and sat side by side with that other well- known ten days' tripper, Mr. William Furness? MIA he ever been a Home Ruler? Would the Liberal Party have chosen him to represent them on this Particular holiday tour? Are the other members of the tour chosen as the cream of the Liberal party? This question of Home Rule has been fully considered for over 30 years, and the whole of Ireland has been visited, and the views of the people taken. Are the trippers competent to settle it in seven days? Who wrote the telegrams? /f the Liberals did, who stood over them whilst they did it? Have they visited the whole of Ireland, or only places prepared for them? Are any of the men chosen, Tory or Liberal, in a position to express an independent view, or even have the .eturage to express a wish to depart from the programme arranged for them beforehand? - -</p>
        <p>If the Liberal party desired to take six Liberals and six Tories on a tour of this descrip- tion, would the Tories agree to the programme being arranged without their being consulted? Is it not a fact that the drilling of men and rifle practices only commenced as soon as the deputation came into view? _ _ _</p>
        <p>, Is it a fact that each member of the deputation had to decide, before leaving Warrington, that Ireland did not want Home Rule, and to pro- mise to sign a document purporting to be a Majority Report against Home Rule, and con- taining declarations that they would not only</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Why were the Warringtonians not taken to Harland and Wolff's?</head>
        <p>Why were the Warringtonians not taken to Harland and Wolff's?</p>
        <p>WHY DID THE ." LIBERALS" EVADE THE ,ASSOCIATION? ULSTER LIBERAL</p>
        <p>The following article appears in to-day's " Ulster Guardian," which, with the exception of the quoted telegrams which appear on this page, we reproduce, by courtesy of our contemporary, intact:—</p>
        <p>LATEST CARSONIAN FARCE.</p>
        <p>STORY OF A MARRINGTON DEPUTATION.</p>
        <p>CAME TO STUDY HOME RULE AND HEARD ONLY ONE SIDE OF THE QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Fourteen Warrington (Lancashire) men have just returned home from a week's tour in Ire- iand, for the purpose of studying the Irish ques- tion. on the spot, and, posing as authorities on the subject, have signed statements against Home Rule. I propose to toll the story of that deputation, and when I am finished my readers will, I think, agree with me that the tour through Ireland was a farce and that the opinion of the members of the deputation was not worth the paper it was written on. The deputation consisted of six Conservatives and six Liberals, and was accompanied by Mr. Herbert Hayes, organising secretary of the Warrington Conservative party, and Mr. G. IL Smith, hon. secretary of the Conservative Asso- ciation.</p>
        <p>The entire expenses of the trip were paid by Lady Greenall, wife of Sir Gilbert Greenall, the brewer, who as recently become an Irish land- lord and is President of the Warrington Con- servative Association. Lady Greenall's interest in Ulster is indicated by the fact that she is President of the Women's Tariff Reform League, which has been engaged in the task of looking ifter the scatter-brained scheme of Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., for finding board and lodging (both of which have been scornfully refused by Sir E. Carson) for the women and children of the Ulster Volunteer warriors when they take the field. The six Liberals were selected by the organisers of the trip, and at least one of these gentlemen is a strong supporter of Tariff Reform, a fact which of course deprives him of the right to be called Liberal, since Free Trade is one of the fundamental beliefs of the party led by Mr. Asquith. The deputation arrived in Dublin on Sunday, the 11th inst., and 'left Belfast on Saturday night, the 17th. In the interval they visited Longford, Sligo, Enniskillen, Omagh, and Portadown, afterwards coming to Belfast. Every mile of the tour was carefully arranged before- hand by certain Ulster Unionists, and although the members of the deputation perhaps were not aware of the fact, they were as much under the control of the Unionist stage manager as if they were chained. Indeed, they could just as easily, and much more cheaply for Lady Greenall, have studied the Irish question at the headquarters of the Warrington Conservative Association with a bundle of Ulster Unionist literature at their command. They would, of •ourse, then only have read one side of the question; just as they only heard one side when they wer6 in Ireland. In Dublin—l am now quoting from an official account of the doings of the deputation in a Warrington Conservative paper—they met at dinner gentlemen who talked about Home Rule being to "the lasting detriment of Ireland"; in Sligo they spoke to "several of the principal Unionist business men and shopkeepers"; at Enniskillen they witnessed "a parade of the Enniskillen Horse"; at Lisbellaw, Co. Fer- managh, they saw a regiment (sic) of Volun- teers "consisting of 164 men." In the whole account of the deputation's wanderings there is not a single sentence to show that they heard the views of responsible men in favour of Home Rule; still less that they studied the question, even if there had been time, which, of course. i there was not.</p>
        <p>Why, some of the members of the deputation did not even know the geography of the country, for their spokesman, in an interview in the Belfast "News-Letter," commented on the difference between the people of the North and Sputh, although the deputation had not been in the South at all, for, as any schoolboy knows, Longford is in the Midlands, and Sligo in the West, while Dublin is in the East, the remainder of the places visited being in Ulster.</p>
        <p>The deputation arrived in Belfast on Thurs- day night, and from the moment they left the train at the Great Northern terminus until they went aboard the steamer on Saturday night they were guarded carefully from association with the terrible Home Rulers; indeed, no Sultan's harem was watched more jealously than were these simple Lancashire men who came to hear all sides, but did not. It is the custom when you have guests to show them your best, but Ulster Unionists do not care a straw for custom when it suits their purpose to break it. So, instead of being taken, as they ought, to the shipyard of Messrs. Harland Sc, Wolff, which is certainly the most famous, as it is the biggest, establish- ment of the kind in the world, although it is controlled by Lord Pirrie, a staunch Home Ruler, who pays X 28,000 weekly in wages, the deputation were escorted, perhaps " led " would be the better word, to the yard of Messrs. Work- man, Clark 8; Co., where they were received by Mr. George S. Clark and taken through the yard. "In the yard," says the "News-Letter," "the deputation were allowed the utmost freedom in regard to interviewing the workers, and they got into conversation with men belonging to both sides of polities." The feat of the Ameri- can who after a twenty-four hours' sojourn in Ireland was prepared to settle the Irish ques- tiKi in half an hour was nothing compared with the feat of the Warrington deputation in finding in the yard owned by Messrs. Workman, Clark and Co., workers in favour of Home Rule and being allowed to hear from these same wtrkers reasons why a Dublin Parliament should be established.</p>
        <p>The deputation alSo inspected York-street Flax Spinning Mill and the Sirocco Engineering Works, being received at the former by Sir William Crawford and at the latter by Mr. Davidson, whose violent antipathy to anything approaching Home Rule has been frequently demonstrated during the past year. Not a single business establishment controlled by a Home Ruler was visited in Belfast. I have made the most careful inquiries, and I find that during the time they were in Belfast</p>
        <p>Ft Good Suggestion,</p>
        <p>the deputation did not spc,al fo any pergon of known Home Rule sympath;ms. They did not call at the offices of the Ulste, Liberal Associa- tion, where they would have tsard the views held by thousands of Protestant Home Rulers scattered all over Ulster; they did not see any members of the United Irish League.. although common courtesy might have suggested that the opinions held by the majority ,of Irishmen deserved at least a perfunctory hearing. No, the chain was kept too tight, and although the deputation had in their possession the name of at least one Belfast gentleman who would be able and willing to state clearly and concisely the reasons why Home Rule should be granted, the gentleman was not troubled with a. visit from the seekers of information.</p>
        <p>As a further proof that all the talk about studying the question was pure pretence I give below the text of telegrams sent on Friday. fore- noon by thirteen members of the deputation to a Warrington newspaper. When the messages were despatched the visitors had only been a few working hours, so to speak, in the city, for not even a Warrington man can investigate the Home Rule question when he is asleep. There is quite a Carsonian touch about 'these messages, and I believe I could in three guesses at the most name the person who wrote them. I suppose Mr. Ravenscroft, who sent the fifth message quoted, thought he was telling the truth; but he was made an abject fool of, and the la9t that those who spoon-fed him allowed him to put on paper. such stupid lie shows that they had a very poor opinion of Mr. Raiens- croft's mental capacity. Most .of the trade union leaders are as strongly in fakour of Home Rule as Mr. Joseph Devlin is, and any Belfast apprentice could have told Mr. Ravenscroft so. I make Sir Edward Carson a present of his Warrington "converts," and I reSpectfully suggest that there should ho a public meeting in the Lancashire town, attended by the Irish Unionist leader, and that Lady Greenall should be invited to formally hand over to Sir Edward Carson the six Liberals, including the Tariff Reformer. Tier Ladyship deserves all the honour possible. X.Y.Z.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>"CONSCRIPTION, WHY NOT 9" Vicar of Burtonwood's Original Views.</head>
        <p>"CONSCRIPTION, WHY NOT 9" Vicar of Burtonwood's Original Views.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. IL. Mitchell, vicar of Burton- wood, has something original to say on the subject of conscription, now much talked about. Writing in his Parish Magazine under the title of " Conscription, why not?" he says: , Yes, of course, why not? The nation is ask- ing for it, the people demand it. Why with- hold that which is for the country's good, for the public benefit? What-ho! cries the fire- eating Commander-in-Chief of the British Army, have we captured a troublesome par- son? "The latest conversion to militarism and conscription," proclaims the National Service League. Alt! just a moment, please, not quite so fast; we don't all keep motors for the pleas- ure of racing, skidding and running down peace- ful pedestrians. Is there one only kind of con- scription? One only kind of compulsory ser- vice? and is that 'service military service? Compulsion to military service is unchristian, inhuman, and altogether abominable. No! No! We want none of your military conscrip- tion; the recruiting sergeant, his plausible tales and lying tongue, notwithstanding, is better by a long way than the compulsory en- rolling officer.. There can be no toleration in free England for compulsory soldiering. Con- scription will never be submitted to which has for its object the dressing of every eligible lad in red or khaki uniform. No compulsion, please, towards barracks, canteens, and mili- tary encampments.</p>
        <p>But all the same, let us have conscription, civic conscription, trade conscription. Aye, every man, every woman of 21, a' worker, a con- script in the civil, industrial service for three 3-ears. There's conscription for you worth hav- ing, conscription we must seek for and insist on. Every man, every woman, trained to•ser- vice ! trained to civic life! to citizenship! to industry! to trade! to the professions! What a changed England that will be when tho, place of the idler, the parasite, the sycophant can nowhere be found. To this end, conscription is necessary, will be as welcome as it is neces- sary:</p>
        <p>All fit to work must become conscripts; there must be no distinctions, no exceptions. All must learn to be useful, to support theiiinves, to earn their own living in the school of service. That will be something like an army in which all able to work are enrolled to do service to their country, by learning how to work, how to plough, how to make straight furrows, how to get coal, how to manufacture, how to make water-tight boots, how to build, how to teach, how to nurse, how to cook, how to be healthy, how to prevent disease, how to live long. Con- scription of this kind is Christianity in its prac- tical working. "If a man will not work, neither should he eat."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WILL IT LAST?</head>
        <p>WILL IT LAST?</p>
        <p>Vt idnes Case.</p>
        <p>"But will my cure last?" That is the anxious question of the unhappy kidney sufferer, and the assuring word of this Widnes resident, who has stayed cured for twelve months, will be glad news indeed.</p>
        <p>- On January 13th, 1919, Mrs. R. Dennett, of 12, Charles-street, Simms Cross, Widnes said: "I was subject to kidney complaint for several years. I have had a large family, and have no doubt this has weakened my kidneys. I suffered from terrible pains in the small of my back, which were often so severe that I would have to cry out. When I stooped the pains were like knives being driven into my back. I was 1 weary and tired, and my work was a burden to me. The pains in my head nearly drove me out of my mind. " The urinary system was out of order, there was a constant desire for relief, and yet the water waepainful and scanty and full of a dark, heavy sediment. "Through reading about a similar case being cured by Doan's Backache Kidney Pills I thought I would try them. To my delight they soon began to put me right. Two boxes of this excellent medicine cured me and improved my general health. I have need them since then, and they always do me good. "One time the shopman sent some other pills, which I tried, but I might as well have thrown my money away, for they did me no good. Now I always stick to Doan's Pills, as I have proved their great value by experience. I have widely recommended thorn amongst my friends. (Signed) R. Dennett." On November 2Gth, rrl, --...,---24ZNARLY TWELVE MONTHS LATER—Airs. Dennett said: "My Cure by Doan's Pills has been a lasting one, and my health is now splendid." Price 2/9 a box, 6 boxes 13/9; of all dealers. or from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street. London, W. Don't ask for back- ache or kidney pills—ask DISTINCTLY for Doc/ II Backache Kidney Pills, the same as Mrs. Den- nett had.</p>
