      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>ORIGINAL IDEAS PRESENTED.</head>
        <p>ORIGINAL IDEAS PRESENTED.</p>
        <p>.4ma exceedingly original and thoughtful paper on "110 w to Reform Mankind" was given by Mr. E. Marshall. of Penketh, to thu Sankey Bridges Men's Adult School on Sunday morning.</p>
        <p>Many of the lecturer's remarks were of a very revolutionary and highly debatable nature and gave rise to an animated and vigorous discus- sion. Mr. Marshall said that men—even the best of men—were born with defects, and their thoughts and actions were largely governed by their surroundings. The present was the off- spring of the past, the parent of the future. lion-yone was seeking happiness, and upon a man's mind depended the manner in --tbich he sought to satisfy his desires, and what tSey shall be, whether base and sensual, or high and cle.an.</p>
        <p>The wanta of a savage were few, civilisation increased wants. The intellectual horizon widened and the brain demanded more and more. The passions of a savage were imin- fluented by thought, while the thought of the philosopher was not influenced by passion. Children were dominated by desires and pas- siting before they were able to reason. It was the same with undeveloped man—he took the line of least resistance. When lie failed in big search for happiness he called in the aid of the supernatural; but, in spite of these things, the brain of man had been slowly but surely devel- opix" At first muscular, brute force demin- ated, but now brain and muscle were working hand in hand, and man generally was learniug to think. Head and hand were becoming partners. Almost everything depended upon the child. Children should be surrounded by everyttlieg beautiful; taught to think for themselves, hut should be brought into contact with the thoughts of others. Teach a child to be self- supporting, teach him that anyone, whether rich or poor, who did not work for his food and elothing, was a parasite and did not justify hie existence. Teach them also to avoid waste— waste of time, of energy and of wealth—then they should no longer see, for instance, the ingenuity and labour of man spent in creation of instruments of war, weapons made for killing and maiming other men, but would learn to pat their wealth to a better use.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH MEN'S.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH MEN'S.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. M. Walker on Sunday gave a short lecturette on "A Poet of the People." The speaker gave a brief but most interesting sketch of Patrick McGill, the navvy poet, who, at the ago of 21, created quite a stir in the literary world with his book of poems, entitled "Soril;i of the Dead End," on account a their powcr and passion. Several selections were given, showing his freshness of outlook on various soh- jects, including love of home, the beauties of Nature, and the varied experiences of a navvy's life. , That he paper was wen received was evident from the number of quotations referred to by</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>BEWSEY MEN'S.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY MEN'S.</p>
        <p>An interesting discussion followed a short lec- turette on the " Statescraft of Machiavelli," on Sunday morning. This led to a consideration of tho position of diplomatists in European life to-day. Most members felt that "high policy" amongst the nations was left too exclusively to small groups of diplomatists. Democracy was insistently demanding a more open-handed foreign policy, for the nations would never get outside the range of war until secret bargain.' ings ceased. Secret treaties created fear and distrust, and tended to aggravate the inter- national situation. Several members spoke of the necessity for the House of Commons to have full control of the settlement of treaties. Where the life of the people was concerned it was for the representa- tives of the people to shape and settle treaties. No sane democracy could possibly tolerate any official group of diplomatists to possess such powers In view of present conditions another illustration was thus provided, showing the urgent need of a better educated democracy. The workers must be more intellectually and morally equipped to make their decision on such far-reaching issues.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>ACADEMY-STREET MEN'S.</head>
        <p>ACADEMY-STREET MEN'S.</p>
        <p>--•-- -- Mr. Ford, of New Zealand, paid a visit to the School on Sunday morning, and gave a very interesting address, containing useful informa- tion as to the life and events in the Antipodes (New Zealand and Australia). It is essential that emigrants to New Zealand, especially, should have plenty of pluck and backbone, tol rough it. Adaptation is a sine qua non, old methods and customs must be left behind, and start life anew. It is to men of such calibre that the progress of New Zealand is due; pioneer settlers who have gone out into the hush and suffered hardship and worked strenu- ously to open the country for civilisation. Life generally, however, is not so bright, owing, per- haps, to the geographical position and climate, as one enjoys in the Old Country, nor is it even equal to that of West Australia, amongst the. "Sand-gropers of Cinderella," as the inhabitants , of that colony are called. The great question at present in New Zealand is the prohibition of the drink traffic; and in , this the newcomers invariably hinder the work of the crusade for the abolition of drink. Fur- ther, too, some of the mast educated and monied men are against the Measures, which need the three-aftlis majority to carry into effect. Strik- ing eiamples were given of prominent men addicted to the immoderate use of stimulants, and of the difficulties the crusade had to combat. When the three-fifths majority is attained— and it is now very near—the speaker consoled himself with the fact that the brewing industry would then have as g;eat difficulty to regain a similar majority to roverse the injunction. . .</p>
        <p>A fireside chat after school elicited several other phases—conscription, sugar industry, &amp;c</p>
        <p>MEANING OF THE ULSTER AGITATION. would be sheer waste of time at the</p>
        <p>present moment to enter upon a discussion of the possible lines which an endeavour to reach an agreement on the Irish problem might fol- low. It would be a truly academic discussion. All the guidance which Sir EDWARD CARSON gives is to lay down the ultimatum that "there is no use in holding conversations or negotia- tions unless they give us as a basis the preser- vation under the Imperial Parliament of those rights which our ancestors won." We are. not told what rights or what ancestors. He may mean that a Protestant and Unionist ascend- ancy must be preserved in effect, though under an altered form. He may mean that the representation of Ulster in the Imperial Parlia- ment, as fixed by the Act of Union, is not to be diminished; forgetting that the, " Unionist" party, in proposing a scheme for reducing the total Irish vote, has itself proposed to reduce the number of Ulster's representatives.</p>
        <p>Or he</p>
        <p>may mean something muit more consonant with the principles of political &amp;Linty, demo- cracy, and of the liberty which has become the life-blood and the chief cementing influence of the British Empire. But if he means something of this nature, his words give a very faint indi- cation of it.</p>
        <p>To those who imagine that the words of the Oppogition leaders have any definite meaning, their attitude presents an insoluble riddle. It</p>
        <p>the Opposition are honestly fighting against a measure which they believe would inflict</p>
        <p>"slavery" or "serfdom " upon a pall of the British Empire, it is unthinkable that the Oppo- sition leaders should bind themselves by any self-denying ordinance not to oppose such "slavery" or "serfdom," if it is to be inflicted, under the mandate of a majority of British electors. It seems almost unthinkable that a party which would resist "serfdom", and " slavery " for any class of the British people would consent to acquiesce in it, provided that the gambling chance of another General Election were secured to the party which froths</p>
        <p>over with righteous indignation against some- thing whit it will take lying down if it has had that gambling chance. "Slavery" and "serfdom " are trivial things in the balance against a gambling chance far the Tory party to get back into office. It would not be difficult to sum up the whole Ulster question, as far as it makes a part in what might be nominally called "practical politics," and is not involved in the whole Irish question, by a single phrase. The Tory party, which formerly wanted to gamble in the food of the nation, now wants to gamble in Ulster, stock in the hope of "bearing" the Parliament Act. The Parliament Act goes some way; but far from the whole way, in putting Liberal measures on a fair level with Tory measures, and Ulster is but a pawn in a game that the Tory 'party is playing in the hope of check- mating that Act, which has its mandate, on principle from one, in detail from a second, General Election. Mr. BOMAR LA vr has let the</p>
        <p>secret out of the hag. What the Tory party wants is not security for Ulster against "slavery" and "serfdom," but another oppor- tunity to defeat the Parliament Act.</p>
        <p>The Usual Way.. Mr. Lamb, a King's. Counsel,. when Lord Erskine was in the height of his reputation, was of timid mannem usually prefacing his plead- ings with an apology to that effect; and one occasion, when opposed, in some cause, to F.rsidne, he happened to remark that "he felt , hirnsell growing more and more timid as he grew older." "No wonder,". replied the witty barrister, "everyone knows • the, Older a lamb grows the more sheepish' he becomes.".</p>
        <p>,</p>
        <p>CThe St. George's Guild, of which Ruskin, was LOTBS PRAISED BY RUSKIN. master, founded at Larey, in the Isle of Man, a mill for the production of good honest cloths. Any- one needing Dress Lengths, Suitings, Rugs, etc., as recently supplied to H.M. the King, of unim- peachable quality, and beautiful design, is invited to send for patterns to THE RUSKIN HOMESPUN, 110, St. George's Mill, taxey, Isle of Mn or G. HOLYBOYD &amp; SON, 110, Station-street. Huddersfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 24, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 24, 1914.</p>
        <p>BIRTHS. MARRIAGES. DEATHS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>PASSING WEEK..</head>
        <p>THE PASSING WEEK..</p>
        <p>THE TRIP.</p>
        <p>_ What will be known as TIIE Trip ended a week ago, and the travellers who returned from tkeir pilgrimage in Ireland—extending over the long period of seven days—have already set- tled down to their ordinary avocations. They, we trust, are none the worse, just as the rest of us are none the worse for their remarkable experiences which included the sight of a, " regiment " of 164 men engaged in "various , movements" at a village called Lisbellaw, and the review of Carson's army which took the field on Saturday without even wooden guns. Still, guns or no guns, it was something to know that the men were determined, for they turned up for review and went through their , manceuvres in " grim " manner. On page swe give the inner history of this epoch-making visit to Ireland, in which twelve—or to be quite accurate, fourteen—Warrington men and a cinematograph operator decided the practical side of a problem that has puzzled the keenest t brains among politicians down the centuries.</p>
        <p>It is something to their credit, too, that they arrived at their conclusions a day before Carson took to the field last Saturday, for their edifi- cation, for we knew in Warrington on Friday evening that Home Buie would be "worse than a blunder—lt would be a crime." The trip and its report were intended to make our flesh creep; but the Fat Boy's tactics have been over- done, and we shall sleep in peace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>FOOTBALL TROUBLE.</head>
        <p>THE FOOTBALL TROUBLE.</p>
        <p>A largely-attended meeting of the members of the Warrington Football Club, called to con- sider the circumstances connected with the strike of the first team players, decided on Thursday night that the committee—which in- cludes the ollicials—had acted in the best inter- ests of the club and that they might be trusted to go on unfettered towards the goal of settle- ment. That decision was the strongest pos- sible evidence of the strength of tile case of the 1 committee, and we hope that the players will come to see things as the meeting was brought to see them. It was clear when the meeting opened that feeling ran high against the com- mittee, but so convincing were the arguments and the facts as presented by Councillor Osborne and Mr. Bradshaw, that the meeting veered completely round, and only five Ifands in a thousand were held up against the vote of confidence passed in the committee. We hope that this clearing of the air will have the same effect upon the players, and that we shall once more see them and the committee going for- ward in unity with the one object of forcing the name of Warrington towards the top of the League ladder. _</p>
        <p>At last night's conference between players and committee the strike was settled, and the team will turn out to-day against Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>DR. MELUOR.</head>
        <p>DR. MELUOR.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. cle-l-lor"s stay-i-n—Warrington has been much too short. We were not long before we learnt how to appreciate his worth, and to understand the value that was to be placed Upon his efforts for the social betterment of the People. We were expecting Dr. Mellor to become a driving force in the town, but fortune has decreed that he shall go to the larger sphere of Liverpool. Hope-street is an influential church, with a record and traditions that will give full scope for the activities of such a man. The place is associated with the name of Dr. Martineau, and, in later years, with that of the Rev. Richard A. Armstrong, wrip so vigorously engaged M temperance work and the New Babylon campaign: Dr. Mellor leaves us on the 30th April, after a Otly of a little over two years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>SCIENCE AND _SMOKE.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE AND _SMOKE.</p>
        <p>After the scientific explanation given to a Warrington audience by such an authority as Professor Vivian Lewes, of Greenwich, that smoke from carbonaceous fuel is highly in- jurious to animal and vegetable life, perhaps our local smoke reformers may no longer be characterised as faddists, but looked upon as inert of a philanthropic LYl&gt;e. out to benefit the conditions of their fellows. Smoke, the Prof eS- sor demonstrated, is not a sign of prosperity, W but a stupendous waste of valuable fuel and the progenitor of various types of pulmonary ton, which, through its gas stoves and fires, has done more than any I disease. Yet in arring other town to reduce the smoke and fog nuis- ance in London, we suffer from a trouble that might be considerably reduced,l.not altogether a eradicated. We noted a number of members of the Health Committee mong the audience, and we hope the lessons driven home by the Pro- fessor may have some effect upon them, and that the sequel may prove an earnest attempt on their part to reduce "that smoke, black or yellow."</p>
        <p>A P.S.N. "COMING OF AGE." comin It must have been a Joy DaY for the surviving members of the founders of the Warrington P.S.A., when they met on Sunday, to celebrate the g-of-age o the society. They were reminiscent, as they had every right to be, for Sunday marked an epoch in the history of the f Warrington P.S.A. Started with fourteen mernbers, it has grown steadily and surely, and to be attained in the future. Twenty-one the progress it made in the past is likely to be eclipsed by the progress year ago the P.S.A. was looked upon as a daring innovation, and Mr. Blackburn, the pioneer of the Movement, who s hailed from West Bromwich, was deemed little better than a fanatic. The good work accom- plished has killed the scepticism which marked the movement in the early days, and• now few churches with any pretension to catering for those outside the congregations could do without the P.S.A., or a kindred society under another name. The Warrington P.S.A. has led the van in our town. Long may it con- tinue to do so, and the more imitators, no ! matter what name they pass under, there are, the better will it prove for the uplifting of the masses of the community.</p>
        <p>GREATEST FRIENDLY SOCIETY MAYOR. Our genial Mayor appears to be in danger of becoming n n colininethe greatest Friendly Society mem- gland. Prior to Saturday he had been installed a member of different societies, and the Oddfellows were the latest to capture him, his installation taking place on Saturday. with the usual ritual of that popular body. So numerous have his joinings of Friendly Societies become that we confess. there is a danger of forgetting to which societies he does belong. It would appear a's if soon we shall be able to write him down a member of all. We have had a pretty wide experience of Mayors in Warrington and elsewhere, and we cannot recall one who has done the Friendly Society business in such a whole-hearted and whole- some manner. So far as can be ascertained, our Mayor has established a national record in this direction, and in setting up the record he is bound to have given a boom to the cause of the Friendly Society movement. When young men see the Mayor joining freely they will have less hesitation in doing likewise, and this will prove of benefit to the societies, and of still greater advantage to the young member; themselves. The Mayoral acknowledgment should thus do double good.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WASTED BRAINS.</head>
        <p>WASTED BRAINS.</p>
        <p>We are glad to know that steps are being taken in Warrington to formulate a scheme by which the educational ladder from the elemen- tary schools up to the universities will be com- pleted by the granting of public scholarships. Mr." Henry Roberts, presiding at the monthly meeting of the Education Committee on Mon- day night, foretold a plan by which it seems possible that the first scholarship to a Univer- sity, given by the town, will be awarded during the present year. The details are not yet worked out, but if they are so far advanced as to warrant this promise, there is much cause to rejoice at the rapid progress that has been made. As was emphasised during the discus- sion, it is wrong not only to a brilliant child with brains, but also to the State, that those brains should be wasted because a parent is too poor to find University fees. Mr. Roberts made it clear that the scholarships will be available only for the class of children whose parents are unable to send their children to College, a very necessary stipulation in view a the abuse to which such privileges are some- times subjected. We have a letter from a former Warrington scholar, now in College, congratulating us upon the fact that there are to be public scholarships in Warrington to prevent the recurrence of the brain wastage of the past, and upon the in- fluence "The Examiner" has, as he feels sure the prominence We recently gave to the subject has hastened the step now to be taken. While we are pleased to think that anything we have done may have contributed to this happy issue. we must say that an enlightened Education Committee will be entitled to all the praise when they have placed us in possession of these facilities for the advancement of the people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>INDUSTRIAL MOTORS.</head>
        <p>INDUSTRIAL MOTORS.</p>
        <p>The Right Hon. R. McKenna opened the new extension of the Motor Works of the Wolseley Motor Car Company this week. Messrs. Vickers (Limited) occupy a leading position in com- merce, employing in all their various works and branches upwards of 30,000 hands. Founded in 1901, the Wolseley Company was a branch of Vickers, although a private company. Then, as now, explained the Chairman (Sir Vincent Caillard), their policy was to use the best materials and workmanship. In 1901 their motor car works occupied 90,000 square feet of floor space. To-day the works covered twenty- , one acres, of which 727,000 square feet comprised the floor area, seventeen acres of machine and other shops. Quite a feature of these Wolseley works is the large number of automatic machine tools. There is a day shift of 650 men in this one de- partment in charge of over, 1,000 machines out of a total of 1.420 installed in the car factory. The Wolseley works are self-contained, and the making of a car can he seen step by step. Amongst those invited to view the new works, in addition to Mr. McKenna, .were Colonel Holden, the Lord Mayor of Birmingham, the Roumanian Minister, the Chinese Minister, the Bulgarian Minister, Lord Norton, Mr. J. W. Orde, Captain Davidson, and, others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>aljurtli li/du%</head>
        <p>aljurtli li/du%</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. There was a good meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. on Sunday, presided over by the Secre- tary, Mr. Farrar, who spoke of his joy at meet- ing an old friend in the speaker, Mr. James Jackson, of Southport. The secretary reminded the brethren of the approaching 21st anniver- sary, which will be celebrated on the first Sunday in March. They hoped for a grand rallying time on that occasion. Mr. James Jackson chose as his subject, "We are saved by hope." Faith and hope, he said, were twins, and they must have both—to live for time and eternity. The hope of the Gospel nad saved many from disaster, as the hope of, business had -saved many from ruin. The' hope of the Gospel would bring the world's sal- vation. The Chairman referred to the happy testi- monies of work done during the 21 years of the P.S.A.'s existence. Men had been brought to the light, homes had been made bright and , happy, and joy and peace had been brought , amongst young and old. Mr. Yearsley also took part in the meeting, and Mr. Payton presided at the organ. .DR. MELLOR TO LEAVE WARRINGTON A Nee Appointment in Liverpool. The Rev. Dr. Stanley A. Mellor, of Cairo- street Chapel, Warrington, has been appointed Pastor of the Hope-street Church, Liverpool, and will shortly go to take up his new duties in the seaside city. His departure will prove a great loss to the religious life of Warrington, but as he will be close at hand, ,doubtless he will be 'a frequent visitor to the town. Dr. Mellor was appointed to Cairo-street Chapel early in 1912, and preached his first ser- mon as Pastor there on 17th March, 1912. His discourse at once stamped him as a pulpit</p>
        <p>tee. -172 F, , . „ , . • . . ' ; .tps, ;ppm • ,</p>
        <p>TTIR REV. Dtc. S. A. MELLOR, who is leaving Warrington for Liverpool.</p>
        <p>orator of remarkable gift and power, and this first impression has been amply confirmed dur- ing his sojourn In Warrington. When he has a message he drives it home true and pointedly. Dr. Mellor, who succeeds the well-known Liverpool minister, the Rev. 11. D. Roberts, was educated at Hertford College, Oxford, was at Manchester College, Oxford, from 1904 to 1907, Ilibbert Scholar. Berlin, and Harvard, 190749' It is an interesting fact that Part of Dr. Mellor's education should have been secured at Manchester College, an institution which owes its inception to Warrington, and especially 'Cairo-street Chapel people.</p>
        <p>Got What She Deserved. Andre De Fouquieres, the cotillon leader o: Paris, is, like most canon leaders, very gentle and mild of manner. During M. de Fouquieres' visit to New York a well-known matron, after talking to him for some 10 or 15 minutes at a dance, said hi her well-known way : "Now trot along, M. de Fouquieres. altogether too ladylike for me." The young Frenchman, rising, answered: " I'm ,sorry I Can't say the same for you, madam."</p>
        <p>You're</p>
        <p>Pviu FTRFI GinTrs never fail to please.— Sole agent. J. S OAR, 1, Ironmonger, 1 to 45, Bridge-street, Warrimgton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>SUEhU., AND SigiOKE.</head>
        <p>SUEhU., AND SigiOKE.</p>
        <p>A Learned Professor Discourses on Mc Pollution of the Atmosphere. WHAT WARRINGTON DOES FOR LONDON.</p>
        <p>A long table laden with glass vessels, syphons and Bunsen burners filled the platform of the Co-operative Hall, Warrington, on Wednesday, giving the appearance of a chemical laboratory, while behind were numerous diagrams. All this display was to demonstrate the evils of black smoke. Professor Vivian B. Lewes, of Greenwich, was the demonstrator, and the meeting was under the auspices of the newly- formed Warrington and District Smoke Aba' ment League.</p>
        <p>Lte-f</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>POPULAR DELUSION.</head>
        <p>A POPULAR DELUSION.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. PARKER, who presided, said there was a popular delusion that those taking an interest in smoke abatement were faddists, men who had their eyes in the air, and that they did not realise the practical difficulties there were in bringing about an abatement of smoke.</p>
        <p>„ There was a further delusion, widely spread and deeply ingrained in Warrington, that it was a necessary part of manufacture that a great •deal of smoke should be emitted, hence they concluded that those works emitting smoke were, the most prosperous—the idea was there was an inseparable connection between prosperity anti a great amount of smoke. Those inter- ested in the formation of the League believed, without causing any injustice to manufacturers, or others, that the smoke emitted in Warring- ton might be very considerably reduced. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Money Question.</head>
        <p>A Money Question.</p>
        <p>This was a money question, as well as onel that might be considered from the (esthetic point of view—they in Warrington were very ' much influenced by money considerations—and while they considered that calculated from the amount of coal consumed in this part of the country, there were at least 12,000 tons of solid matter given off from the chimneys, both factory and domestic. Fortunately the winds carried away probably five-sixths, and 2,000 tons of solid matter came down upon the ground, houses and people of the borough. If it were possible for the soot to come to depots and be carted away, the trouble would not be so great, although they would realise more than they did the amount of matter that had to be dealt with. It was something like the quality of mercy, "It droppeth as the gentle rain from Heaven upon the place be- neath," and in a way they could not realise that it had come down.</p>
        <p>Equal to Half the Rates. It was very difficult to calculate the monetary loss caused by smoke, and, in his opinion, the actual money loss must be at least half the rates paid in the borough. They grumbled, and rightly, perhaps, if ?A. or 3d. went up on the rates, but they were careless about the money lost which arose in various ways from the effects of smoke; and because of this general indifference and neglect or the subject by the 1 authorities the factory chimneys had been left almost to themselves, and, to a large extent, the authorities had left the manufacturers to themselves. It was unfortunate that many upon their authorities were either manufactur- ers, or closely associated with manufacturers, that it was difficult for them to deal with the matter perfectly independently from friendship or fear. What ought to be done was not always done in the public interest. He would like to say in regard to the Warrington Town Council and the :Health Committee that he sincerely believed they were anxious to abate the nuisance and that they would welcome the co-operation and support of the public in the matter. (Ap- plause.)</p>
        <p>A BREEZY LECTURER. Experiments to Prove Facts. Professor Ltrivzs proved a breezy lecturer, and lie infused a good deal of humour into his lec- ture. He held the attention of the audience with a series of expressive and clever experi- ments and demonstrations familiar to the chemists' lecture theatre, but rarely seen on a public platform. The audience were most appreciative and felt the educational value of the lecture.</p>
        <p>_ The lecture described the atmosphere as the birthright of the people, and therefore no one had a right to pollute it any more than a per- son had a right to put poison in the water supply of a city. Some people looked upon a smoky town as a sign of prosperity, but they forgot the economic waste that smoke repre- sented. No one would dream of taking a hand- ful of dirt and throwing it into a delicate, well- adjusted machine, yet they were throwing many handfuls of smoke into that beautifully adjusted machine—the atmosphere. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Poisonous Gas _Emitted. 1 Experiments followed, showing the constitu- tion of the atmosphere and lithe gages which formed the component parts. Any carbonaceous matter, such as coal or carbon, united with the oxygen to produce a deadly go, called carbon dioxide. Some 31 million tons of oxygen were , removed daily from the atmosphere, and chiefly, through the burning of coal, converted into the deadly carbon dioxide, yet the quantity of oxygen did not ditainioh, because Nature re- o Th e 1- vtiviiatd the :,,!ro,orassphin:w by returning the oxygen o't Nature 'known an the diffusion of gases. When a great chimney belched out large quan- tities of carbon dioxide the law of diffusion POsed of the gas equally became a overme o top 1 ei er a wt i ov re l d.and dllfs it was winter in this country, and no vegetation to convert the carbon ' dioxide back to carbon, which the ' plants re- tained, and oxygen to revive the atmosphere, it was summer in other elimee, and there the sun and vegetation did the work of breaking up the carbon dioxide produced in this country.</p>
        <p>What a Bit of Parsley Did. Thus, said the Professor, there is a marvellous process at work to purify the atmosphere throughout the world. My assistant has pre- pared an experiment to illustrate it. We have in this tube a half-penny worth of parsley placed in water saturated with carbon dioxide. It has been allowed to stand in the sun for a fortnight, and in that time the carbon dioxide has been converted into oxygen and carbon by the parsley. To the top of the tube he applied a smoulder- ing taper, which burst into a bright name, showing that oxygen was present. Thus, added Professor Lewes, the parsley has been doing its duty to humanity. (Applause.) The atmosphere was the workshoP, where everything the people required woe built up, and if into that beautifully working maehine they threw filth it would hinder its work, and by such hindering they brought about disease, rin bLonfehitie yegtplizczaszetkes inikeseprtahtihnisfios and they hye and pneumonia. Fifty per neat. of the fogs were brought about by the smoky filth. It had long been a moot point as to which was the greatest sinner in the making of smoke— the manufacturer's shaft or the domestic chimney. The smoke reformers said it was the factory, the factory people retorted that it was the domestic chimney. It W 94 six of one and half a dozen of the other, with, perhaps, a little accent on the domestic chimney. (Laughter and applause.) In the South of England the house chimney gave 70 per cent, of the smoke: in the industrial parts of the North the factory chim- ney reversed the position.</p>
        <p>There was smoke, and smoke. Domestic smoke was given off at a lower level and 'travelled nearer the surface of the earth, and in that way caused more damage than the factory smoke, which, coming out at a greater height, formed an overhead cloud, that would float for, perhaps, CO miles. It was that cloud which reduced the power of the bun's rays to an enor- mous extent. ,</p>
        <p>An Exploded TheorY. There was' an old school of reformer who in- sisted that smoke was rather healthy , than otherwise. The idea, of Dr. Stenhouse and others, was that carbon absorbed foul gases and that sulphurous gases were healthy because</p>
        <p>(Continued in next cold/n*4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>STRIES AND POLITICS.</head>
        <p>STRIES AND POLITICS.</p>
        <p>"Labor °maid and Ir. Dukes' Latest Letter. "THE EXAMINER "AND WORKERS.</p>
        <p>TO THE EDITOR OR " TRH EXAMIRER." Sir,—l fail to see yet any progress that can be got by the method in which Mr. Dukes is treating the subject of our discussion.</p>
        <p>It might suit him very well to evade tho prickly points of my letters by calling me</p>
        <p>names, or saying there is "nought doing," but I think the people who are taking an interest in our discussion—and I am pleased to note the evidence that there is a good number interested</p>
        <p>—want something more substantial than this. " The Examiner" is read by thousands of working men, who do not get the chance of knowing what is going on upon their com- mittees, and I think its columns are a perfectly legitimate place for me to make my complaint, and Mr. Dukes to make an explanation. _ .</p>
        <p>Let us see what Mr. Dukes' latest letter teaches us. Your readers will remember that in my first letter I pointed out that Mr. Dukes, had thrown over strikes, and said that we needed political action now. Mr. Duke-s then vigorously denied anything of the kind. He asserted that he had never opposed political action, nor did not now be- lieve that strikes had failed. My next letter tried to remind Mr. Dukes of some of the dogmas that he used te expound. I ask your readers to study Mr. Dukes' latest letter, and find out for themselve, how many of my asser- tions he now denies. The most notable thing about it to me is the exclamation that he still believes tfie fight is in the workshop. It looks as though we shall soon see him rushing back to his old position—of those times when he nsed to try to impress everybody with the dogma that it was no use Rending„working men to Par.</p>
        <p>'lament; it was only too-ikvious-that throuila mixing up with middle and upper classee they</p>
        <p>lose touch with working-class aspirations a'nd get "atmosphered." I might explain that in everything that Mr. Dukes has advocated he has always pointed out to persons disagreeing that it was only too obvious at that particular time. I fully expect in his next letter to see him telling us again that it is ridiculous to advocate political action, for politics are only a reflectien of economic conditions, meaning by that the persons controlling the economic machine will also control the political machine. This is a Marxian dogma Mr. Dukes used to lay great stress upon. Or I suppose we shall see him emphatically pointing out the only too obvious item that "you can get the workers to fight industrially, but not politically." And I sup- pose we shall see him backing up this statement by saying that politics appeal to the ingrained prejudices of the working-class, but it is easy to see, when you ask_ them to fight for more</p>
        <p>wages, you bring the Liberal and Tory and all the bag of tricks into one unflinching line of fighters. You coalesce their interests. Or maybe we may hear again the shibboleth that "Do we expect the property classes to hand over their wealth to the workers by merely passing resolutions in the House of Commons?"' But whichever way the eat jumps Mr. Dukes' previous position was far more logical than the present. In order to outline an extension of his advocacy; in this case politics, he had to point out the weaknesses in strikes. And in order to advocate political action ha now says that he never opposed it. Of course, he did not actually say that strikes had failed; he only said you could not depend upon the workers to fight unitedly. And that, after all, the immediate strikers, the workers. were no better off. So therefore we must send working men to Parliament to stop the soldiers from shooting the workers down, and also sea that they are taught something about econ- omics. I suppose if Mr. Dukes ever gets to Parliament he will return to his old love, Das. Kapitol, and advise this trifling pamphlet as a preliminary discourse. I asked Mr. Dukes in my last letter whether he had never been approached with the prOposi-</p>
        <p>tion that he should -stand as Labour candidate. He does not 'attempt to deny that propos4tld3,. and I suggest. that his change is accounted for by that fact. WHEN THE PEOPLE UNDERSTAND. people understand what When the mass of the life should and could they will not need so unnatural, that poverty is When many philanthropist the people under- s. • that tseven-tenths of their labour stand that ugliness not needed, time is thrown away to others, and that life is not a pig philosophy, then we shall move I along- f,r as can re*, So the history of the world consists of the mass of the people being trampled on by men and women with ambition for posi- tion, money and lust. And I emphatically saY that I see no indication that the people are going to make matters any better. I know they are always prepared to use ignorant revolt, but I say now the name that I said in my pre- Yiolll3 discussion with ,Mr. Dukes, we want intelligent adaptation. In conausion, 1 take Mr. Dukes' hanil and say</p>
        <p>with him what he said at the Freedom Fellow- ship Rooms that progress is going to be slow- I do not say thie because of any ulterior motif, I serve. Mr. Dukes quite missed my joke lest week. If he wants to know in whose interests he should toe the tine, I say in the interests-of common-sense. LABOR OMNIA VINCIT.</p>
        <p>P. 13.—1 think 1 ebould be lacking in ordingai courtesy if I omitted to signify my notice of the letter signed "Friends of Labour." I think it a perfectly clear and common-sense letter. I do not agree with Re contents and may return to it later. L. 0. V.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>SCIENCE AND SMOKE—continued.</head>
