        <p>A Conductor on Tour. , Mr- 11. Stone, the well-known musical con- suctor from the Hippodrome, has accepted an engagement for a five weeks' tour as musical director of Jazon and Montgomery's No. 1 Corn- -1n"13, in " Dick Whittin4ton." He la. been kindly released from his Warrington engage- klent by Mr. Turpin, and on Saturday night will appear at lluddersfield. , In all probability he will return to Warrington again for February 23rd.</p>
        <p>Overcrowding in Schools. Mr. E. Plinston, at _a meeting_ of the. Educa-</p>
        <p>tion Committee on Monday, referred to over- crowding in the St. James', Sacred Heart, St. Barnabas' and St. Mary's Schools, and hoped the erection of new schools would be hurried ". They were likely to lose grants if not, as the Committee's stock of excuses to the Board Of Education had become exhausted. It was reported that a delay had occurred at the Oak- wood-avenue new school, but it was hoped the work would go on immediately after a confer- ence with the architect.</p>
        <p>The Firemen's Call. Liletitote7: evident on Saturday flight that War- aut firemen can respond to the call of for they were in full force at to Lion theßa.:'°r dinner. Amongst the toasts were inteid`° the Superintendent and Deputy-Super i - reume ett, and the latter, n response, Kaye a °f the year work. 'There were 30 fire.. An e... b...4.,...ce11ent programme was af temards given 1e f g ollowin artistee : Messrs. Vernon, IL .. u W. Dalton and J. Archer, all members of "rigade whilst help was giver by Messrs. J. ,se, a Carter and F. Wright. Mr. Wright and Mr. J. kiror _ _, blind pianist, acted as accompanists, ''iso contributed pianoforte solos. Bewsey Old Hall on Fire. th xt.'elriewhat of a sensation was created in e —'l"eY district on Sunday morning when it was ...r!Ported that Bewsey Old Hall was on fire rtowever, every fear of great damage waa atllaYed by the prompt manner in which the local brigade responded to the alarm, and in a ,shnrt time the fire was conquered. The cub- Ii supposed to have originated by en old sat beam which was built into the chimney "telling fire. The burning debris fell on a number of boxes and paper and ignited a parti- tinn, but the firemen quenched the flames by ilullering a number of slates and ponring a good taPnly of water into the danger zone. The sanlage was only small Pony Under a Tramcar. The unusual eight of a pony under a tramcar "As witnessed by a large crowd in Bridge-street on Tuesday afternoon. A horse and trap be- leilifing to Mr. 11. Toft, of Bridge-street, and 4riven by Mr. Toft, jun., were going down the atreet, when the driver endeavoured to pull up to avoid a horse and lurry. Instead of the animal stopping, it darted, towards the centre of the road, the shaft of the trap catching the front of a moving tramcar. The pony fell, and although its legs were not touched by the wheels, one of its hoofs was securely trapped by the spring of the car, and it was only with thAt aid of a jack that the car W LV3 slightly raised to allow the pony to free itself. The aniniai illfisred no severe injuries.</p>
        <p>Legh-street Band of• Hope. There was a crowded audience at Legh-street vening at the concert clieolroom on Thursday e , 0, ,r__. The , in connection with the Band so ndL 11" v -lklayor presided and offered '° "nragement to the Band 0 11 ope wwaorrdkserso,faennd..l the evils of the ' tjave some instances illustrating ' ink, which had come under his notice as chief wiles ouncillor J , niagistrate of the town. C the Band of ,Illaw also gave a convincing temperance ad- :Pa. Part-songs were given by by Ada -one t, i -Juvenile Choir, lia__ n OTI ,p. 71,, Blida Taylor, Master d Evelyn Nay- -1 rii.xiteu; duet by Ada Hamer an SsohneVlley and Mr. ILPVtations by Lily I'Ye' 4Cla W - •' • Hunt and Mr. Gilbert; Hilda Hewitt, May knd hn_ hitby, Lily R. ilen„. The aeaere,inerous songs by Mr. spruce Cunp,'''lnsts were Miss and Miss A. lir 10. moving a vote of thanks to the Mayor 'n as • hi o • 11. 7, Parkes described e poeas th Onique tell of Leg-street never in the his Clid of Rope Society had they been honoured Y the presence of a Mayor of the vown. 14°Iieeftlarl cains £5O DamagCS.f '&amp;11 inte • occupied ithe Ju resting case occup • ton in dge Stanger on at Warring * which p - - - Thursday shire county p„,, .C. Smith, of the Lance ia horse attention o `"-re Force, sued William Rogers° , ,„ dealer,, Ship-yard, Warrington, for conlP 'lon for personal injuries,, alleged to have been, _ • 12eivt'd in consequence of negligent driving nnlY the defendant and his servants, through them 210 t having a number of horses under control, on he • 41_ highway.----From the evidence it that on Sunday night, September gath, the defendant and his servants were bringing, °Y appeared lattd, from the Salford Docks, to Warrington, Iltree colts, which had been bought in Ireland. ,i',.C. Smith, who was Cycling in uniform, alleged that when the horses pass through Rixton they were not under control. The colts were pre- ceded by a governess car, in which was the eefendant. A motor cyclist, with a side-car • ttaehed to his machine, came from the direc- tionOf of Warrington, and \it was alleged that one the colts dashed into him and made the driver loose control' of bin maeliine. which I"lashed across the read and knocked down the ,oliceraan, who received severe injuries. These ~nluries would be the cause of a permanent dis- ,nguration. Iris eyesight was also somewhat I,l,oPaired. The injured constable did not blame IN) cyclist for the accident.—Ris Honour gave inidgment for X5O, th amount claimed. ' ''' _--- RITCHEN Rimers, Fran Cams, TlLES.—Large !neck, reasonable prices.—J. S. GARLAND, Iron- "'Longer, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Oddiellow Appointments. Mr. Henry Taylor, Bold-street, has been ap- pointed secretary of the "Constitution" Lodge and also secretary of the Children's Burial Society in connection with the Independent Order of Oddfellows (Warrington district), in succession to his father, the late Mr. Frederick Taylor, who held the offices for forty years.</p>
        <p>Will of Mr. Edwin Woods. Probate has been granted of the will of Mr. Edwin Woods (61), of Rock Villa, Latchford Without, formerly of Warrington, wire manu- facturer, founder, and managing director of the Longford Wireworks Company of Warrington, manufacturers of steel woven wire seats and mattresses, and this shows that he has left 410,508.</p>
        <p>Junior Liberals' Whist Drive. A most successful whist drive was held at the Junior Liberal Club on Tuesday, about 70 ladies and gentlemen being present. The prize-win- ners were: Ladies-1, Miss A. Walker; 2, Miss E. Booth; consolation, Miss L. Fleming: gentle- men-1, Mr. F. Hobin; 2, Mr. W. Sansom; con- solation, Mr. G. Toulmin. The catering was in the capable hands of the Ladies' Committee, under the able superintendence of Miss T. Booth and Miss Womenley. It is hoped to hold other such pleasant evenings during the season.</p>
        <p>~,,</p>
        <p>Spinsters Make Merry. Socials conducted by young ladies always prove attractive, and this may be applied to the merry" evening which was spent by the mem- bers of the Young Ladies' Class and their friends associated with the Working Mission, Bank Quay. The excellent tea served as a stimulus to the concert, competitions and games which followed. Miss C. Jones made a splendid "chairman," and those who took part in the programme were Misses B. Hough and 0. Dale, and Mr. T. Tanner, while Mr. I'. Atkinson was the accompanist. During an interval Mr. R. Tanner presented Mrs. llall, who has suc- cumbed to the wiles of matrimony, with a framed photograph of the class, as a token of the members' good wishes.</p>
        <p>Golborne-street Baptist Choir. The choir held their annual supper en Thurs- day, when about forty sat down to an excellent repast, which had been provided by the deacons and their wives. Reports, presented by the organist, Mr. Bickers, and the secretary, Mr. Sherlock, showed that the choir was doing a good work. During a break in the proceedings a presentation, consisting of a case of cutlery, was made to Mr. 11. Barnes (choirmaster), on the occasion of his marriage to Miss Jevons, also a member of the choir. Congratulatory speeches were made by the Bev. 11. Ogden, Mr. Few (senior deacon),- Mr. Davies, and Mrs. Savage, high terms of the worth of Mr., and liu dhis labours in connection with the Bwahionaes 11, s church. Mr. Barnes having responded, the remainder of the evening was spent in games, Sze.</p>
        <p>Warrington Photographic Society. Tha above society were on '1 uesday entertained with a most interesting account of the working arrangements of the great institution for those afflicted with insanity at Winwick. This lec- ture, illustrated by many views, was given by the Photographic Society's Prefddent, Mr. A. Davis, and included practically every phase of activity from dormitories to cellars, from the well-fitted dairies to the comfortable piggery. Mr. Davis dealt with the history of Winwick, the Hall, Rectory and Church before the Asylum was ever thought of, showing one good picture of the Old Hall, and many pretty ones of cot- tages in the district. Mr. Holloway moved, and Miss Jackson seconded, a vote of thanks to the President for his lecture.</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Church Tea Party. A tea party and concert were held at the Institute, Knutsford-road, on Thursday evening. There was a large gathering. The chairman was the Rev. W. T. Jaye, and he Was supported by Mr. Atherton and Mr. R. Lees. A hearty vote of thanks was proposed by Mr. Atherton, seconded by Mr. Lees, to the ladies who had presided at the tables, and to the artistes, for their kind help. The choir sang the glees "Silent night" and "Elderada." miss D. Brough was very much appreciated in her two songs, "A birthday" and "At my window," and was recalled. Miss Wardle was heard to ad- -1 vantage in "A little grey home in the West" and " Sunshine and rain," her encore song being "Hush'een" Mr. W. Hughes, who has a very sweet tenor voice, sang "Lead, kindly light," and "Nearer, my God, to Thee." Mr. McArthur gave a fine rendering of "Galloping Dick" and ''The Trumpeter," and, recalled, gave "Absent," and, by request, "Annie Laurie." Mr. A. Davis sustained the humorous songs very hap- pily, " Zummerzetzhire" and "Mountains of Mourne," for which he was heartily encored.l The trio "A little farm well tilled" was given by Messrs. T. and S. Hughes and E. Fludder, and Mr. G. Lloyd and ten members of the choir ended the programme with a humorous sketch, "A sprig of holly," which brought forth israousantrdsealyo.efnsiehglnauo::hter. The accompanists were Miss A. Bibby and Mr. J. W. Jowett. The School tea aandcappritiazie-gpirvotgamwmase heldwas arranged by Miss N. Gandy.</p>
        <p>Travellers Annual Meeting. The eighth ann .1 meeting of the Warrington Branch of the Cola was held at the Lion Travellers` Associa- tion Mr. H. L. Taylor, the roentirlifnotelg c honai Saturday. siding. The Honorary Secretary, Mr. pre- ryr Mr. T. Per- cival, submitted his annual report, p brief account of the work of th:rb;strc;ll dgauvreinga the past year. He referred to the number vice-presidents as evidence of the interestthat of was being taken in the branch. The Honorary Treasurer, Mr. W. P. Ashton, presented his re- port, and the balance sheet was considered very eatisfactory, as the branch have now a substan- tial sum in hand. He pointed out that during the year the sum of thirty guineas bad been raised in aid of the Pinner Schools, and that in presenting this amount to the institution, Mr. W. G. Hayes, Mr. IL Mercer and Mr. W. L. Walker had been appointed life governors, and they would have two votes each at every elec- tion of candidates in future. Mr. W. Peter Rylandrs was unanimously re-elected President. The Vice-presidents—Mr. H. R. Armitage, Mr. A. E. Boardman, Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Mr P. Caldwell, Mr. John Dolan, Mr. F. A. Frost, Mr. J. Fulton-Smith, Sir Gilbert Greenall, Mr. Henry Greenall, Mr. .T. Lee, Mr. James Locker, Mr. W. Milner, Mr. John A. Ransome, Mr. W. Sloane, Colonel J Reid Walker, and Mr. W. Webster—were re-elected. Mr. T. S. Steel was again elected honorary solicitor. Mr. P. O'Brien was elected chairman, and Mr. W. P. Ashton vice-chairman; as also were the honorary treasurer. Mr. T. Percival, honorary secretary, and Mr. W. L. Walker, honorary assistant sec- retary. The following, with the officers, form the executive for the year; Messrs. A. J. Allen, C. Hamblett, W. G. Hayes, A. 0. Hilditch, W. H. Owen, D. J. Price, T. Richardson, H. L. Taylor, A. H. Rooney, R. Lunt, li. Mercer, J. IT. Singleton, B. Smith, H. Walsh, and G. Vernon. Mr. Taylor, on completing his year of office, was presented with a chairman's badge, and elected honorary vice-president of the branch. Mr. W. G. Hayes proposed that a Provident Fund be established to provide a grant of .:ClO, to be paid on the death of a mem- ber to his widow or next of kin. This was seconded by Mr. A. E. French, and, after dis- cussion, was carried.</p>
        <p>OSRAM and TANTALUM ELECTRIC LAMPS at redncecl prices.—Agent, J: S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 24, 1914.</p>
        <p>An American Tea.</p>
        <p>The members of the Wycliffe Sewing Meeting held an American tea on Thursday afternoon, in the Memorial Buildings. A large number were present.</p>
        <p>Commander Evans to Visit AVarrington. Commander Evans, of the Scott Polar Expedi- tion, will visit Warrington to lecture in Parr Hall, Saturday, February 14th.,</p>
        <p>• . -</p>
