      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Pastor as "Speaker."</head>
        <p>Pastor as "Speaker."</p>
        <p>A Parliamentary programme occupied the meeting of the Wycliffe Y.P.S. held on Thursday night.. The Rev. T. T. James acted as Speaker. Mr. W. T. Eccles, Prime Minister, intrOduced a Liberal measure, which was unsuccessfully opposed by Mr. S. C. Owen, as Leader of the Opposition.</p>
        <p>Preachers for To-morrow. Bewsey-road Wesleyan—Morning, Rev. E. Morgan; evening, Rev. IL Middlewood. Golborne-street Baptist—Morning and even- ing, Rev. 11. Ogden. Legh-street Primitive Methodist—Morning and evening, Rev. A. J. Chessum. Cairo-street Chapel—Morning and evening, Dr. Mellor. . .</p>
        <p>Christadelphians—Evening, Mr. C. Morris, of Southport. Bold-street Wesleyan, Guild Festival—Morn- ing and evening, Rev. Joseph Manning, of Burnley. St. John's Presbyterian—Morning and even- ing,. Rev. W. Reid. . _</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational—Morning and even- ing, Rev. T. T. James. Latchford Baptist—Morning and evening, Rev. J. S. Chedburn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Guild of the Sacred Heart.</head>
        <p>Guild of the Sacred Heart.</p>
        <p>_ The Guild of the Sacred Heart (St. Mary's C.Y.M.S.) held their annual meeting at their rooms, 10, Dial-street, on Tuesday, the Chap- lain, Father B. :Rawson, presiding over a large attendance. Father Mawson congratulated the members on what had proved to be, in every way, a record year, and he also thanked the members of the Council for their services during their period of office. The society has now grown too large for the accommodation at its disposal, and new rooms are to be erected during the cur- rent year on a site immediately adjoining the present rooms. Father Wilson gave a detailed description of the scheme proposed. Bro. S. J. Price was re-elected president, and Bro. James Dolan was elected vice-president. Bro. John Dolan, who had been compelled to resign the position as vice-president, received a presenta- tion. The President thanked the Chaplain, on behalf of the members, for his kindness and help during the past year, and his remarks were heartily endorsed by the members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>District Bank.</head>
        <p>The District Bank.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the shareholders of the Man- chester and Liverpool District Banking Co., Ltd., held at the head office in Spring Gardens, Manchester, Sir Edward Donner, chairman of the bank, presiding, the directors reported that the gross profit for the half-year ended 31st December, after making provision for bad and doubtful debts; and setting aside an amount sufficient for depreciation of investments, was ..t308,788 15s. 3d.; to which had to be added the amount brought forward from 30th June, £47,331 ss. 6d.; total, ~t 356,120 Os. 9d. From this sum the following deductions were made: Total expenses at the head office, London office and branches, including salaries, stationery, income tax, &amp;c., ~t 120,754 17s. 10d.; leaving a net profit of £235,365 2s Ild. The directors recommended the declaration of a dividend of 21s. per share, free from income tax, £165,900; leaving a bal- ance of £69,465 2s. 11d. Of this it was pro- posed to carry to reserve fund, £10,000; to pen- sion fund, £10,000; and to next account, £49,465 2s. 11d.</p>
        <p>The report was adopted. The balance sheet of the bank will be found in our advertisement columns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Police Ball.</head>
        <p>The Police Ball.</p>
        <p>The annual ball of the Warrington Borough Police, in aid of the athletic funds, was held in the Parr Hall on Thursday evening. The hall was tastefully decorated for the occasion by Mr. Ralph Johnson. The arrangements for the ball were executed in a most efficient man- by Inspector llodgkiss and Sergt. Woodland, assisted by a committee. The Mayor and Mayoress (Councillor and Mrs. Peacock) were present, and Mr. Nicholls, the chief constable, who was accompanied by a goodly number of his valuable staff. The M.C.'s were P.C.'s Bennett, Hayes, Barr and Smyth, who are to be felicit- ated on their capable efforts in this direction. Their programme held 21 dances, and these ' gentlemen were untiring in their efforts to secure the happiness of the 230 guests. The music was supplied by Mr. Tom Booth: royd's band, and, as usual, they gave an excel- lent selection from the classical Hoffmann to the latest rag-time. If increase of patronage goes on so rapidly—this is only the second ball—one can readily surmise what an auspicious event the Police Ball will become year by year in the list of social events in Wiirrington. The cater- ing was carried out by the Winmarleigh Cafe in its usual first-class style.</p>
        <p>The Charity Ball. The fourth annual ball was held in the Farr Hall on Tuesday, under the auspices of the Catholic community, Warrington, in aid of charities, and it is very gratifying to learn that from a financial standpoint the ball has been a great success. The guests numbered about 190. The Mayor and Mayoress, Mr. and Mrs. Nield (the late Mayoress), Councillor and Mrs. Locker were among those who were present. The stewards were Messrs. E. A. Connor, T. Cunningham, 11. Dolan, M. Cunningham, W. Hardman, and T. Stirrup, and during the even- ing they carried out their duties in a very effec- tive manner.</p>
        <p>The room- was most tastefully decorated, and the designs were carried out in red and green. Plants and flowers were suitably introduced. 'A special floor was laid for dancing, and part of the main hall was apportioned as a drawing- room, and most comfortably fitted up with divans, cosy chairs and soft carpets. The sup- per room also was skilfully arranged, so that the whole presented a most pleasing picture. Mr. Johnson carried out the decorations in his usual first-class style. Mr. Tom Boothroyd's hand provided an excellent programme.</p>
        <p>Many exquisite dresses were to be seen, quite in the latest mode. Some were charming crea- tions a la tango, and others were in the brightest and daintiest of colours. But the most charming feature of the ball was the look of unassumed and genuine enjoyment which was on everyone's face; in fact, it has been one of the most successful balls of the season. The committee are to be congratulated, and they will no doubt look forward to the event being still more auspicious next year. The catering was carried out by the Winmarleigh Cafe.</p>
        <p>Working Men's Infirmary Committee: Mr. Crosfield's Offer. At a meeting of the Working Men's Infirmary Committee held on Monday evening in Toft's Assembly Room, Alderman Burton presiding, it was announced that Mr. A. H. Crosfield and Mrs. Crosfield had offered to arrange a concert in aid of the funds of the committee, and the offer was gratefully accepted. A decline in the receipts of the committee amounting to ..tlOO was reported, and it was explained that the deficit was caused by the failure of one or two firms to subscribe as they usually did. It was hoped that this cause would now be eliminated.</p>
        <p>The Secretary (Mr. J. IL S. llampson) re- ported that the proceeds of the annual concert amounted to £47 ts. 10d. to-date, but the returns were not yet complete.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wadsworth and Mr. Daintith reported their experiences as representatives on the Infir- mary Board of Management. They expressed satisfaction with the work of the institution and tho staff generally. The committee arranged te assist in the sale of tickets for the concert to be given by the teachers and children of People's College on the 29th inst., the proceeds of which are to be devoted to the Infirmary and Nursing Associa- tion. The Chairman congratulated one of their number, Mr. Joseph Bailey, upon his appoint- ment as a county magistrate, and Mr. Bailey, having received the good wishes of the meeting, generally, suitably responded. It was announced that the Warrington Butchers would play the St. Helens Butchers at football on the Football Ground on Thursday, February 12th, in aid of the funds of the com- mittee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>SL John's Church Bazaar.</head>
        <p>SL John's Church Bazaar.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening a well-attended concert and social was held in aid of the funds of the above, when the following contributed to a most enjoyable programme, viz.: Misses Brough, Black, Cotton, Pairclough, Johnson, Mounfield, Tanner, and Wrench, and Messrs. Ashcroft, Hannan, Nurse and Tanner; some pleasing juvenile items being rendered by the Misses D. and E. Hardcastle and Miss Reid.</p>
        <p>The Parish Church Tea. It was a jovial gathering at the Fairfield Schools on Thursday evening, on the occasion of the Parish Church annual tea party. Almost 180 sat down at the tables; and amongst these were the Rector and the Curates. The after- proceedings were of a versatile description, so Mr. Darby, the church organist, was responsible for tocomply he o with the inclinations of all ages. concert arrangements, and the M.C.'s for the dancing and whist were Messrs. Antwis and J. Knott respectively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>MAYOR'S PRISON VISIT.</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S PRISON VISIT.</p>
        <p>Watches the Released Nen and Women Leave Walton Gaol.</p>
        <p>UNIQUE MAYORAL EXPERIENCE.</p>
        <p>Some weeks ago the spectacle of a young girl, !gazing. through her tear-bedimmed, downcast eyes, from behind the polished brass bar sur- mounting the prisoners dock at the Warring- ton Police Court, called for the sympathy of the Mayor and his brother magistrates, and as a consequence she Was sent to a home in Liver- pool. This incident aroused the interest of the Mayor in the work of the Police Court Mission and with the idea of obtaining first-hand in- formation, he made arrangements for a visit to several homes in Liverpool. His enthusiasm was contagious, for the Mayoress caught it, and at 6.45 last Saturday morning, in company with Air, W. R. Laws, they left Warrington and motored to the prison gate at Walton, Liverpool, where they witnessed the discharge of pris- oners, and later visited the mission room, where the Church of England Temperance Society dis- tribute free breakfasts Here the party were met by the secretary, the Rev. P. W. Pheysey, and the Rev. Collin Dawson, vicar of St. James', Orrell, and the Mayor took the opportunity of putting many queries. He expressed himself delighted with all he saw and heard.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>MAYOR'S NEW RECORD.</head>
        <p>MAYOR'S NEW RECORD.</p>
        <p>- - _ The interest of Warringtonians is heightened by the fact that the visit of Councillor and Mrs. Peacock to the prison gate is the first visit of any mayor and mayoress, except that a Liver- pool Lord Mayor once paid such a visit. After departing from the breakfast room a visit as paid to the labour home for men at Bootlew.Everything was working in full swing, and it was delightful to think that the men who were committed to the missioner's care had such places to go to. There is a commodious dining-room and each man has his own little bedroom. The party then motored to the Hostel of Hope in 'Bedford-street—the home to which the girl who had incited the mayor's sympathy had been sent. She was not there at the time, how- ever, but a letter from the girl, addressed to the matron, was read, in which she spoke highly of the treatment given to her whilst she was under the roof of the home. The Church of England Temperance Society's home for inebriate women in Upper Parliament-street- was also inspected, and it was noon before the party returned to Warrington. As an outcome of the Mayor's interest in the r work a meeting in connection with the Mission has been provisionally fixed, to be held in the Parr Hall some time next month. Seen by "The Examiner" representative, Mr. Law ex- pressed the deep debt of gratitude he and his society felt to the Mayor and Mayoress for their practical sympathy with their ndeavours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>From Everywhere.</head>
        <p>From Everywhere.</p>
        <p>The average woman tells everything she knows, and 6, lot more besides.</p>
        <p>t There are times when, the to the Poet, a head of cabbage boiling in ule po has a sweeter odour than a bunch of 'violets.</p>
        <p>The Modern Way. Come to our suffragette house-wartning." "Whose house arc you going to burn?"</p>
        <p>love," says a keen the moonlight was like rays, said: The Logic of Love. . on the beach the other night. Moonlig "There's no logic In can make ,s4tmystically observer. "Two ht whitened young People the sand. The sea in bf reoasi LI et idru si i lbveenre. a t hT thheo s ey ocuanl ing "'How do you know I You happy?' "'Because,' groaned, ,the Young man, 'you make me so miserable.</p>
        <p>IRISH FAMINE POEM. Three Grains of Corn. [Written by Miss Edwards in 1847 with refer- ence to the Irish famine that year.] Give me three grains of corn, moth.._. Only three grains of corn; It will keep the little life I have Till the coming of the morn. I am dyipg of hunger and cold, mother And halfn the Dying of hunger y of such a death My lips have never told. i uangoenr and Cold;</p>
        <p>filit beside, It has gnawed like a wolf of my heart, mother— A wolf that is fierce for blood; AIGI ntahwe ilnigvefloorngladcakyofanfodotdhe ni I dreamed of bread in my sleep, moiler And the sight was heaven to see; ' I awoke with an eager, famishing lip. But you have no bread for me.</p>
        <p>How could I look to you, mother How could I look to you, For bread to give to your starving boy, When you were starving, too For I read the famine in your cheek, And in your eyes so wild, And I felt it in your bony hand, As you laid it on your child.</p>
        <p>iroolhreslaon n d an done,dsees What has poor Ireland done, mother-- us starve, Perishing one by one? Do the men of England care not, mother— ThWathathtehwasorpidoe The great men and the high, For the suffering sons of Erin's isle, Whether they live or die?</p>
        <p>There is many a brave heart here, mother— Dying of want and cold, While only across the channel, mother, Are many that roll in gold. There are rich and proud men there, mother, With wondrous wealth ih view, And the bread they fling to their dogs to-night Would give life to me and you.</p>
        <p>Come nearer to my side, mother— Come nearer to my tide, And hold me fandly, as you held My father when he died: °mirk, for I cannot tee you, mother, My breath is almost gone. Mother, dear mother ! are I die Give me three grains of ccrn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <p>The Members' Meeting Leaves the Question of Settlement</p>
        <p>in the Hands of the Committee.</p>
        <p>RESULT OF LAST NIGHT'S CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>are paid 275. M. and upwards. I do not know whether that is scandalous. (I Voice: "No," and applause.) The 275. 6d. for a win is the minimum. (A Voice: "Does every player get it?") No, it is the minimum, every player does not get the minimum. (Applause and a Voice: "Some get more.") Let me make the statement perfectly clear. In 1908-9 the players were paid 15s. and upwards for a win, and 10s. was the minimum. At pres- ent 275. 6d. is the minimum. (Loud applause.) There has been an increase of 12s. Gd. per match to the lowest paid member of' the team, and the others have profited accordingly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Committee Kept Agreements.</head>
        <p>Committee Kept Agreements.</p>
        <p>The committee have always kept to their agreements with the players. Even when the gate money has not been sufficient to pay their wages, or when the club was overdrawn at the bank, they have, on no single occasion, asked the players to accept less than the wages they signed on for.</p>
        <p>Last year the gate money at the Barrow match amounted to £4 19s. 4d. The home and away lexpenses came to £55 3s. Id.—(A Voice: " Oh !") —so that there was a loss of £5O 3s. 9d., but the players' wages were paid in full. (Loud up- ' plause.) The "gate" of- the Wakefield Trinity match came th £33 ss. 3d.; wages and expenses to £54 148. 11d., a loss of £2l 98. Bd. St. Helens match gate money was £26 ss. 2d.; wages and expenses, £5O 6s. 5d., loss £24 Is. 3d. So you see some provision must be made to get a reserve fund sufficient to meet such unprofitable results.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>£lO3 in a Week.</head>
        <p>£lO3 in a Week.</p>
        <p>In the holiday matches we paid a total in wages of £lO3 155., and with £l3 in Christmas boxes the total was .6116 158. The Secretary had written to 24 clubs asking if it was customary to pay broken time, bonus money, or give Christmas boxes, and 20 replied. Of those 20, Barrow, Batley, Broughton Ran- gers, Dewsbury, Keighley, Leeds, Runcorn, Swinton, Wakefield Trinity, Wigan and York said they paid no broken-time money, no bonuses, and no Christmas presents. The other clubs said "No" to the questions with the fol- lowing exceptions: Bradford paid bonuses on ' points at the end of the season; Iluddersfield and Hull gave each man ..£1 as a Christmas present; Hull Kingston Rovers gave an induce- ment for finishing among the first four in the League, and on one or two occasions gave Christ- mas presents; Hunslet occasionally gave bonuses for matches; Leigh paid different wages for win- ning and losing; Rochdale Hornets paid 3s. broken time if the men went away before twelve o'clock, and occasionally gave match bonuses; St. Helens gave 2s. ed. broken-time money and bonuses for finishing in the first four; .and Widnes paid 3s. broken-time money for York- shire matches, and occasionally bonuses, not ex- ceeding 55., for winning away matches. In Warrington they paid 6s. for broken time to players who worked; and no distinction was made between working players, whether trades- men or labourers. (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Stirring Challenge.</head>
        <p>A Stirring Challenge.</p>
        <p>At a later si:age Mr. Osborne objected to several interruptions, and, in reply to a remark from the audience, he said there had been too many rumours about the committee. It has been suggested that the committee are being paid and are making money out of this. If any man can prove that I, or any other mem- ber, has received a halfpenny from this club, I am prepared to give all I have to the Infirm- ary. (Loud applause.) A man can stand a lot, but there comes a point past which he cannot go, and it has come to that point with me. I want fairness or lam going to clear out altogether. (Loud applause.) Replying to the statement by Mr. Mason, that if not supported the committee had threatened to resign en bloc, Mr. Osborne said if he was asked to do certain things in which he did not believe, and which were contrary to his prin- ciples, he would never be a party to them. (Ap- plause.)</p>
        <p>THE SECRETARY'S STRONG DEFENCE. Mr. T. P. 13radshaw effectively replied to several attacks made upon him at the players' meetink. With regard to the Huddersfield Cup</p>
        <p>with the players in an amicable and sportsman- like manner."</p>
        <p>I think, he said, if we leave the committee with full power to deal with the players none of us will regret it. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Chalmers seconded. A Member: Is there no possibility of having a standard rats of pay for the teamf</p>
        <p>Mr. Bradshaw: It intention of the com- mittee to bring all the men to one level, but</p>
        <p>you must nnderstand the present oommittee cannot break agreements already entered upon.</p>
        <p>AN AMENDMENT MOVED.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Hands said the 'bonus question was at the bottom of the matter. The men seemed to think they had a precedent for expecting bonus. What the club wanted was the best possible football and to see the lads on th• field again. The members were there to settle the strike, and it could be settled before they left the hall. (Voices: "No," and applause.) Tie moved:</p>
        <p>"That the members of the team who played in the lluderstleld match receive per man, and in the future bonuses be not entertained in League matches." Several members seconded this.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <p>MR. T. P. BRADSHAW, HON. SECRETARY OF THE CLUB.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <p>on Thursday night. The sequel was that the meeting practically unanimously confirmed the committee's action throughout the controversy, and proposed that they should meet the players with the object of arriving at an amicable and sportsmanlike settlement. Such a conference took place last night, and the result is as given above.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Locker presided and on the platform were Alderman Tinnion, ex-president, and the following members of the committee: Councillor E. Osborne, chairman of the committee; Messrs, F. T. Barber, T. Bunch, T. Barnes, J. H. Whit- ham, W. Hill, J. Winstanley, J. T. Sampson, T. Pemberton, the hon. treasurer, and T. P. Bradshaw, the hon. secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>PRESIDENT WHO "REFEREED."</head>
        <p>PRESIDENT WHO "REFEREED."</p>
        <p>It was a happy augury of a peaceful meeting was the welcome cheer the committee got upon entering the ball. The President put the mem- bers in the beat of humour by declaring that he had come there to "referee" the gathering, and would use his whistle to regulate the game.</p>
        <p>PLAYERS' LACK OF SPORTING SPIRIT. It was Mr. W. Mason, one of the conveners of the members' meeting, who opened the ball. Ile quoted from a lengthy statement which, he said, he had prepared without any consultatiob with the players on strike, and lie also wished it understood that personally he made no attack on the committee.</p>
        <p>At considerable length Mr. Mason detailed what he considered to be the players' grievances, as he understood them to have been set forth by tho players at the meeting on Saturday. [A full report of this meeting will be found on page 10.] Ile thought, however, the players might have shown a better sporting spirit and played the Hull Kingston match under pro- test. _ . _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Policy of °the Loaded Pistol.</head>
        <p>Policy of °the Loaded Pistol.</p>
        <p>I know, he said, there must be discipline, but I appreciate there are two kinds of discipline we might mete out. First, the discipline of the loaded pistol Botha has been employing in South Africa, and seeondly, the discipline of reason- ing and compromise. I want to know whether the discipline of the loaded pistol or that of reason and compromise was used? I am not supporting the players in their demands. Mr. Mason added that footballers were not like workpeople, who could go anywhere to sell their labour; they were tied hand and foot to the club, lie had heard it rumoured that if a vote of confidence in the committee were not passed that night they intended to resign, so that there was a prospect of a strike of the com- mittee as ,well as that of the players. He con- cluded by putting a long list of questions to the committee, and these were answered by the secretary at a later stage.</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN'S IMPORTANT STATEMENT. Councillor Osborne made an important state- ment on behalf of the committee. It was not the intention of the committee to hide any- thing. They were satisfied that they had acted according to their principles, and what had been done by the committee was in the interests of the club and the players, a statement which could be substantiated. They were extremely sorry for the climax reached. With regard to the list of questions, they could give fair, frank and full answers to every one. (Applause.) The first requisition was not acted upon because it was contrary to the rules—the rules the members had framed for the committee to work upon. The committee were of the opinion that the bonus question was the real cause of the unrest amongst the players. It was the in- tention of the Northern Union Committee, at an early date, say the next annual meeting, to make such a provision in the rules that no bonus other than that provided for in the players' agreements would be allowed to be paid to any player in the Northern Union. The committee were perfectly unanimous in their opposition to the bonus system in ordinary matches. (Ap- plause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Minimum Wage of 275. Ed..</head>
        <p>Minimum Wage of 275. Ed..</p>
        <p>If the men were entitled to recognition at the end of the season, he thought that menibers ought to see that the players got it. The wages question had been referred to. The wages paid in the season 1908-9 were 15s. and upwards for a win. (A Voice: "Scandalous.") Just listen! At the present time the phvere</p>
        <p>finals Thomas, in an interview with a contem- porary, said be was prox i sed 45 f the War- rington team won the cup. That was true. Thomas, however, said he only received winning money of 108. That was not true. Each man i received winning money n addition to 10s. for the try scored. So in that Cup final Thomas received £2. with 10s. winning money, 6s. broken time, and £l, the gift of the President-43 16s. in all. (Hear, hear, and applause.) At the commencement of the present season Thomas met the committee and claimed an in- I crease in wages, and this was refused by the Chairman, not by Bradshaw, the secretary. (Laughter and applause.) He was told it could i not be done n fairness to the other players, and then Thomas said: "I'm out for myself; let the others look after themselves." Now he said he was out for the local lads. (A Voice: "Don't believe it," and laughter and applause.)</p>
        <p>" Everything Due to Him." George Thomas has had everything that is due to him. From the time he \ became connected with the club until the present he has received in hard cash nearly £9OO. lie has not much to complain about. (Voices: "No.") lie ought to be 'satisfied. (A Voice: "They all want more."),</p>
        <p>Mr, Bradshaw then answered the questions put by Mr. Mason. lie said the payment of practice money was stopped three years ago; broken-time money bad not been refused to anyone qualified to receive it; and a Christmas present of £1 a. man was conidered handsome by the committee, who gave no cause for a belief that it would be 10s. a match. When the players asked for a larger Chrhstmas present,</p>
        <p>F. A. NEUBERTa.m.</p>
        <p>Consulting Optician,</p>
        <p>The effect of Eyestrain on the Nervous System and consequently on the General Health, is serious.</p>
        <p>It necessitates the greatest care in the selection of Spectacles to give relief, and the Importance of Sight.testing cannot be over- estimated.</p>
        <p>Our Optical experience extends over 25 years and we have every confidence in recommending our system of sight-testing.</p>
        <p>72, Sankey Street</p>
        <p>(next to Viinmarleigh Cafe),</p>
        <p>WXFIFILINICTON.</p>
        <p>MIIPPE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <p>tue amount of which was to be dcdu,:ted front the end-of-year bonus, the Team ._.tannaitteo promised that the request should be laid before the full committee.</p>
        <p>The players had always been treated witli courtesy, and, having regard to the size of-the "gates," Warrington paid better wages than any other club in the Northern Union. Higher wages were paid by only four clubs. lii answer ' to another question, it was stated that it was impossible at present to place all the players on one level as to wages, but the committee ,had that end in view. It was also said that during the last two seasons there had been nothing to lead players to expect bonuses. Ano-ber qiies- tion was, How could the committea'not iiieet the players, because there was ne Aluorum, while there was ,a quorum to cancel,:thi3 King- ston Rovers match. Mr. Bradshaw said it 'was a Team Committee meeting only, and;they had power to cancel the match, but could not deal with' such a matter as the players then raised. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>NO ILL-FEELING. FOR PLAYERS.</head>
        <p>NO ILL-FEELING. FOR PLAYERS.</p>
        <p>_ Councillor Osborne stated that he attended the last 'meeting of the N.U. Committee and laid the facts before them. The League Com- mittee were anxious, as were the Club C 0122. mittee that the matter should be settled in as amicable a manner as possible, but the N.U. Committee at the same time recognised that such a breach of discipline wtis contrary to the interests of N.U. football, was not fair to, the members of clubs who paid money to see matches, and left the matter to be dealt with by the local committee. They, as a committee, were prepared to deal with the matter in as fair and straightforward a manner as was consistent with the responsibility attached to their office. We have not, he added, the slightest bit of ill-feeling towards any playing member. I say that honestly, but I do think, in. the interests of the game, a repetition of a matter like this is not in the true interests of sport, and some settlement should be made at once, consistent with a safeguard, so as to prevent any recur- rence of such an unfortunate position. (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>FIRST PROPOSITION.</head>
        <p>THE FIRST PROPOSITION.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Wright, to bring the matter to an issue, proposed: "That it be left to the committee to settle</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <p>~. LOCKER, "refereed" over the meeting distinction and success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>"VOTE OF NO CONFIDENCE'.</head>
        <p>"VOTE OF NO CONFIDENCE'.</p>
        <p>Councillor Osborne: This amount;to .a rote of no confidence in the committee. (Voices: "Withdraw amendment.") If the morer thinks it is not, I can say that it is.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hands: Do not put me in a false position. Councillor Osborne: I didn't.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hands: If this is a rota of no confidence, I propose a vote of confidence along with my amendment. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>THE CLIMAX OF THE SITUATION. Councillor Osborne said they had come to the climax of the situation. It was a vote of no confidence. The players were not asking for a bonus; but for an increase in the Christmas gift. What they wanted wa.s another sovereign on the top of that already given. Warringtou had already done better than most clubs in this respect. (Hear, hear.) The players had never been led to expect a bonus. Mr. Hands, on this explanation, withdrew the amendment.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN TINNION'S VIEWS. Alderman Tinnion was sincerely sorry about , the trouble. lle asked them not to quarrel over the matter, but to continue the confidence in the committee they had formerly expressed, and leave the matter of settlement in their hands entirely. They would endeavour to settle it in a fair and sportsmenlike spirit. (Applause.) The resolution proposed by Mr. S. Wright was carried almost unanimously, only some four or five hands being held up against it. A vote of thanks to the *President for "refereeing" the meeting so well was passed amidst cheers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
