      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>OVER A JAMAICAN CLIFF.</head>
        <p>OVER A JAMAICAN CLIFF.</p>
        <p>My adventures began very early, as I was not more thau seven years old when I and ray whole family fell over a steep precipice. My father returned to Jamaica from a visit to England, bringing back with him not onlv his family but a smart new carriage and a pair of valuable English carriage horses. Now those animals had of course. never seen a black man until they landed at Kingston and although an English coach- mail` had also been imported, all the stable- 'pork Was naturally done by; negroes, and the horses :never got reconciled to them. There were difficulties from the first and when we started for our summer home in the beautiful cool uplands of "St. Anna,' my tether dietet-mined to drive the new horses himself taking the English coachman on the box. The long journey had to be made by easy stages so the first night only saw us at a little half-way inn, and it was early in the morning of the second day when we started ti cross Monte Diavolo. All luggage. had been -sent on in carts and the party in the Britchzka (how old-fashioned it sounds!) consisted of my mother my father's youngest sister (a pretty 'lrish girl to whom every- thing was new and charming) an English nurse and my little sister and myself. It is carious how vividly I remember it all land rithen I came over the fame pass several years afterwards the whole road seemed as fanailliar as poeuitZe tp me. We travelled very slowly some three or four hours, as of course it was all collar work, and we hied nearly reached the top ft he mountain wiles a black out-rider on a mule came trotting back to warn us that we must draw aside into one of the refuges hollowed out in the mountain to allow vehicles, to pass clad* ether.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>NARROW ESCAPE.</head>
        <p>A NARROW ESCAPE.</p>
        <p>The English Coachman had developed symptoms of fever the night before and had been left behind to come on in -a buggy, so my father was obliged to take the head groom—an excellent black servant, on the !box with him. This man had been given strict orders not to ltsave the box on any ac- count but the moment the horses started a Little at the warning sound of the conch shell another negro out-rider began to blow vigor- ously he leaped off the box and rushed to their heads seizing their bits. Of course all that happened afterwards I could only know from what my father told us. It seems the horses at once began backing, and I can perfectly remember the feeling of the earth crumbling beneath us and the gradual slip- ping back of the heavy carriage. My father jumped off the box with the idea of propping up the hind wheels but we were too far gone for that and the last thing I remember Was hearing the horses scream and feeling my mother suddenly snatch me to her. MI was darkness for what seemed a long time until I felt a gentle shake and a whisper from someone who was lying across me, (are you hurt, dear?' Then I found I Was face down- wards on a flat slab of rock and my dear nothels arms were still round ail.. I as- sured her I was quite all right and soon wriggled myself free. How astonished I was to see that she and I were alone, half way down a mountain side amid a tangle of trop- ical gm/qierv. I don't remember Abeing in the. least frightened, only deeply interested and full of wonder as to what would next happen. I believe I owed my life to the thickness of my little straw cottage bonnet which, a moment before the accident had been securely tied on ray head, with a severe rep-oof on my naughtiness in taking it in- cessantly off and on.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>HELP ARRIVES.</head>
        <p>HELP ARRIVES.</p>
        <p>As soon as I struggled to my feet my mother bade me try and climb back to the road and tell my father that at all events she and I mere unhurt. This I did with some difficulty and it must have been a very rag- ged and dishevelled little girl who finally pulled herself up the last steep bank. I had the sense to keep as much as possible in the track of broken branches made by the fall- ing carriage and (tr.ll7Tting horses. and I be- gan by picking up the various little things which had tumbled out as we rolled over and ovee. But these treasures had soon to Be abandoned as I needed both hands to drag myself up out of .the thick tangle. In fact it was one of the wonders of the whole affair how I did it unaided. If .1 shut my eyes even now, I can see ,my father diatractealy !mailing up and down the read grasping his hair with both hinds. He 'could hardly be- lieve his senses when. I appeared unhurt be. fore him and announced that my mother 'was also "quite well." He then scrambled down to help her and I proceeded to strut up and down the road holding up a very smart para- sol of my young Aunt's which I had been strictly forbidden to toneh and which it affiarded me the nhist (lively satisfaction 'to wave encouragingly to the Nurse and my sister who were lodged in the . topmost branches of a tall 'tton tree! Several negroes who had by tiro- appeared on the scene at once be climbing to help them down which they .-ventually did, won- derfully little hurt but frightened into hys- terics.</p>
        <p>Presently my father re-appeared 'bringinT up my poor mother who was a good deal cut and bruised, and I can remember her lying on the carriage onorions which had been by that time refr:Pv-d, and tryinz to find plaister and sal-volatile in her little frothing medicine ch,st which must have fallen/ out direct/y, for it was quite unhurt. Then I .remember the soir!den appearance of several white men, mime on horse back and some in carts and a doctor kneeling in the dust by the side of my mother. These, I afterwards heard. W.RP. 4"},. officers and sol- diers of the detachmert of an English regi- ment quartered near. asd whom one of the nerrro ont-ride'a ..1v Far .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>SPECIAL OFFER ,I',(/)I2E 801,11ByT.</head>
        <p>SPECIAL OFFER ,I',(/)I2E 801,11ByT.</p>
        <p>P.O. sig Rocnres 10/6 Latoirar HAT (Trimmeil or Untrimmed 3/0. Poqt Free. Pleaae mentioi if re inired in Felt. Plnqh. or Velvet, aleo rolonr MARVELLOIJSI VALPE. ntr RANTF.P,D TO GIVE aATIfiPkOTION. c‘nnot repeat after our stork is dearer]. BURTON &amp; Co.. RO. Old Christchurch Road. Re. urnemouth (aP)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>TOWNSHIP TITTLE-TATTLE.</head>
        <p>TOWNSHIP TITTLE-TATTLE.</p>
        <p>The police are being congratulated In connection with the arrest over the "Looking for Miss Montgomery" case. The footballers were glum on Saturday. They came home with a defeat. The "Heathens" do not like such. Business has been brisk on the Ship Canal. The "Heathens" have been, in consequence, Tied up a good deal—usual grumble. The Garden Society meet on Monday. Mr. White, the secretary, has a report to Present-which ought to please. It is a society worthy of encouragement. The local Wesleyane are now rejoicing. Spring cleaning has now commenced, . And the chapel is to be re-decorated.</p>
        <p>Garden Society.—The annual meeting of the Stockton Heath Garden Society takes place in ~the Hall Cafe ,on Monday evening at 8 o'vlock. Sir Gilbert Greenall is expected to preside.</p>
        <p>Church Re-decoration.—The Wesleyan Church premises are now undergoing re-decoration, as a -result .of the -handsome sum raised in con- nection with the Sale of Work just before Christmas. The Schoolroom has been first taken in hand, and when completed the church will be dealt with in an artistic matmer.</p>
        <p>St. Thomas's Band of Hope.—There was a good attendance of members and friends at .St., Thomas's Band of Hope cm Thursday evening. The opening service was taken by the 'Vicar. Two excellent addresses on kindness and duty were given by Mr. C. Hallows and Mr. Stan- nerd. and a very interesting recitation was de- livered by Mr. Hough. Several musical items were very well rendered.</p>
        <p>I • / 'Women s Adult School.—The annual meeting and election of officers was held last week-end. Success had attended the 'work of this school ever since its formation, and a steady increase in membership and interest in the classes had been shown. The prospects were exceedingly bright. -The officers elected- were: President, Mrs. W. H. Sherburn; vice-presidents, Mrs. Holmes, Mrs. Lynch, and Mrs. Spencer; trea- surer, Mrs. J. Elwell; secretary, Miss Elliott • lecturette secretary, Miss Sherburn; registrar, Miss J. English; conductor of sewing class, Mrs F. Cox.</p>
        <p>Band f Hope Entertainment.—The scholars Hill Cliffe Sunday School acquitted most excellently in the Stockton tfhroefrom the of Ifeath Wesleyan Schoolroom on Monday. hen they gave an interesting entertainment, under the auspices of the local Band of Hope. The Rev. George Curtis presided. Master A. Mayhill gave a couple of violin solos, whilst a goodly array of scholars contributed songs, recitations, action songs, and drills. Mrs. J. T. Roughsedge deserves credit for the manner in which the children were trained. Further interest 'was added to the programme by the addition of a humorous sketch, "Uncle John from York- shire," in which Mrs. J. T. ROUghsedge. Miss Eley, and Messrs. G. Moss, J. T. Roughsedge and T. F. Moss took part.</p>
        <p>"Looking for Miss Montgomery."—At an Occasional Court of the Stockton Heath Ses- sions on Monday, Reginald Abey, 35, Patterson- street, Earlestown, was Charged with attempt- ing to enter the dwelling-house of Mr. George Smith, 50, Grappenhall-road, Stockton Heath. —The police evidence showed that Mrs. Smith had been out shopping on the afternoon of Friday. January 16, and when she returned, about 4.30 p.m., she found the accused in the backyard. He had a ladder reared against the wall, and evidently it was his intention to enter the house by the bedroom window. Mrs. Smith asked him what he was doing, and he replied that he was "looking for Misa Montgomery." The man went away, and Inspector Breese and Sergt. White made inquiries and obtained a description of him. On Saturday night Sergt. White was in Walton New-road when he saw a man answering the 'description leaving a house. He told the officer he belonged to Earles- town, and that he was "looking for Miss Mont- gomery." Prisoner, the police said, had made a statement ,in which he was alleged to have adMitted breaking into premises at Warring- ton, Earlestown and St. Helens.—He was re- manded,for a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Under the Microscope.—An interesting paper on the "Wonders of the Microscope" was de- livered by Mr. W. H. Atkinson, Warrington, to toe Penketh 'Wesleyan Bible Cies&amp; on Sunday. .1t the close a conversation took place, and this was opened by Mr. C. E. Parker. Mr. Atkinson displayed a large number of interesting slides under the microscope, and the meeting proved .one of great educational value.</p>
        <p>Billiard Match. Penketh Liberal Club played a Warrington Liberal Club team se- lected by Mr. Clay on Tuesday evening. Pen- keth won all the games. Scores; Penketh names first: C. D Wilkinson 100, J. Durkin 73; W. Lightfoot 100, G. W. Simpson 57; S. Tunstall 100, W. Patrick 97; F. Houghton ,100, H. Shaw 86; Fred Dale 100, W. Turton 87; J. Tunstall 100, W. Pickup 59; J. Bowyer 100, A. T. Clay 63; D. Matthews 100, J. Matthews 62; Penketh 800, Warrington 584. Penketh won by 216.</p>
        <p>Sunday Bird Catching.—At the Warrington County Sessions on Wednesday, James Ackers, 6, Cyril-street; John Green, 25, Cyril-street; and Fred Ellison, 26, Powys-street, were summoned for using nets and lime at Penketh for the pur- pose of taking wild birds on a Sunday, contrary to the County Council by-law.ASuperintendent Forster said that it was illegal to catch wild birds on Sunday, but not on any other day. The defendants were seen near Penketh Hall Farm on Sunday, January 11th, by two police officers. A net had been placed on the ground and a stick covered with lime was placed in the bushes.—Defendants pleaded ignorance of the by-law and were fined Is. and costs each.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Enterprise.—A special appeal for offerings to the Trust and Society funds of the Penketh Wesleyan Church was made on Sun- day. The services were full o,f inspiration, and the response was a most remarkable one, the offertory for the day amounting to over £7O. This amount clears off deficit balances and pro. vides for the overboiuling of the organ. The preacher for the day was the resident minister, the Rev. R. Afiddlewood, who spoke in the morning on sacrificial giving from 1 Chron. xxi. 24. The evening sermon was a plea for propor- tionate giving from the text "Now concerning the collection." At the close of the day the thanks of the stewards and trustees were ex- pressed to all who had willingly responded to the 'appeal, and by their generous offerings en- sured so gratifying a result. Special anthems seirses rernosdteorned, matisesacDhosdedrsviacned, solo being sboeni.ng</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>GREAT SAN KEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SAN KEY.</p>
        <p>St. Mary's Sunday Schools.—The annual tea took place the other evening in connection with the Great Sanket and Penketh Mission Sunday Schools at Vier Sankey Schoolroom. The younger scholars were entertained by lantern slides in charge of Mr. J. Woodward, whilst the elder ones spent the evening in games, singing and dancing.</p>
        <p>Drowned in Canal.--Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy coroner, held an inquest at Sankey on Wednes- day on Ernest Roughley, general labourer, of Park-street, Warrington, who was found drowned in the .Sankey and St. Helens Canal on Monday. Roughley was last seen alive•on December 15, when he left home to go for a walk. The district had been searched without result until a passing boat brought the body, much decomposed, to the surface. The father stated that Roughley met with an accident in a railway warehouse some time ago, and since then he had been peculiar in his manner. A verdict of "Found drowned" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>GRAPVEN HALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPVEN HALL.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. M. Oliver's Visit.—Liberals are eagerly looking forward to the visit of the prospective Liberal candidate, Mr. P. M. Oliver, as he always has something interesting to say. He will addyess a meeting in the,Met-hodist New Assembly Room, Knutsford-road, on Tuesday, the meeting starting at 7.30 p.m. The other speaker will be Mr. J. Bethell-Jones, of the Ninety Five Club, and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes will preside</p>
        <p>Independent Methodist Church.—ln connec- tion with the Temperance Society, the opening concert was given in the schoolroom on Friday by the Warrington Band of Hope Union Juvenile Choir. A very tiltereeting programme had been arranged by the conductor, Mr: A. Spruce, to whom great credit is due Councillor Henshall addressed the audience in respect to the aims and objects of the society.</p>
        <p>Liberal Meeting.—The annual general meeting of the Grappenhall branch of the Knutsford Division Liberal Asso6ation was held on Wed- nesday night at " Varunna," Victoria-road, by kind permission of Mr. F. Fox The following officers were elected: President, Mr. C. Jackson Holmes; chairman, Mr. J. Kenworthy ; trees- rer, cr.. JacksonFilurro wisiomts, ; lsec3retjaeriv,ort secretary. Mr. Fox Messrs. Fiaonxd, wWo.rJe J. eeet ed Walker.d A. e egßa taetls itboo n the, aunedn eJr. aTI . wCoun- cil. To the Central Executive Messrs. 9. Jack- son Holmes, J. Kenworthy, and F. Fox were elected. The following -were appointed as the committee: Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes, J. Ken- worthy, W. J. Walker, J. T. Rowland, F. Fox, F. L. Garside, F. Burrows, J. Moss, W. H. Brad- ford. N. Cook, A. Rathbone, J. T. Ward, F. Leach and W. Barton. It was decided that a social and tea should take place at an early date. Votes of thanks were accorded to Mr. J. Ken- Wde9rrtedhYasfsecor Fox also being thanked for the admirable services he had ren- :etrea:ryid.ing' Kr' F.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>SEWAGE SCHEME INQUIRY.</head>
        <p>SEWAGE SCHEME INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>Allegations That Proposed Site Is Not</p>
        <p>Suitable Through Flooding.</p>
        <p>A FARMER'S WATER-LOGGED CORN.</p>
        <p>Another step was taken in the Grappenhall sewerage scheme on Wednesday morning, when Mr. 11. Shelford Bidwell, Local Government Board inspector, attended at the Large Room, Grappenhall, to hold an,,inquiry into the appli- cation for a Provisional Order to empower the Runcorn Council to put info force the powers of the Land Clauses Act with respect to the pur- chase and taking of lands otherwise than by agreement, in the township of Thelwall, re- quired by them for the purpose of sewage dis- posal (including the provision of an approach road to the disposa4workB) for the townships of Grappenhall, Latchford Without and Thelwall. Mr. Miller was counsel for the Rural District Council, and Mr. H. Hatton appeared for Mr. Stanton. Mr. Fox attended as chairman of the Thelwall Parish Council. Mr. Henry Greenall watched the proceedings on behalf of the Rich- mond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd.. the War- rington 'Tube Co., Ltd., and the Warrington Estates Co. The representatives of the Man- chester Ship Canal. Co. were Mr. H. A. Reed (chief engineer), Mr. F. B. Greenwood (assist- ant engineer), and Mr. D. Dunlop (land agent).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL'S VALUE.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL'S VALUE.</p>
        <p>After giving the areas and populations of the three parishes, Mr. G. F. Ashton (clerk to the Rural District Council) said that there had only been a slight increase in buildings. The assess- able values for special expenses were: Grappen- hall, £11,151; Latchford Without, £5,402; and Thetwall, „r 3,900. The only outstanding loan was in the parish of Grappenhall of £572 oe. 3d. for private street works. There was also a con- templated loan of £1,620 for the same purpose in Grappenhall. The private street works loans were repayable by the owners. The rates for the current half-year were: Poor rate Is. Iod., and a special sanitary rate of Id. in the for Grap- penhall. The product of Id. rate was £46 9s. 3d. for Grappenhall, Latchford Without £22 10e. 2d., and Thelwall £l6 ss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>ONLY TWO OBJECTIONS.</head>
        <p>ONLY TWO OBJECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Miller stated that notices had been served on Admiral Jones Parry and Mr. Henry Stan- ton and the Postmaster-General. Notices of objection had been received from Mr. Stanton and Mr. H. Warburton, who was the occupier of a portion of the land. Notice of dissent had been received 'from the Thelwall Parish Conn- sewThereaglaensdchwemaseconnection with a Coun- cil. The other persons served had not answered. parishes named. requiredf rhe three in co The scheme and the suitability of the present site were considered at the inquiry in 1912, and the land was necessary for the scheme to be given effect. They were applying for compul- sory powers really owing to a difficulty that hail been experienced in getting proper access. The Inspector: You have no difficulty in ob- taining the land; the owner is ready and willing to sell?</p>
        <p>An Ambiguity. Mr. Miller: Yes. He was at one time, but I cannot say now. We weilt as far as making a provisional agreement for the purchase -of the site. That agreement contained such right of way as the owner possessed, and no doubt that clause contained the ambiguity which has after. wards led to the trouble. An effort to get Mr. Stanton to give that right has failed, and we made attempts to get access to the site in other directions, but find that is quite impossible. That made compulsory powers necessary. Mr. Diggle, jun., on behalf f the engineer, said the site was exactly the same which was contemplated at the previous inquiry. The o scheme was exactly the same, they had neither dropped nor postponed anything. They had considered three other sites for the outfall works, but the one chosen was the best. By Mr. Hatton: It was not merely 292 houses which made the scheme necessary. Mr. Hatton: Do you mean to say that there is no suitable site to be found ,withia the 1,610 acres at GrappenhallP</p>
        <p>The Beet Site.</p>
        <p>Mr. Diggle: I don't say that, but I say this is the best site. haring regard to the position of affairs generally. On one site the Warrington Garden Suburbs, Ltd., had said they would not agree to it. --</p>
        <p>-Mr. Greenall: No negotiations took place.. Mr. 'Tattoo: Is it not a fact that this land is subject to flooding?</p>
        <p>Mr. Diggle: Not to my knowledge. Mr. Hatton: Won't the total cost be consider- ably more than the estimate of 12 months ago? • Mr. Diggle : I don't see why it should, except the cost of legal proceedings, if they come. Mr. Hatton said they had been told there was no change between the position now and last year. There is a change, inasmuch as the County Council of Lancashire are seeking to take away a portion of Cheshire, at least one- third of the area of Thelwall.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Ashton said he was not going to antici- pate that Lancashire was going to be successful, but they would not touch the sewerage works. Mr. Diggle said that they had had no evidence in regard to flooding, and if the land were flooded, the tanks would not get flooded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>On Fldoded Land.</head>
        <p>On Fldoded Land.</p>
        <p>Mr. Hatton said the proposal involved bring- ing from Grappenhall into this already flooded area an outfall 18-in. pipe. After having made further statements as to flooding, he said that Mr. Diggle and the officials/ at Runcorn could not know as much about the flooding as the local witnesses. It would be a great blunder to take that land. A better site could be got in Grap- penhall, where the works were required. The Council had refused to entertain any scheme but that one. They could get a better outlet in Grappenhall by reason of the contour of the land. The best of outfalls could be got into the</p>
        <p>objection to purified effluent. Gra Ship Canal, and ,the company would have no wanted this scheme, for in Thelwall there had only been a growth of 36 inhabitants in ten ppenhall years. There had only been ten houses built in 20 years, and he did not think 20 had been built in 50 years. He hoped ,they would refuse to sanction the *theme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>CORN BURIED IN WATER.</head>
        <p>CORN BURIED IN WATER.</p>
        <p>Harry Warburton, Old Hall Farm, Thelwall, said the land was very wet, and he had potatoes and oats a yard deep in water very frequently. Sometimes they could sail a cock boat on it. It was often flooded. He had the land in water for a mile in length and half a mile wide. It was like a lake in the summer of 1912. Evidence as to flooding was also given by Robert Brookes, John Cook and Mr. Whitlow. Mr. Miller said the statements which Mr. Hatton had made with reference to the Council not looking for other sites iv Grappenhall were untrue. The engineer had been all over the dis- trict, and, selected the present site as the best. Where was there evidence of a better' site in Grappenhall? In regard to the statements as to flooding, he might say that any evidence was given and every effort made to hamper and re- strict a scheme of that sort. A repetition of the flooding would be prevented by the Council see- ing to the pipes, if there was any choking or backing up. The inquiry was closed, and the InOector visited the site mentioned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>THELWA.LL.</head>
        <p>THELWA.LL.</p>
        <p>The Vicar's Memory.—The services at the Thelwall Church on Sunday wore a distinct air of mourning, and funeral marches were Played both morning and evening. The hymnsin- cluded "Jesu, lover of my soul," whieh As:as sung to a special setting by Sir Frederick Bridge, and which was a great favourite with the late Rev. Ppm Williamson, the vicar. The Closing hymn of the day, "Now the labourers task is o'er," was touchingly rendered, signifi- cant of the reverence in which the late Mr. Williamson was held. No allusion was made bY the preacher, the Rev. Edward Gunner, to the work of the late Vicar, on account of t the express wish of the family.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>• •</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—The Temperance meeting of the Guild was held on Thursday. Mr. M. Llewellyn presided and commented upon the apathy of the Churches .towards the drink traffic—the dangers of which they did not seem to realise. Miss Mason and Mr. Angus were the soloists. Mr. J. Upson gave an interesting address, in which he emphasised the evil wrought by the consumption of medicated wines.</p>
        <p>Brooks, Lamb's-lane, Tale of -Three Fewls.—ThoMas •'McDonald the Warrington Petty Sessions on Wednesday to stealing three fowls, Patigate, pleaded guilty at value 7s. 6d,, belonging to was found by P.C. Kay early on Saturday morn- ing with the fowls in hi's possession.—Prosecu- David Jones; Bruche Death, Padgate.—Prisoner tor said he did not wish to press the case, as the prisoner was a relation, he having married wit- ness's niece.—The magistrates bound him over for six months.</p>
        <p>Bowlers Make Merry...--The annuarTennis and Bowling Club social wo held on Thursday at the Winmarleigh Cafe. as follows: Ladies: About 70 members re were present, and ented with zest into the whist drives, &amp;c., which contributed to a most successful entertainment. The prizes for whist were distributed by the President, Dr. Bennett, Gentlemen-1, Ikirs. Duckworth; 2, Mrs. Walker; consolation, Miss Houghton; special for lady playing as "gentleman," Mrs. Pendle- bury. li)r. Sutherland; 2, Mr. Kilshall; consolation, Mr. NiCholson. Solo whist: Ladies—I, Miss Timewell ;2, Miss Short; gentlemen—l, Mr. Page; 2, Mr. Osborne.</p>
        <p>Pretty Local took place at the Padgate Wesleyan Church on wedding..—A pretty wedding Monday between Miss Lydia Jowett, daughter of Mrs. Jowett, Marsh House-lane, and Corporal G. Dunn, of the Garrison Artillery, stationed at Dover. The bridegroom is the son of Mr. Dunn, of Woodbine-terrace, Latchford. The bride was given away by who also acted as the organist, whilst the her brother, Mr. J. W. Jowett, bridesmaids were the Misses Jackson and Evelyn Jewett. The Rev. E. Morgan oonducted the service. A reception was afterwards held at the home of the bride's parents, and the happy couple later departed for their honey- moon, which is to be spent in Southport. Mr. Massey was the best man. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>LAYNIMA</head>
        <p>LAYNIMA</p>
        <p>several Morris dances, songs, &amp;c. Mr. Charles James was equally successful either under the role of a violin soloist or singer; whilst Miss D. Scragg's pianoforte solo was also a clever performance. Miss E. Dalton's and Mr. Elliott's classes combined in a nigger minstrel entertainment, which was heartily appreciated; Annie Percival, Harold Owen and Sydney Daw- son being prominent members of the troupe. A musical sketch, "The Washing Day," by the Statham choir, was a further, variation in the programme. The scholars of Miss Rawlins' and Miss Callqeder's claws presented a series of meritorious tableaux desceptive of the days of the week. "A visit to old Betty" was the title of a dialogue given by Gladys Dixon and Lily Cash. A popular item was the sketch "The New Waiter," in which the characters were well sustained by the Misses H. Goffin, F. Cadman, M. Kirkpatriek, and Messrs. S. Hinds, T. Hewitt and P. Holt.</p>
        <p>The Rector, with suitable remarks, presented a large number of books to last year's successful scholars.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>LINES 'FROM LYMAL</head>
        <p>LINES 'FROM LYMAL</p>
        <p>• "Good Knight, Sir !" This-was said on Monday evening. The Knight is a local resident. A prime not a king knighted him. It was a bit of the Buffs' ritual. And thereafter they- made very merry. Just as did the knights of old. The May Queen festival whist and dance Has been the feature of the week in 'gram. It proved an enjoyable function.</p>
        <p>Justices on the Bench.—At the Lymm Petty Sessions on ,Wednesday the following were on tile ,Bench: Hon. J. E. Cross (in the chair), Major Groves and Messrs. T. J. Ridgway, A. W. Callender, S. W. (*Mild and S. Davies. The cases were of little interest.</p>
        <p>Musical Subcess.—The annual distribution of prizes and honours certificates of Trinity Col- lege, London, was held in the 'Concert Room of St. George's Hall, Liverpool, yesterday. Amongst the recipients was Miss Fanny Holt, daughter of Councillor J. H. Holt, of "Rildonan," Lymm, who received the honours certificate and also the silver medal for violin' playing. This medal is awarded to the candidate obtaining highest marks for the year in the 4"unior Divi- sion for violin playing. Miss Fanny Holt ob- tained 90 marks, so that both she and her teacher, Mrs. William Cooke, are to be con- gratulated,6o marks qualifying for a prize and 80 marks for honours. The Lady Mayoress pre-. sented the prizes.</p>
        <p>lkibt Wished Good-night.—At the Petty Ses- sions on Wednesday Alfred William Kent, a beat motorist, was summoned for failing to stop a motor car of which he was the drier, and further with failing to keep his rear light burn- ing so as to illuminate his number plate.—P.C. Atherton stated that at 11.50 p.m. on December 12 last he was on duty in New-road, Lymm, when defendant passed ,him in a motor car. lie noticed that the rear light was not burning. Later the defendant again passed the constable, but failed to stop when he stepped into the road, h\eld up one hand and blow his whistle. —Defendant stated that he thought the con- stable was only wishing him " Good-night"— lie was fined ss. for failing to stop when re- quested and 2s, 6d. for failing to have a lighted rear lamp.</p>
        <p>National Reservists Sup.—There was a good attendance of the members at the hotpot supper and smoking concert held at the Fleece Hotel on, Saturday by the Lymm Company of the National Reserve. Capt. Kershaw (0.0. Com- pany) presided and was supported by, Colour- Sergeant J. Wilcock, Sergeants Hughes, Bell, Thomason and Astel. Colr.-Sergt. J. Wilcock proposed the toast, "Capt. W. K. Kershaw" in well-chosen words, and it was accompanied with the singing. of "lie's a jolly good fellow." Capt. Kershaw thanked the N.C.O.'s and men for the kind manner in which the toast had been re- ceived, and gave some sound advice with refer- ence to the working of the company. He hoped the members would exert themselve,s more in the future than they had done in the past. They had some excellent N.C.O.'s and men to make a good company, and he hoped before long to see the Lymm Company the finest in the Che- shire National Reserve.</p>
        <p>atpet Provincial 00 nva i tntcoi theal knighthoodaboveG raGrand d lpordi gmoe of the hotefhl det hat to tit h,a enc Fleecehßro.e st ejr. R.A.0.8. " Lymm " Lodge.—There was a large Hotel on Mondly. The special occasion was the 1 emtPhlreueovsvince ("Sir " C. F. Wilcock, C.P.C.G.H. The installing officer was: Wilson), assisted by the members of the Knights Chapter of Manchester, and they performed the ceremony in a most creditable manner. The P.O.P. of Lancashire, Cheshire and 1.0. M., " Sir " W. 11. Bradbury, K.G.H., also took part in the ceremony. The ille bry- toast "Our newly-installed Knight" WKS ably proposed by Bro. " Sir " F. Collins, R.0,11,p.p.G.P. " Sir " James Wilcock are- sponded, and thanked the brethren for the high arnedndwer honour conferred upon him in most suitable terms. The usual toasts were duly honoured, and among those taking part were the following: Knights Sutton, Smalley, Miller, Davies, WoL fenden, and Cooper, and Primos Burns and Carr. A large number of Warrington brethren r a. r was n weresir ,i,n w . Sutton,attendance.B Tors Tmhoonmy ao ley, and ~ sir " T. Davies, and others.</p>
        <p>May Queen whist Drive.—The May Qwmuheoiensnt drive a. nd dance in the Conservative Club As- sembly Room. The Chairman, in a brief dCnoym m nightit t ee , whenwe were stronglyth ey g a gave en t evidence e secondll e on speech, alluded to a rumour which he under- stood was current in the village, to the effect that the committee had not issued any balance- sheet. This he stated, to be at complete vari- ance with facts, as a very exhaustive report of income and expenditure had been prepared and printed after each festival. Mrs. Gotobed Pre- sented the prizes to the following whist win- ners :—Ladies: 1, Mrs. Saville; °, Miss Boulton; 3. Miss Thomas. Gentlemen. 1, Mr. Nichol- son; 2, Mr. Buzzard; 3, Mr. W. Sansom. The catering, which was . done by Miss Hazlehurst toaiticill.the ladies of the committee, gave satisfac-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>PARISH CHURCH SCITOLARS"FEA.</head>
        <p>PARISH CHURCH SCITOLARS"FEA.</p>
        <p>A Pleasant Function.</p>
        <p>The annual tea was held at the Drill Hall on Monday in connection with the Pepper-street, Statham and Newfield Sunday Schools. The Rector and a capable band of teachers' and workers , were bent upon giving their guests a real good time, and right loyally they, succeeded. A concert was given by meniblOrs of the various schools. Gertie Ogden, Ida Kitcher, Gertie Goffin and Edith Higgins, four scholars of the Newfield School, each contributed a reci- tation, after which the Statham junior girls gave the neticin song. "How'd you like to be a baby girl?" The Misses 11. Goffin, F. Cadman and M. Kirkpatrick were well applauded for their humorous songs, as were the two last- named in their duet, "The spider and the fly." The contributions of the Misses Moleswarth's. Washington's and Hatton's classes consisted of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>COUNCIL EMPLOYES DINE.</head>
        <p>COUNCIL EMPLOYES DINE.</p>
        <p>Generous Councillors Give the Workers An Enjoyable Time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>YEAR'S WORK REVIEWED. %</head>
        <p>THE YEAR'S WORK REVIEWED. %</p>
        <p>• The members of the Lymm Urban District Council on Wednesday night continued their generous custom of entertaining, the Council employes by an annual dinner. The venue on this occasion was the Railway Hotel, Heatley, when the landlord, Mr. T. C. Heaven, provided the company with an excellent repasts the diners giving Unmistakeable evidence of their appre- ciation of his efforts on their behalf. The Chairman of the Council, Mr. S. Davies, presided, and was supported by Councillors Thurston, Groves, Smith, Warburton, Gotobed, Holt and Lockyer. Dinner, however, was but the prelude to an enjoyable evening. After the loyal toast had been honoured, the company were favoured by Mr. H. Thomason with the song "Thorn," the same vocalist being heard to advantage at a later period in such songs as "Simon the Cellarer" and "The Wolf." Coun- cillor Davies said that while regretting the absence of some of the councillors and workmen I through sickness, he felt pleasure at having Councillor Lockyer with them again. He offered a hearty welcothe to their new employes and hoped that all would do their best in the inter- ests of the Council and the district.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. B. Parry, the other principal enter- tainer, whose humorous solos were much appre- ciated, then sang "The Jolly Bargee," and his additional solos included "Grown-up Children's Games" and "The Little Tin Soldier."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>"AN EXCELLENT BODY."</head>
        <p>"AN EXCELLENT BODY."</p>
        <p>"The Council" was the toast proposed, bY Mr. S. W. Gould, who said that the subject O' his toast was an excellent body of gentlemen, representative of about every trade and P"fes" soee once s of a them member. gr. Gould said m sioft, and therefore their combined must be beneficial to the district. After giving Local Board, of some which hmhine was i senc that the present Council had transacted some very important business during the last few years, and they must be congratulated on their achievements. Ho hoped that the Waterworks would be a successful undertaking.</p>
        <p>htahnekedtoalast,r.Cso.usvnc.iGlloourldthewhlteom G. B. v. InREreWoIYA a. s a gentleman with a deep attach. he described D-FOLD. Thurs to public woik ki , for s Ind words. Speak- meat . is twelve years' experience as a conn- ing- of h cillor, be expressed his gratitude for what he had learnt, and said tkat gentlemen who under- take public work are, educationally,rewarded one ißntDgo nE tot D ONE THOUSAN thousand-fold. Ile acknowledged that the mem- bers did not grudge working, and what they did was carefully taken in hand. Songs were cOntributed by Messrs. W. Mort, A. B. Edwards and W. Shaw, to Councillor Smith's accompaniments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>WORKERS A HAPPY FAMILY.</head>
        <p>WORKERS A HAPPY FAMILY.</p>
        <p>Councillor -G-o-t-0b.e.d...p.i:0-pOsed..;;I:1;;" Work- atne;•ees if i:hgh sickness,rreogurettedthat therean d co were so h o many absentees new employes, making special reference, to Messrs. Barnes, Diamond and Simpson. The Gasworks' men Were also mentioned, as was Mr. D. Mort's department, whom he aptly described as " a happy family." He hoped that both old and new employes would Continue their con- nection and do their best for the Council, who would look after their interests. Messrs. W. Ratcliffe and C. I. Barnes suitably responded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>HEATLEY DEVELOPMENTS.</head>
        <p>THE HEATLEY DEVELOPMENTS.</p>
        <p>In proposing the toast "Our Guests," Coun- agethntele =s, who • • • - cillor J. 11. Holt commented upon the industrial developments which were taking place round Heatley, and said he was glad to see the realisa- tion of the things that had been hinted at a year he said never failed them, and that the Council were Gould was of always glad of his assistance; Mr. G. A. Cook was commended for his service in helping with the work in connection with the transference of the Waterworks; and he spoke highly of Mr. •Wi. Mort's supervision of the sewerage scheme. or In his reply, Mr. Gould stated the pleasure tr Mr. two W. it gave him to serve the district in a public capacity. • ,</p>
        <p>"THE OFFICERS." "The Officers" was the toast proposed by Councillor 0. G. Smith, who said that a great deal depended upon the stamp of officials they had, and in their case they had every reason to be pleased. Mr. W. Mallard was praised for the excellent manner in which he attended to his duties as Clerk to the Council, and other officers were favourably spoken of. The proposer also mentioned that., the gatheaing being held at Ifeatley was a token of the. recognition of the ward which their members were striving for.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>42 YEARS' CONNECTION. _ _</head>
        <p>A 42 YEARS' CONNECTION. _ _</p>
        <p>- Mr. Mallard thanked Mr. Smith for his appre- ciative words, and said that he first became asso- ciated with Lymm in the year 1872; since which time he had striven to do his duty. Mr. D. Mort's response was of a brief and pithy description. Councillor T. if. Warburton proposed The 'Artistes," and expressed the pleasure that their efforts had given. Messrs. Parry and Thomason replied. A vote of thanks was accorded the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>WIN WICK.</head>
        <p>WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>Whist Drive and Dance.—A whist drive and dance, in aid of the Winwick Church School Extension Fund, was held on Friday. There was a good attendance, and a very enjoyable evening was spent The winners were :-- Ladies: 1, Mrs. Smith; 2, Miss Winstanley. Gentlemen: 1, Mr. C. H. Allway; 2, Mr. W7M. Leather. Mr. B. Stone gave the prizes and Mn 'Davies presented them to the winners. Mr. F. Uncles was the M.C. for whist, and Mr. CI, Finch for the dance. Mr. B. Wilson was the accompanist.</p>
      </div>
