      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>EDDISBURY.</head>
        <p>EDDISBURY.</p>
        <p>NEW LIBERAL CANDIDATE. Dr. Charles Hayward, doctor and barris of Liverpool, has been adopted as the LibJTh , candidate for the Eddisbury Division of Cl shire at the next General Election. The Of was made by the Liberal Associati division at their annual meeting atu rdal in the Temperance Hall, Chester** r' 1-1-arrard fifteen lives in Liverpool. About months ago Ihe was adopted as Liberal candidate for Rad- norshire, but decided a little later not to pro- ceed with that candidature. He is 48 years old. ' and is the third on of Dr. John W. Hayward, [of Birkenhead. After his education at the Liverpool Institute he took up the study of medicine at the Edinburgh University, and after be had become qualified began practice la Liverpool, first as assistant to his father. He An honorary physician of the Liverpool Hah- nemann Hospital. Dr. Hayward was called to the Bar in 1898, but has not practised. Eddis- bury has been held by Conservatives ever since the creation of the division in 188.5, with the exception of the four years 1906-1910, when the lion. A. L. Stanley succeeded in winning it. Mr. Stanley contested the division at both the elections in 1910. but was defeated. Mr. Stan- ley, now 'Sir Arthur Stanley, has been appointed Governor of Victoria. At the first contest /0 1910 the Conservative majority was 688, but ill December of the same year it was reduce'd to 289.</p>
      </div>
