      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</p>
        <p>JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>'Unreasoning Prejudice. The paragraph 1 wrote last week pointing ont Us unreasoning prejudice there was against -fever hospitals and similar erections, conveyed w wrong impression in one respect. The build- 'int the Guardians propose to place near the Error Hospital is the epileptic block, not the Union -Offices. The site for the new offices is 'near the 'centre of the ton, and has already been decided upon.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gordon •Harecy. It must be welinigh a quarter of a century •ago since I made the acquaintance of Mr. A. G. C. liar 1. , Harvey, who was a prominent personage t the meeting held in the Co-operative Hall on 4..lmiclaY evening under the auspices of the Jhunior Liberal Assoiciation. I have often met Ll:a ill the meanwhile, and I have with good i:,sasou been accustomed to regard him as one of the 'float sincere and earnest of politicians. He has made considerable sacrifices for his Cori-. victions and the cause he has at heart. His rh People. at.Littleborough, where he is at the ,lead of a big manufacturing concern, hold him to highest esteem and affection. A bachelor with no strong family ties, Mi. 'Harvey has lievoted his life to the service of his neighbours. Largely through his personal efforts and in- Lli...snoe, the little town in which he has his home 7ts one of the finest and most thoroughly —'•`"PPed and up-to-date Council Schools in the Country; and he has in various waif; encouraged helped the people to make their surround- ings a, beautiful as circumstances permit. His 4ttitude in relatien to the black smoke nuisance ;vas Plainly indicated in a special message to .„i.lll° Examiner" a while ago, published along_ th other "Smoke Pointers!' And none.may etort Upon him with the ancient gibe, " Physi- lutri, heal thyself"; for, as possibly enough I „kalltvol romarked on a former occasion, factory b. e has been practically abolished at both of is 'tar iitvhParl°mills. Moreover, he is to introduce nl,,anientary Bill to compel others to take _:"° has shown to be so simple and effective dealing with the scourge of our indus- coramunities; or, to express it more pre- eisslY and perhaps more accurately, to constrain • Persistent offenders to fall into line with the eeortiscientious and the scrupulous and enlight- elbow, ! hod of their fellows. More power to his</p>
        <p>" Encouraging Eggs." I read with, interest last week's " hints to Amateurs to Entice Fowls to Lay." I•have several friends who make a hobby, of poultry keclnng. and at least three of these are fairly entitled to rank among the fortunate ones who ila:re salved the problem of winter egg produc- tion. I may first mention, without pretending seriously to commend, certain advice which I recently overheard a professed expert bestowing "Psa a youthful novice. ' After soleranly em,- Pn• hasisin the importance of keeping the hens' feet .iens I feet warm and of using "pot eggs" of abnormal size "as a pattern for 'em to go, by," the joker asked the beginner if he had taken the precau- tion to provide "spring nests." The youngster having admitted that he had not so much as heard of them, his instructor went on—" Oh, but you must have spring nests above all. And You don't even know what they are? Well, I'll tell You, because you can't afford to be without ern. There's a sort of false bottom arrange- ment, and it is delicately adjusted so that the egg as soon as laid is quietly carried :off and safely deposited in a receptacle underneath. The cf coursc, turns round to view her wprk and tinPge in the usual triumphant cackle, but, capteogtit:le nest as bare as Mother Hubbard's r too tr and concluding that she was a little smoih:llells. she forthwith proceeds to deposit in'ssent e! Now, you know, with eggs at their 'rite, that's a consideration." The ""ner A read Lis that Mr. Sam Brighouse has completed errin Years as distriet coroner, and, besides in g that his sentiments are in sympathy the.o/d song with the refrain "l'll live as •—las I eau, ali, sh I gather that it is his liatentien to " stick to his position as coroner" 4 long " Possible. Experience would have led Zine to ade "ssume much even in the absence of ha: Ire emation which he has himself made. I 'newri some half-dozen coroners in Lance- aad Yorkshire, and those who have passed waY all died in harness. It is the same with ~I_l4gistrates' clerks and County Court judges. I cpihrinnot recall a single one of these retiring into ae 'rate life. There's no too-old-at-forty non- titers about public officials. lam glad to think is every reason to suppose Mr. Brighouse _wll.be well able long to continue his Useful and e .111 lent service to S.-W. Lancashire. Thanks t° his strung convictions and the fearless- ' with which he habitually expresses them, `,..111. name is almost as widely familiar as that of .7tetr.Plowden, the London magistrate, who has hieved distinction by means of rather different characteristics.</p>
        <p>Cancer and Coal." To the suggestion of the paragraph under this headiug in last 'week's "Table Talk" colour is levib by the easily demonstrable fact that.chim- 116Y sweeps are highly susceptiblo to cancer, or wt any rate that a greater proportion of that tJass as compared with any other are attacked bY that dreadful and unhappily increasing malady. For this, then, as well as for many ther reasons, it is well that " Uncle Jack" did oot follow the original juvenile inclination, of which he told us in his Children's Corner" 'last Saturday.</p>
        <p>Dangerous Level Crossing. The Penketh Parish Council are urging the Lon don and North-Western Railway Company to erect a footbridge at Fiddter's Parry Station, - 'considering the crossing very dangerous. I should not for a moment.dream oflnding fault " that account, but has it occurred to lo cal now '‘ethorities that our public highways might, Ives •44„:"ars at almost any point be described as •-'.ereati level crossings? Many more lives rthe sacrificed on these than' at such place's ee-Z° ~,°oe so strongly deprecated at Fiddler's 4 eitation. At the latter and all similar in_ 44etiol'iig: it is somebody's business to . sible ef„Passengers and to keep away 11r-aiesP°onr- -1 but people are daily. killed. ve • on the highroads iby }noes le swift-moving ebien whose drivers too often appear to think .tho„ Y,. if not solely, about "getting there," e„. -ge coroners' juries generally exonerate- -14.„11 ille M all More reckless among them from say prejudiced . ""e• Somethat lam prejudic and itnfair b will ili ' lit InY view of the matter receives fl.lg; . ill-"cant confirmation in a totally unexpeelet! (re,artex•• On Sunday I glanced over the annuai 1,3„,,_9rt of the home and foreign missions of the tit7ed llethodist Church, and in a communica- nt° it J. General Superintendent of the writesrica district, .the Rev. . B. Griffiths, therein thus (inter alia): "It is true ak1.738t its climate is trying, but it is not so van- orie as that of England. We have our periods drought and scarcity, and you have your tts...asens oi rain i% lath destroy crops. •We have ,'-' yontend again st several fevers—bad enough 'al.loteed-----hut I doubt arliether they are so terrible I,_"urge 48 pllthisis in the homeland. We i."‘ve our beasts of prey but I feel that .'my life nreong them than among the motor mike in the streets of your great cities."</p>
        <p>" Cheek." orin Catherine Connor, convicted of begging at W • eon. assailed the Bench insolently with clrtongue; but I remember a woman in similar etimstances suddenly snatching off one of Ifer Caofts. and before the officer in charge could in- tjrfere to prevent it, throwing it violently at ,ne head of the presiding chairman. Such things have happened again and again in the _Cater counties. and must usually have been pre- "aditated—deliberately planned beforehand, ttldeed., •</p>
