      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>PETER RYLANDS : One of Warrington's Brilliant Sons.</head>
        <p>PETER RYLANDS : One of Warrington's Brilliant Sons.</p>
        <p>No one-who knew Peter Rylands in the days of his strength will quarrel with the heading we have placed at the head of this sketch. To few of Warrington's sons has been given an intellect so restless and penetrating, and few 'men in their brief day have left so distinct a mark upon\ the town. That the results' of his work are not more manifest is due not to any want of vision or of forceful endeavour,' but because a nature so impetuous in the very nature of things called from' conservative natures strongest resistance, and because, to the loss of the town, his powers were diverted to the wider field of national politics. _</p>
        <p>It is due to the memory of Peter lands that we should recognise his influence upon Warring- ton. " Rylands Brothers" and "Pearson and Knowles" are mimes that will be a sufficient memorial, and in commercial things he is not likely to be forgotten; but there is, perhaps, room for a little more appreciation than has yet been given to his influence upon municipal life.</p>
        <p>This brief sketch will not attempt to appraise him either as a man of business or a man of affairs, still leas to trace his sayings and doings after he became a star in the political- firma- ment. It will aim at a brief narration of events and opinions in the-life of one, who, in his day, won the admiration of multitudes and whose works live after him..</p>
        <p>THE RYLANDS FAMILY. Peter Rylands began his career at Bewsey House, January 18th, 1820. This building, which has for many years been used as the Liberal Club, was the home of Mr. John Rylancls, a Wrarrington manufacturer, descended</p>
        <p>sail-cloth business had languished and almost deserted Warrington. The wire business, which Mr. John Rylands had left almost entirely to the management of his partner, was found to be scarcely paying its way.</p>
        <p>-- - A i i 0 0 , --, , ~,'.* ~.•,,,,,,,:. , ' ' - i ..i - , , ' &gt;\'' • ';'• `l'''' , ~,,,,,•1 7.;:fAf.*,,,t ' ,',. ~:-„z,,'..-,,,,,,0t,41,..;,., , ~ -- .., ~..„, _</p>
        <p>Pnotograph by Birtles. THE LATE PETER RYLANDS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <p>through a long line of Lancashire yeomen. Upon his mother's side Peter Rylands could boast of honoured descent. The wife of John Rylands was the daughter of James Glazebrook, vicar of Belton, and vicar also (in a day of plural livings) of St. James', Latchford, a clergyman distinguished for evangelical zeal and untiring labours.</p>
        <p>Three sons of Mr. John Rylands began their career in the roomy house in Bewsey-street— John Rylands, jun., Peter Ryland,, and Thomas Glazebroek Rylands. To all these belonged fine qualities and intellectual gifts, and each seems to have revealed -quite early the tastes which were to remain through life. To Peter and Thomas Glazebrook seems to have belonged in quite a marked degree the instinct- for Natural History, and long expeditions in search of specimens filled their leisure hours. When quite a youth Peter wrote artieles. for "The Naturalist" and "The Magazine of Natural History," and discoursed to the Warrington Natural Blistery Society on "Bird Mimicries" and "Bird Structure." EARLY MENTAL LIFE.</p>
        <p>In these earns inquiring days he became a ' disciple of Gall and Spurgeim, the apostles a, Phrenology, and was soon a stout chiumpion of the much-ridiculed scieace. . There appears to have arisen in those days a Warrington Phrenological Society (under whose inspiration we have not discovered), and for this society he wrote in 1838 three papers upon aspects of the ecience. That such articles should flow from the pen of a youth of 18 is evidence of mental precocity, and the acceptance of his, Naltural History articles by magazines of repute shows that his ' efforts were appreciated outside the local sphere. To such pursuits he gave his best strength. Business had not yet claimed his powers, nor had he yet entered the sphere of party and munic.ipal warfare, which was soon to claim Yet there is evidence that science was not veyi age his fientwerietsitl aof khaios wyloeidi tghe, aafnditsinlasutarr- achievements it is natural that we should be arrested by one of the incidents of his early days.</p>
        <p>co n ELECTION.h sixteenth six In 1835, before iCrMo he Me had p tis year, a mock election "was carried out te b number of the youths of the town. Tory yanda Whig candidates were put in the field (Olive and liarson were the names) and speechifying and voting were indulged in with the gravity and enthusiasm of a great national campaign. .It was Peter's first contest. With the courage of a gladiator he took the arena and mide speeches. When the Progressive party held a dinner to celebrate their victory he was chain to preside, a fact which suggests that he was felt force in the party, and in the presence of ' 200 supporters Made a speech that revealed mature thinking and gave promise of real eloollOlCO by ;and bye. There seems, however, to have been some un- certainty about the result of this election, for while.the Whigs were holding a feast of celebra• tion in Bridge-street, the Tory. section were likewise celebratieg victory at the same hour in another place. Thum early' did he know the baptism of poli- tical excitement and begin to learn the art of gripping an audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>RUDE SdOCIC.</head>
        <p>A RUDE SdOCIC.</p>
        <p>In taking note of‘these early and interesting achievements it ought also to be recorded that there were few distractions to his mental life. Though be went daily to his fathcr'c office, he spent his time almost entirely in reading books ot natural history and writing letters and articles. Up to the age of 21 he had give* no thought to business, havitig lived in the cheerful assumption that his father was rich and that work was never likely to be one of his neces- sities. The elder Rylands, it should be explained, was engaged in two branches of trade. He carried on business as a sail•cloth.xnaker, and, in paltnership with Mr. Greening, had a mill for the nianufacture of iron-wire. A painful shock came to the three sons at the period when they were growing into young manhood. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>FIRM OF RYLANDS BROTHERS.</head>
        <p>THE FIRM OF RYLANDS BROTHERS.</p>
        <p>The shock, though painful, proved a stimulus to the three young men. They, studied the business with care, and having conultuted that reorganisation and new methods were needed, they bought out the two owners, and the firm of "Greening and .Rylands" became "Rylands Brothers." Some years of hard study and labour, not• unmixed with anxiety, lay before the young men, but their integrity and thg quality of their goods put them into repute, and their place in the world of wire was soon estab- lished.</p>
        <p>It was in 1843 that the firm of Rylands Brothers was established. Twenty years later the brothers were entering upon a new venture, which consisted in the establishing of a works in Warrington for the puddling of iron suitable for the wire mills. Ten years more and this works was joined with those of the Dallarn Forge Company, and with the collieries of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles at Wigan. Under the title of "The Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company," and managed by the directors of the former companies, the firm entered upon a career with which every War- ringtonian is familiar.</p>
        <p>Peter Ryland* was a young man of 23 when he flung hiinself into business. How much it has meant to the town that he apd his brothers developed the wire trade perhaps no one can estimate. Without wire and puddled iron Warrington might have been a cleaner, but it would certainly have been a different and less prosperous place.</p>
        <p>RELIGIOUS TIES AND OPINIONS.</p>
        <p>The Ryland, family were staunch Noncon- formists and their home was Stepney Chapel. This church, founded probably at the end of 1778 by some seceders from Cairo-street, was the earliest Independent Church in Warrington, and had its modest meeting-place in Flag-lane. John Rylands filled the office of deacon, and the register shows that his children were carried here for baptism. Peter was six months old when baptised, and the minister who performed the ceremony is revealed in the register to have been Job Wilson, of Northwich. He was re- ceived into membe'rship on December 3rd, 1841, and for many years served as the secretary of the church.</p>
        <p>The facultj for independent thinking, which was to mark him so conspicuously in the poli- tical sphere, showed itself in two little Volumes on religious subjects which he wrote during his years of 'service at Stepney Chapel. In 1845 he Published a pamphlet under the title "The Mission of thq Church; or Remarks on the Relative Importance of Home and Foreign Missionary Efforts in the Present State of the World."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>AN ATTACK UPON THE CHURCH IN GENERAL</head>
        <p>AN ATTACK UPON THE CHURCH IN GENERAL</p>
        <p>The argument runs counter to the general belief of the Church and is negatived by experi- ence, but the courage and force of the statements are admirable. Briefly summarised, the con- tention of the writer is that the Church has blundered in taking up Foreign Missions; that until the English are themselves a Christian nation they can never do much good among the heathen; that the Missionary opens the door to the rum seller, who does more harm than he does good, and leaves them in the last state worse than in the first; that to thoroughly Christianise England would be the quickest way to evangelise the earth, because of her out- standing position; and that the large sums of money spent in winning a few savages would win multitudes at home.</p>
        <p>A TILT AT THE PAID MINISTRY.. Much more remarkable as a piece of inde- pendent thinking, and certainly more cour- ageous, was a book of 118 pages published in 1847 and entitled "The Pulpit and the People; or, An Inquiry into' the Cause of the Present ;Failure of the Christian Agency." After rather gloomily asserting that "the Christian agency, viewed in relation to the spiritual necessities of mankind, is a lamentable failure," Mr. Ryland, points to the days when the Church made rapid and triumphant strides. Probing for a cause, he finds it in the fact that in Apostolic times, Christianity depended upon individual effort. It is pointed out that the Apostles differed only from ordinary men in their zeal and ability, that they formed no special class apart from the rest, and that in the Early Church all men and women were nainisters.</p>
        <p>The making of a distinction between clergy and laity, which is traced to the days of Tertullian, is regarded as having paralysed the Church by putting the multitude out of action, and leaving the work to the few. The serious aspect of this, as the author viewed it, is, that the preaching of the Gospel became a trade and not a labour of love, that the best preaching was then given not to those who needed it most, but to those who could pay best for it. The argument is summed up in terse and business-like terms; the Early Church, with no wealth or social position, made rapid strides forward, the modern Church, with an income of ten millions a year, is losing ground. , Those who want a stimulant might do much worse than read "The Pulpit and the People." A. M. (To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>INDOOR PLANTS.</head>
        <p>INDOOR PLANTS.</p>
        <p>not Tt hbee poloehafis re ed ot fo gt rhoew titudes who cultivate plants in windows, with gi en rgtt fle no w, ee re i n thereplants are oin ehe ' great success. Plants are like human beings, to keep in good health, light, air, and sunshine are essential for their well-being. They must be kept free from insects, and the foliage fre- quently efeansed to remove dint. The pots must be kept clean, otherwise the pores become clogged, and the growth of the plant necessarily suffers. The soil must he kept sweet, and care- fully .dieted as to water and stimulants. Plants do not do well in gas-lighted rooms, with a few exceptions. Ferns soon begin to turn brown, and flowering plants do not last long. The best plants for a gas-laden atmosphere are aspidis- tras and Kentia belmeoreana. Geraniums and fuchsias, if brought into the, rooms when com- ing into flower, give a bright appearance, but the plants are of no use after flowering is fin- ished.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>•IT MEANS OSTRACISM. ,</head>
        <p>•IT MEANS OSTRACISM. ,</p>
