      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>TABLE TALK.</head>
        <p>TABLE TALK.</p>
        <p>The World's Tribute of Interesting</p>
        <p>Items of News.</p>
        <p>QUAINT AND AMUSING.</p>
        <p>"The Germans don't know everything; they don't always know how to translate English and Scotch words," said Sir Frederick Bridge in an address at the London Institution on "The Beggar's Opera." The song for " Polly " was set to a delightful old Scotch tune, Gin thou were mine awn thing," and the translator not knowing the meaning of "gin," looked up the word in the dictionary and translated it " schnapps " I</p>
        <p>Reducing' Enormous Loss in Eggs. It has been found by foreign experimentalists that infertile eggs offer much greater resistance to deterioration than fertile eggs. To assure a high quality of product and prevent in a large measure the losses now experienced, it is urged that producers observe the following simple rules: Give the hens clean nests; gather eggs at least once daily; keep eggs in a cool, dry place; market eggs at least twice a week; kill or sell all mature male birds as scion as the hatching season closes. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>Effect of Alcohol on Children. . .</head>
        <p>Effect of Alcohol on Children. . .</p>
        <p>A commission appointed by the German Gov- ernment to compare the descendants of drunk- ards with those of moderate drinkers has lust made i:ss ar erpeosrutl. t olf t itiui e tch it en a tfeasmt ihl ai eta: orf oeratcyl threes per cent. of the children of drunkards die within a few months of their birth, against only tg per cent. of the children of moderate drinkers. Among the children of drunkards there are-10, per cent. idiots, 8 per cent. epileptics, 8 per cent. dwarfs, and in only 17 Per cent, of them does the ' intelligence develop normally.</p>
        <p>Electric Light Bulb as Barometer. To Use a discarded electric bulb as a fairly of pliers While holding the bulb under reliable barometer the point is broken off with a pair water. As the bulb is a.vacuum, it completely fills with water. If the bulb is now suspended the water will not run out of it in fair weather when the atmospheric pressuie, is nor- mal or high, but if the pressure falls, as happens when had weather is-approaching, the water will begin to bulge out of the small opening and sometimes a small drop may even fall off. When, endfrom, a wire or thread fastened at the socket with returning fair weather, the atmospheric pressi ire increases, the water can no longer bulge or drop out of the bulb. - , , , ---</p>
        <p>Why the Wise Rise "Early. -- It is a curious fact in psychology that nobody can stay at the same mental and physical level for 24 t ours together. In the morning you are more matter-of-fact, for instance, than later in the day. It is in the morning that the best brain Work is done, too—brain work of .the sort that requires industry and clear thinking. And it is about 11 in the moreing that our body reaches its highest point of energy. In other words, you are stronger, though almost imper- ceptibly, at 11 in the morning than. at 3 in the afternoon. You reach that highest point twice in the day, for about 5 in the afternoon the muscular energy has risen again. But from 5 onward it declines steadily all through the even- in.., and on till between 2 and 3 a.m.—"Science Siftings.".</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>Letter-Boxes and BM.</head>
        <p>Letter-Boxes and BM.</p>
        <p>A Munich paper declares that the degree of a nation's culture may be judged by the number of its letter-boxes and by its purchase of soap. Judged by this test, Germany has the largest number of letter-boxes, 153,187, as compared with 83,100- in France, and 69,332 in the United Kingdom, while Greece has only 913, and Turkey in all its possessions only 486. But the final test must be the number of letter-boxes that , a country has in proportion ta its People, and it is Denmark that can claim the first .place, with one letter-box for every 234 of its inhabitants. Switzerland comes second, Luxembourg third, Germany fourth, and FraTICO fifth. England is very low on the list. As for the second test, the amount of soap used, Great Britain is at the tar of the tree.</p>
