      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <p>THE BEST PROOF of genuine value is an increasing sale. Mothers, daughters and grand- daughters have used Steedman's Powders, which still stand as t,..1 unrivalled medicine for • children. \ Observe the EE in Steedman. These powders contain no poison.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>Literary Oleanings.</head>
        <p>Literary Oleanings.</p>
        <p>THE FIENDS OF NOTRE DAME.</p>
        <p>It may surprise a good many people to learn that, so far froin having overlooked Paris for centuries, tile gargoyles of Notre Dame have in reality oecnpied their present position for only some fifty or sixty years, and are, moreover, the work of an Englishman. This statement is based on a communication from Mr. Harry Hems, of Exeter, which appeared in "Notes and Queries" of May 7th, 1910 (11. S. i., 369), and from which the following is an extract: "Many people are under the impression that these date from median-al times, but, as a matter of fact, the creatures are little older than the venerable- looking tall, crock-eted pinnacles upon the ex- terior of Exeter Cathedral, the eldest of which is, as a matter of fact, fully a quarter of a cen7 tury younger than myself. 'lndeed, the Notre Dame gargoyles are not French workmanship. Most of these cleverly manipulated nonde- scripts, full of quaint conceit—some doing duty as water-spouts, but in many instances simply curious creations perched over the battlements, &amp;c.—are exact reproductions of the old decayed original ones, removed by Viollet-le-Duc, the eminent architect, when he restored a great portion of the fabric about the middle of the last century." An expert worker in stone, named Frampton, a native of Beverley, carved them under Viollet-le-Duc's direction. "Country Life."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>BOERS AND LABOUR.</head>
        <p>THE BOERS AND LABOUR.</p>
        <p>People do not shake off the habits and tradi- tions of a century in a ,decade, and force has been the tradition and practice of South Africa r for a couple of Inindred, years or so. The Boer always felt that he was living over a volcano. and got used to being ready for an explosion. The moment he sees trouble his hand goes to the rifle and his thought fly to the commando. So it comes that 60,000 men are under arms, and General Delarey has marched through Johannes- burg' at the head -of the burghers af Lichten- btirg. For the Boer has sCented trouble. Thel Labour leaders forget the natives and they for- get the Commando. That is not the only folly of which Labour has been guilty in South Africa. It has grasped at the shadow and lost the substance.; In no country in the world was Labour better Off than in South Africa until after the war. Among the British camp followers were some piofes- sional agitators from Australia, men who had learned the trade but could not make a living at it in their own colony. In the new territory they saw a virgin field, and hastened to exploit it. With a strong distaste for work they com- bined a fine command of language, which en- abled them to make others discdntented with it, too. Hitherto the results of their, efforts have been disastrous. The first real strike was called in 1907, just after the Transvaal got self- government. The Government sternly repressed any attempt at violence, the men surrendered and wages were reduced : It may be observed that, so long as the men did not strike, the mine owners never raised the question of wages.— Globe."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>PARIS STREET TRAFFIC.</head>
        <p>PARIS STREET TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>The Paris street traffic usually terrifies the Londoner on first acquaintance. I. don't know whether the actual number of motor vehicles in Paris is greater than that of London, but the 'state of the streets gives' that impression arid the reckless manner in which they are allowed to be driven is a public scandal. Drivers pergist- ently ignore the fact that there is a right and a wrong side of the road for them, and the agents who direct the traffic are unable to enforce their authority. The curious fact is that with the advanced state of motor traction in the Gay City the old-fashioned fiacre should l)e toler- ated.</p>
        <p>Whereas in London the hansom cab has almost disappeared, the horse-drawn cabs of Paris are still plentiful. From the latest sta- tistics, however, it would seem that even in Paris the taxicab is fast gaining a monopoly. Ten years ago there were ten thousand horse cabs plying for hire. To-day there are less than three thousand.</p>
        <p>The beginning of the hired horse cab takes us back to 1643-, during the reign of Louis XIV. An engineer named Sauvage conceived the idea of letting out vehicles to seat five or six at so much an hour, and established himself in the Rue Saint Martin, at the Hotel Saint Piacre, from which the cabs took their names. The scheme was highly successful, and a few years later one of the King's riding masters obtained the assent of the' authorities to establish cab stances in the public squares and other suit- able places, this being the real beginning of the Paris " voiture de place."—"Erening Stan. dard."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>IN ABRAHAM'S COUNTRY.</head>
        <p>IN ABRAHAM'S COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>The journey from Beirut to Aleppo, scarcely longer in point of mileage than from London to Manchester, took us nineteen hours. A good- natured Turkish officer shared the compartment, and helped to enliven the way, as well as to keep undesirables from travelling with us. Be- yond Hama, on the river Orontes, we could see several of the high water wheels which are used to raise water from the river to the high ground on either side. Tbese naura, as they are called, are of huge dimensions, some of them nearly seventy feet in diameter. As the wheel passes through de water, small boxes are filled with the fluid, and, when they reach the top they empty their contents into a trough, from which the water is directed into the necessary chan- nels. Night and day these wheels turn, and are only to be stopped by diverting the force of the water below into another course. Altogether, it is a curious example of Syrian engineering, and the creaking of the wheels, together with the blowing of the wind through the spokes, makes queer music, which rings in the ears for long after the train has left this wonderful river behind. According to Egyptian monumebts the town of Aleppo was in existence in the time of Abra- ham. In antiquity, therefore, it must almost rival Damascus, and it is all the more strange that it appears to have no connection whatever with Old Testament history. From the middle of the town rises the ruin of an immense castle ' which serves as barracks for the Turkish troops. This castle has only one entrance, spanning the moat eurronriding the citadel, and neither. visit- ors nor residents are allowed inside the keep. Beyond the castle and the native bazaars, which are extensive and fairly clean, there is little to claim attention in this Syrian town. It was, however, touched by the hot wind of industrial dispute while we were there, and it was curious to find in the impassive East so modern and Western a thing , as a strike in progress—a transport strike, too, which delayed our journey. We were taken from Aleppo almost to the bank of the Euphrates by the railway which the Germans are now building right across Asiatic Turkey as far as Bagdad, the city which scintillates in the " Arabian Nights." Surely there is nothing so romantic in the whole his- tory.of railway enterprise as the iron rail which will link up Constantinople and the ruins of Babylon, which will touch two of the four rivers of Eden, the Euphrates and the Tigris, and which will enable one to go from the Tarsus of the apostle to the Padan-Aram of the patriarch. Already a trestle bridge allows the train to pass over that river upon which the sixth angel of the Apocalypse poured out his vial. The track is laid also for many miles on the eastern side towards Mesopotamia, but it is not yet open for ' public traffic.—From "At the Cradle of tbi Piariaichs" in the "Sunday at Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>:I'V'o4l-'gs()=%=ALs() i‘;</head>
        <p>:I'V'o4l-'gs()=%=ALs() i‘;</p>
        <p>5 CHILDREN'S CORNER. Conducted by UNCLE JACK. a2ww)Aosemcivo„,,,o"vio,eo</p>
        <p>"I DO ENJOY READING IT."</p>
        <p>My Dear Children,— It is . only a small, plain postcard that has reached me, yet it made my old heart throb with delight. The postcard contained a few words' of message, which expresses more than a long letter could, the fact that you do appreciate the "Children's Corner," hence the cause of my joy. The letter is from a new member, and is as follows :</p>
        <p>125, Wellfield-stret, Warrington, January 21st, 1914.</p>
        <p>_ Dear Uncle Jack,—l should like very much to become one of., your nieces in the "Children's Corner." Ido enjoy reading it, and I have seen some •of my friends' names in. I am ten years old.—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>JEssxE H. birlß.</p>
        <p>A nicely put, well-written letter, Jessie. and I ,am pleased to enrol you as a member. ' I have also received another interesting letter, which is as follows :</p>
        <p>15, Hoyle-street, Warrington, January 18th, 1914.* Dear Uncle Jack,—l vish to be enrolled as , one of your members of the "Children' Corner, so 'would you Mind putting my name in your big book? and by doing this I shall be greatly obliged.—Yours faithfully, J. STEPHENS. P.S.—I am`very anxious to be in you, competition entitled " What I would like tc be when I an grown up, and why."</p>
        <p>I have great pleasure in also enrolling this new member, ad hope he will enter our com- petition. And now a word about the competi- tion. ,The subject is "What you would like to be when you are grown up, and why?" Just tell me in your words shat you would like to he, and give the reasons for your choice. The competition does not close until 7th February. so you have still ample time.</p>
        <p>Youri loving UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>PEARLY GREY'. I've the sweetest little kitty, So fluffy and so pretty, And my little kitty's name in Pearl,y-Grey. ,She's the dearest little mite, And her name just suits her quite, Though we only call her Pearly every day.</p>
        <p>Her eyes are. round and blue— I love blue eyes, don't you? And her coat is—well, it's simply pearly-grey. She is very dlund and fat, Such a darling baby cat, • But I think she looks her sweetest when at play</p>
        <p>She will Scamper her and there, • And then roll beneath a chair, Till I can put "Pearly, Pearly, Pearly-Grey:" When she'll ;give a sudden leap And arrive all in a heap In the middle of my lap, dear Pearly-Grey</p>
        <p>She is never still at all, Except when, like a ball, She lies curled up fast asleep, my Pearly-Grey. Pr9m morning until night She's my plaything and delight, Oh! there never was a cat like Pearly-Grey! —Amy G. EDDISON. in "Books for the Bairns."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>HISTORY OF THE POST OFFICE.</head>
        <p>HISTORY OF THE POST OFFICE.</p>
        <p>_ In the twentieth century, when we can send a letter half round the world for a penny, we are apt to forget that in the memory of many who are still alive letters were dispatched and re- ceived under very different circumstances. The word "post" comes from ,the Latin "posits," meaning fixed, and is derived from the posts or fixed stages which were established all over the huge Roman Empire, .where mounted men were kept in readiness to bear private messages or public news from one to the other, much in the same way as, in the last century, Buffalo Bill and his comrades carried the pony post from one side of the American continent to the other.</p>
        <p>llerodotus (born at Halicarnassus, in Caria, 481 ac., died at Thurii, in Italy, 424 8.C., an eminent Greek historian, usually called "the Father of History") tells us that a similar sys- tem of couriers existed in ancient Persia; and Xenophon (born at Athens 444 8.C., died at Corinth 359 8.C., a celebrated Athenian general, philosopher and historian, the disciple and friend of Socrates), writing 400 8.C., describes the postal system established by King Cyrus.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>In China and Mexico.</head>
        <p>In China and Mexico.</p>
        <p>In China, in the thirteenth century, there was a very complete postal system in working order, Marco Polo, the Italian traveller, describing it in full, and stating that the stations were only three miles apart. The Aztecs, the vanished race which once ruled Mexico, had also a postal service carried on by foot messengers, with stations about six miles apart, but in this case the letters dirtied seem to have been those of the Government only.</p>
        <p>In England, in the early times, letters, whe- ther public or private, were only sent by special messengers, though later on carriers with strings of pack-horses, used to traverse the country, and they would carry letters for a consideration. In the reign of Edward I. posts were estab- lished all over the country, at which horses could be hired by those wearing the Royal livery, and in the following reign private indi- viduals were also allowed to hire fost-horses. In 1548 a law was passed enacting that the charge-for the horses should not exceed a penny a mile—a penny, of course, representing a far larger sum in those days than it does now—and in 1581 there was an official called the "Master of the Postes," who might claim to be the first Postmaster-General, though probably his duty was only to attend to the supply of horses. The first regular post was, established by Charles 1., and a few years later the rates of postage for a single letter were: For any disc tance not exceeding 80 miles, 2d.; 140 miles, 4d.; any greater distance in England, 6d.; and from London to Scotland, Bd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>Great Postal Reformer.</head>
        <p>Great Postal Reformer.</p>
        <p>Many improvements occurred from time to time, but the most important step taken was the institution of mail coaches by John Palmer (now known as the great postal reformer), the son of a bresier, who was born in Bath, and in 1770 became the proprietor of the Bath Theatre, soon after purchasing the theatre at Bristol. During his journeys from one theatre to an- other, he noticed the slowness of the, State Post, for the mail took three days to go from London to Bath. lie therefore laid before the Govern- ment a scheme for the establishment of regular coaches to carry the mails, and was so successful that he was appointed Controller of the General Post Office at a salary of .R. 1.500 a year. The mail coaches began to run in 1784, in the face of great opposition, and eight years later, in con- sequence of his disagreements with other officials who wanted to go hack to the old haphazard way, Palmer was obliged to retire. During the mail coach era the roads were im- proved, and the speed of the mails was ini•reased from about six to a little over ten miles an hour. Mails were first carried by railway in 1830, over the line which ran from Manchester to Liver- pool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>CHILD'S LOVE STORY.</head>
        <p>A CHILD'S LOVE STORY.</p>
        <p>"Its perfectly ridiailons to suppose," I ertid, "that any grown-up, serious, sober. man could fall in love with a child of fifteen or sixteen, as you say, and marry her, and be anything but horribly bored with her if be did."</p>
        <p>The man sitting opposite to me' laughed. "I could tell you a story," he said, "to ,how you the truth of what I-say. It will have the. merit of being true." The man who spoke to me was a well- known and very popular Austrian diplomat- ist, whom kind fate had thrown in my way at an inexpressibly dreary entertainment to. which I had been dragged, in Rome. "Tell me the story by all means," I prayed him "It might relieve the desola- tion of this scene of gaiety." . "You have heard presumably of Prince Heinrich of Langen"-Schwalbach, the 'eldest' son and heir of the reigning Grand Duke of langen-Schwalbach ? The Grand Duke, yolP- know, is not a very popular person, nor, to say truth, very respectable one. He comes of a good old family, but he has come a long v.ay from it, and he drinks beer to a degree which even a German constitution can hardly. stand much longer. "Yet fer soine obscure reason which I never could understand, His Highness isn't popular, and most of the Grand Duchy, as well as some very great personages in the German Empire, incited .Prince Heinrich to take as much as possible of the government into his hands. Especially they were anxious that he should marry, and set up a small court which should be a somewhat more re- spectable social centre than his father's.</p>
        <p>-"But Prince Heinrich had reached his thirty-fifth birthday and would marry no- body. It was useless to talk to him about his duty. He was sent off to one court after another where moderately pretty girls or clever girls were reported to be available. and he always came back with the same story, that as a mere matter of novelty and amusement, and even of gain, he would pre- fer the State Council to arrange a match be- tween him and a Princess of German East , Africa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>A. SOLITARY WALK.</head>
        <p>A. SOLITARY WALK.</p>
        <p>"One day when the Imperial authorities were beginning to get seriously annoyed. and sending some very strong hints every month, Prince Heinrich went off with a sigh to the court of the Duke of Detmold-Auer- stadt, whose eldest daughter, Princess Louise, was said to be very charming. Her merits were doubtless many. She had a round, pleasant face, like an affable moon; she had . all the virtues known as homely; could make bilberry jam and shortbread and cheese cakes; could tell you, to within once, how often a child would be sick after eating a dozen ices; and had two new remedies for a cold in addition to the 178 ordinary ones. In the ordinary course of things the Prince would have received an unexpected telegram next day, sumMcming him home imperative- ly on State business; but Imperial remon- strances had become so emphatic lately that he hesitated, and stayed on wondering whe- ther he could possibly face life with this lady. On the third morniing of his visit he went out for a long solitary walk in the for- est below the castle, in order to decide \ the matter with himself once for all. A great carpet of wild anemones was under his feet, and the spring sun shone through long lines of pines and silver birches to where the tur- quoise and gray waters of the Baltio lay be- low. He wandered on till he came to the edge of a cliff, at the base of which little wavelets were plashing idly, and ferns grew thick, and the air sang with the heating of birds' wings. Suddenly he came upon a small hollow among the ferns with the stately stems of tall pines round it, and, lying in the middle of it, a child, who raised her bead and studied the intruding visitor with cold displeasure. " Prince Heinrich took off his hat, and bowed low, and murmured a few common- places about the fine morning and Ariadne and the pine-scent and the view. • The child before him, he realised vaguely, must be the youngest daughter of his host; he had seen her twice for a moment in the dimly-lit castle passages, and he had heard something about a concourse of governesses and professors whose efforts were making the young lady one of the geniuses of Germany. He studied Princess Elsa now in some little astonishment as she stood up with a quaint little bow and her eyebrows raised in dignified inquiry at his intrusion. How on earth could he have over- looked such a person in the very darkest pas- sages of her father's castle ? Even the plaits in which her hair was done in stiff German fashion could not hide the vivid gold of its colour; her cheeks and lips might have been pink and white porcelain; and her eyes were the blue of the Mediterranean with tte rune sun-light on it.</p>
        <p>RECALLING PAST BIRTHDAYS. " ' You are very quiet here, Princess,' the man stammered, 'being at A loss for some- thing to say. "'lt is my birthday. I am fifteen to-day. I have come -here. . . .to</p>
        <p>"The quiet dignity of the rebuke, Prince Heinrich tells me, had an odd effect upon him. First, it showed him that the child thought he bad followed her here intention- ally; then it caused him to reflect that if he had seen her before he certainly would have followed her.</p>
        <p>"'I hasten to assure you, Princess, that I have come to your retreat by the merest acci- dent. I will but apologise and pass on. I wish I had the vanity to fancy myself wel- come.'</p>
        <p>" "The chilly dignity vanished out of the young face, and a frightened little smile, as of a child who is not quite sure whether she has done something wrong, came there in- stead. _</p>
        <p>" `I —I hope I haven't said anything_. rude? Will you come and sit down?' "Quite eagerly the'Prince obeyed, and took his seat on a little bank of moss, the child sitting beside him, with her hands lying idly in her lap and her eyes looking across the Baltic waters.</p>
        <p>—"You come here to recall past birth- days?' he asked gravely and unsmilingly, knowing how such young folk hate any sus- picion of mockery. "'I have resolutions to make, of course, for my new year. Alas to think that though the year and the resolutions may be new, I am not!'</p>
        <p>"Wholly unable to repress a smile at this quaintly serious lamentation over human im- perfection, Prince Heinrich stood up, walked away for two or three steps, and coming back, stood over the little philosopher, quiet- ly studying the beauties of this flower-petal face and graceful young figure. Conscious of his gaze, and a little troubled by it, the girl stood up, still looking out to sea.___ THE PRINCE CHARMED. "The child had but spoken three or four sentence ,s and each of them had, charmed Prince Heinrich more than he had ever been charmed yet by three days talk with • any other woman or girl. A sudden extraordin• ary whim took possession of him. "'Would you like,' he _asked, 'to come this year and rule over men and women, to make laws for them, to teach them to love vou, and to obey you because they love you?' Yes, one day. . . Come and be queen,' said Prince Hein- rich in a nervous whisper, 'over my people and over _</p>
        <p>rincess Elsa raised her oyes and studiect him silently and gravely for a moment; then dropped her eyes, satisfied. "'You mean what you say, but you have forgotten before I am grown up. De not let us talk more of it now. " ° I want you now.' "' Now !'</p>
        <p>"The lovely child-face flushed pink all over, a little smile of pride and excitement and pleasure parted tho young lips; only the grave blue eyes looked out to sea with doubt and bewilderment and anxiety, and Prince Heinrich watched them with a chilly fears 4whose strenc-th amazed himaelf. But sud- denly proud resolution spreaa all oyez tne child's face, and she turned to her lover with a serious little' bow. __ - _ -</p>
        <p>I have spent all my last birthdays thinking of myself,' she said. 'You COME, to-day to remind me that I have duties in. life, and that oae cannot begin too soon. I iccept the proposal with which you honour Mt', if you are really good enough to mean it.' "And prince Heinrich found himself de- manding and receiving the consent of the Duke of Detmold-Auerstadt, and communi- cating the whole t 3 tory to me, long before he had recovered from his astonishment at 014 answer."</p>
      </div>
