      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>FOOD FOR POOR CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>FOOD FOR POOR CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>In an interview with The Examiner" repre- sentative an officer stated that the march had done•an enormous amount of good. "There were lots of people," he said, "who said they never understood anything about the regiment, and never knew the troops had such a good time. In Widnes the children were given a half-day holiday on the occasion of our visit, and in St. Helens, children, poorly clad, came up with plates and basins and got the food they wished to obtain. Such marches round the districts from which the regiment comes should be made at least once every two or three years, and then recruits would rapidly come in. If, every third year, instead of having manceuvres, the authorities would let the men march through their own districts—say, the South Lancashires in South Lancashire, and the Liverpool Regiment in Liverpool—it would be worth more than all the advertising and all the money spent on manoeuvres. " There were a lot of working men in St. Helens who, although they were employed, were desiring of joining us, after they had seen and heard how well the men were treated. South Lancashire is not a very good recruiting district, but it will be a great deal better when more efforts are made on the lines adopted this last week." _ _</p>
        <p>" Do you think the services of the band have aided you?" queried our representative. "Undoubtedly." was the answer. .</p>
        <p>, The annual dinner of the officers was held in the Drill Hall, Warrington, on Saturday night, and many complimentary speeches were made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>BOYHOOD AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>BOYHOOD AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan President's Reminiscences.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Samuel Collier, President of the Wesleyan Conference, has been spending a few days at Runcorn, the place of his birth. He conuucted services there on Sunday, and in the afternoon addressed a great rally of old scholars of the Brunswick Wesleyan School, where he attended as a boy. Sir Frederick Norman pre- sided, and there was much enthusiasm. Mr. Collier said his earliest recollection was of being taken by his little sister to that school. Although he was sixteen years old when he dedicated his life to God, he felt first in that echool at the early age of eleven years the in- fluence of the Holy Spirit. Young people, Mr. Collier observed, now enjoyed much greater measure of liberty all round than then. On Monday the President was entertained at luncheon in the Town Hall by Mr. R. H. Pos- nett, chairman of the Runcorn District Coun- cil. The toast of his health was proposed by Sir Frederick Norman, and supported by Mr. David Lewis (Mayor of Widnes), and Mr. Ed- ward Imison, all three friends of Mr. Collier from the days of his early boyhood, and school- fellows of his at the old Millhouse school before o helii went to Birkdale uceakl r wele Churchedgi ga inCadoudnrceislsonof Mondaywelcomenfrohni the local he said there were those who looked down upon the Free Churches as a lot of sects, but #when they had set their own affairs in order they would perhaps have sympathy and cbarity for others, some ers. He did not want to add to the flame; the t Free conclusion Churches verrau the lgere at t had ffi charitycn lbtey their watchword until their friends arrived at had met with in regard to the Bishop of Uganda.</p>
        <p>,',L4Actaxl?:</p>
        <p>He hoped the I).S.A. movement would be ad- vanced during the year, and also spread its principles of brotherhood. (Hear, hear.) Al- though they were devoted to making Sunday afternoons pleasant, he did not see why every afternoon should not be pleasant. An attempt had recently been made to try and make Sunday a little more pleasant for the tramwaymen, but the proposal was not carried through. He could not understand how the Tramways Committee, after having arrived at their decision, were weighed by the deputation. To yield to deputa- tions of that kind was indeed decidedly weak. One writer in the paper had suggested that the Tramways Committee should provide cushions and foot warmers so that the worshippers would have the comfort when going to church on ' Sunday morning. (Laughter.) "Trams to Heaven." He thought after that the Mayor and Corpora- tion would be waited upon to provide trams to take people to Heaven. A minister in America was once riding .up a mountain in a train and remarked to his friend, "Why don't they take them to Heaven in trains?" The conductor, overhearing this, said "It won't pay; there is not enough traffic." (Laughter.) Ito did not see why the tramway- men should not have a pleasant Sunday like the rest of people, and he hoped they would soon get what they wanted. (Hear, hear) Religion ought to be made as pleasant and as presentable to the people as possible, for it was a thing of joy and gladness. Every part of the being of man came within its teachings, for it touched life at every point. There were some people who thought that Jesus Christ_ did not countenance such things as gaiety and laughter, but he would say that Jesus Christ took gaiety and laughter as things for granted. The world in which He lived was -a gay and festive one. Contrary to the views of the croakers and kill- joys, the world was really a very beautiful place in which to live.</p>
        <p>Antithesis of Sorrowful. It was the very antithesis of a sorrowful world, and he could not understand people going through it with long faces. He could not: understand how it was that some people were alwaYe Looking on the dark side of life—(hear, hear)—with -the long-drawn-out groan. One would think that Christianity and religion were burdens to them. In the New Testament they saw there was plenty of joy, gladness and hap- piness. (Hear, hear.) He knew it was the endeavour of the P.S.A. movement to present religion in all its bright- ness and grandeur, and he believed it was the duty of all Christian organisations to do what they could in that direction. (Hear, hear.) They must not count anything common or un- clean if God had cleansed it. Jesus Christ came into the world not merely to save souls, but to save the man, body, soul and spirit.</p>
        <p>Recreation as Re-creation. He would, however, like to warn them that they must take great care that their recreation was re-creation, and not something else. Whilst healthy recreation and wholesome amusement _ _</p>
        <p>was necessary, he believed that a warning was needed on account of the mad race for pleasure and amusement which seemed to take hold of so many people, and which was playing havoc with the home life of the country. Some people were only at home to dress and sleep, for their life was one continual round of pleasure. It would be a very sad day if the home life of the country were destroyed through this mad taco for pleasure. (Hear, hear.) These people wasted most valuable time in the pursuit of pleasure. At one end of the social scale they had the man who must have his motor car and his yacht, and he was driven to make money by dishonourable means, and at the other end they had a girl who stole a silk handkerchief and pawned it for ninepence. When asked for, an excuse she said she had done it to go to amuse- ments.</p>
        <p>" Mad Race for Pleasure." • It was to that, that the mad race for pleasure was leading the nation. They were on the earth to serve God, to achieve strong Christian eliaracters,and to make the lilies of fellow-beings brighter, holier and better. (Applause.) FIVE PRESIDENTS IN 21 YEARS. _ . _</p>
        <p>Mr. CLEGG thanked all those who had assisted in the services, including Miss Watts, who played the pianoforte accompaniments. During the whole 21 years they had only had five presi- dents—Mr. Kent, Mr. Hickman. himself (Mr. Clegg), Mr. Jones, Mr. Nelson, and now he had ,been elected again. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Mr. Corlett is the energetic secretary and Mr. Ittiasball the able treasurer.</p>
        <p>Richest in the World. Speaking generally, their society was the largest and• richest friendly society the world. They had £16,000,000 capital and 1,500,000 members, which spoke well for the thrift and self-dependence of the members. Dealing with. the admirable way in which the various lodges were conducted, he said that in regard to the National Insurance Act they were going to administer it strictly in accordance in with the law, and also with credit to them- selves. They had good and capable officers in the lodges, which was a very good sign. He would like to see all the members animated with that spirit of self-dependence which char- acterised their antecedents. They should strive to do all they could for their fellow men, and if they did that he had no fear for the War- rington District in the future. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>A NEW EXPERIENCE. P.P.G.M. BRADSRAW proposed the toast of the " Newly-initiated Member." It was, he thought, the first time in the history of the Friendly Society movement in Warrington that they had a Friendly Society worker as Mayor of the bOrough—(applause)—and he thought Friendly Society members appreciated the fact that one of their members had been elected to that high position. He thought the turn-out Which members of Friendly Societies made when ! the Mayor went to church in November was one which showed that Bro. Peacock had been ' elected Friendly Society Mayor of the borough. (Applause.) The Mayor had taken a great in- terest in the Friendly Society movement for a good many years. Ile started in his own society at the very bottom, and had since been through all the chairs of office.</p>
        <p>Joined Nearly Every Society. He had, during the Past few years, joined nearly every society with a vikw to getting in contact with the different lodges, and they wel- comed him as a member of the Manchester Unity. (Hear, hear.) In the very early days of the society they were suspected by the Churches and hounded by the State. There was now a splendid feeling of brotherhood obtaining throughout the lodges. In that lodge there were 600 members, and notwithstanding the large amount they had Paid out for sickness they that they must not forget that malingering had X 4,000 invested. which was bringing in 41 and 41 per cent. After eulogising on the benefits of the Act in cases of sickness, he said was going on, and until the doctors were placed under the control of the Friendly Societies they would not have the satisfaction they desired.</p>
        <p>cessTlfiney, tealrli:loTlOffitleiec i, Pet Schemes." The Mayor's 3alnadyoit•hiavtouhlde lwilerda sstuicll- all knew his Worship . find time to continuo his activities in connec- tion with the Friendly Society movement. They speaker see completed. One was the bridge. (Laughter.) There would be a doubt as to whether—when the other parts of the hi'idge were completed—the part which had People would hardly recognise it. Ile (the opened belonged to the new bridge or not. take drastic measures to complete that object. bridge was It was ) was afraid the Mayor would Tiave to to be hoped the workmen at the bridge would not go on strike. (Laughter.) He was afraid that the delay in the completion of the caused not so much by the work- men or the contractors, but by aldermen who went down to the bridge day after day to see how the work was progressing. (Laughter.)</p>
        <p>, Are Aldermen the Delay? The workmen were so filled with admiration (hear, at seeing real live aldermen that they could not get on with their work. (Laughter.) If the Mayor would bring into effect a bylaw to the effect that tho Chief Constable take into custody all the aldermen found on tile building, the ili oal dnuigil,h‘g, itslei bridge would be completed during the Mayor's term of office. which the The other scheme the Mayor wished to see completed was the widening of Buttermarket- street 'Which had been delayed far too long. If they turned out the Corporation steam roller Mayor could consider during the next week. (Laughter.) - . shoeme demolished Sunday .nightTilat awllaistillaaersiLadaneld)sq</p>
        <p>In conclusion he assured the Mayor that he would always he welcomed amongst them. and, as P.P.G.M. Massey said in the iniliation charge, 'We hope you will live long and die a good Oddfellow."</p>
