      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>IT IS REALLY NECESSARY</head>
        <p>IT IS REALLY NECESSARY</p>
        <p>Ist</p>
        <p>EVERY WOMAN Should send two stamps for our 32-page Illustrated Book, containing Valuable Information how an Irregularities and Suppressions may be entirely avoided or removed by simple means. Recom- mended by eminent Physicians as the only Safe, Sure and Genuine Remedy. Never fails. Thousand. of Testimonbll3. Ertablighed 1862. MR. PAUL BLANCHARD, Claremont House, Daleton-lane. London. THr T'</p>
        <p>tE NEW FRENCH REMEDY. Not No 2 No 3 . CURESIIIiStS 'HERAPIONChe price 219 leading BLOOD POISON, KIDNEY, BLADDER, URINARY DISEASES. DISCHARGES. WEAKNESS. PILES. SEND STAMP ADDRESS ENVELOPE FOR FREE BOOKLET To DR. LE CLERC MED. CD, HAVERSTOCK RD, H AM PSTEAD, DON,RNG. I A CUR—E-1 PARIS DEPOT 12, RUE CASTIGLIONE. NEW YORK DEPOT : 90, BEEKMAN ST. I FOR YOUI TRY NEW DRAGEE (TASTELESS) FORM OF EASY TO TA,,E TH ERAPION SA LZIANSOnCURE. SEE THAT ERASE MARKED WORD THERAPION IS ON BRIT. O,IVT. STAMP AFFIXED TO ALI...GENUINE PACKETS.</p>
        <p>"I -low IS IT TERT CLARKE'S BLOOD Mricruan has obtained such a great popularity?" is a question which has perplexed many. The answer is that it is unquestionably. the finest Blood Purifier that Science and medical skill has brought .to light. Thousands of wonderful cures have been effected by it. For Scrofula, Scurvy, Eczema, Skin and Blood Diseases, Bad Legs, Pimples and Sores of all kinds, its effects are marvellous. Sold every- where at 2s. Pd. per bottle. 13eware of worthless imitations and substitutes.—Advt. 2.</p>
        <p>All along the route the men were received with much enthusiasm, and at St. Helens and Widnes they were accorded a cordial and hearty recep- tion. Concerts, which were very much enjoyed, were given by the band, and " Tommy " had plenty of opportunities of heeing the district, as ,well as being able to visit the picture palaces , and theatres free. The Mayors of Widnes and St. Helens entertained the officers and men, and the townspeople were delighted to have their own regiment amongst them—especially the pit- brow girls, who " blew " kisses to the men. St. Helens and Widnes were the happy hunt-: ing grounds, and on the return march to War- rington on Saturday there was some enthusiasm displayed, and when passing Messrs. Crosfield's Soap Works the whole of the employes, having been granted half an hour's leisure, turned out and cheered lustily. As a number of the men Put it, "'We never knew how much we were thought of until now." Major Green, the officer commanding the depot, and Lieutenant Baines, the recruiting officer, have visited the town each day by train.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>TO TAKE</head>
        <p>TO TAKE</p>
        <p>"TRAMS TO HEAVEN."</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Wynne Jones and the Run.</p>
        <p>ning of Sunday Cars.</p>
        <p>Warrington P.S.A.'s 21st Birthday</p>
        <p>Since its inauguration in 1893 the Warrington P.S.A. Society has proceeded apace, its activities have increased, and it has been vastly enlarged in numerical strength. The 21st anniversary and coming-of-age meeting was conducted on Sunday afternoon. Mr. E. Lunt presided, and the principal speaker was Mr. E. Wynne Jones. Mr. Clegg, the president, was also on the plat- form. During the afternoon excellent selections were given by the I'.S.A. Orchestra, under the able leadership of Mr. Hannan. Mr. A. Pearce was the baritone soloist, Mr. Crooks solo violin- ist, and Mr. F. Jubb played a flute solo. Mr. Hedgecock read the lesson, and Mr. Minshall led the prayers. The speeches signified a very optimistic and hopeful spirit, and the whole meeting was brief, bright and brotherly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>STARTED WITH 14 MEMBERS.</head>
        <p>STARTED WITH 14 MEMBERS.</p>
        <p>The CouamAs said he scarcely thought it was so long since that society was first got together at Friars' Green, under the auspices of the Y.M.C.A. He was favoured on that occasion with being appointed the first secretary, and he was very pleased to be there that afternoon to see such a healthy gathering. They started with a membership of about 14 on the first Sunday, and that number had grown consider- ably from time to time. Mr. Kent was the first President, but he•liad passed away. He (the speaker) was, however, pleased to see several friends there who were present at the first meeting. Their President was one and he was to be complimented on having stuck to that gathering for so long and done so well for the cause. (Applause.) He was glad to see they were making such progress as to have an orches- tra. They could not muster one at first. He was much struck at the first meeting of the I'.S.A. Federation he was privileged to attend in Warrington /by the kindly, brotherly feeling which perrneated the gathering, and in which everyone seemed to participate Ile felt he was amongst those who were trying to do some- thing for the betterment of mankind, and in doing that work they were not set apart by creeds or doctrines. He hoped they would be as successful as they deserved to be in all their efforts. Mr. 'Jones, who would address them, was an old president. and, having that cause at lipart: had worked his hardest for it. (Ap- plause.) HEVIVED PLEASANT MEMOBIES. Mr. Jones said that gathering brought back memories of many pleasant afternoons spent in that P.S.A. He sincerely hoped that the 21st year was going to be a memorable one in the history of the P.S.A., because they were making a definite, systematic stand, not only to improve their position, but to carry on the great work to which the P.S.A. had been called. He had looked up a copy of " The Examiner" of 21 years ago, in which there was some very interesting news. In the issue he saw that the river could be crossed on ice floes.• The Town Council had met, and from what he could gather they were no better then than they were now. (Laughter.) They were discussing as to how much shonld be allowed to those who went on deputations to London. They were very particular about the dignity of the Council, for one Alderman said he was determined to keep up the dignity of the Council, and was not going to drink "pop" for anyone; he was going to have wine. He (the speaker) ventured to say that the present Mayor of Warrington would keep up the dignity of the Council better than all of them, and he could do it without wine. (Applause.) There was also in that issue of "The Examiner" a report /that distress prevailed in the town, and worthy efforts were made to cope with it, although the Town Council were discussing whether members should have ,t 2 per day, whilst there was so much want about. On January Bth, 1893, the P.S.A. was founded in Warrington. The Ship Canal was completed in that year, and other interesting events took place. Principles of Brotherhood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>MAYOR AS ODDFELLOW.</head>
        <p>MAYOR AS ODDFELLOW.</p>
        <p>[The Bridge is Humorously Discussed at</p>
        <p>Installation Social.</p>
        <p>DO ALDERMEN CAUSE THE DELAY?</p>
        <p>Friendly Society workers are exceedingb proud of the present Mayor, for not only ha he shown an enormous amount of interest ix• the movement, but many members in the towx can take him by the hand and call hth. "Brother." Saturday was a red-letter day it the history of the local branch of the Odd. fellows, Manchester Unity, for his Worship was initiated a member of the "Prince of Wales " Lodge in the Pelican Hotel, by the District Lecture Master, P.P.G.M. Massey. The cordial hand of fellowship was extended on every side, and the Mayor must have been greatly impressed by the magnificence and grandeur of the initiation ceremony. The room , had been tastefully decorated, and at the top end 'were the words, "A hearty welcome to his Worship the Mayor, Brother Peter Peacock."</p>
        <p>BRILLIANT OFFICIAL ARRAY. A highly enjoyable social evening was spent, ' under the presidency of N.G. Price, who Was supported by the following officers:—p.p.o.m. I,awrence, P.D.G.M. Spann, I.P.P.G.M. figham, P.C.S. Webster, pl:).G.m.'s Massey, I'. S. Steel, T. Rigby, IL llalford, Holbrook, U. P. Bradshaw, G. F. Freeman and Peter Les- ter. The lodge officers were: L.G.M. Marsh, V.G. Jones, E. S. Dudley, J. T. Tonge, and P.S. Wadsworth. Past Giands, officers and mem' wrs of the Warrington District were also Pres- lit. Councillor Osborne was one of the visitors The Management Committee had gal'T to a ;rest deal of trouble in arranging an enjoyable wogramme, and they are to be congratulated m the result. The artistes who entertained the ^ompany were: N.G. A. E. Price, P.P.G.M. Ntassey, P.G. Willett, Bro. Warburton. Mr. W. Whittle, Mr. W. 'Kilduff and Mr. E. Walker.</p>
        <p>3000 WARRINGTON MEMBERS. The toast "The Duke of Lancaster " was honoured, on the proposition of the pIAIRMAN, who said that being loyal Oddfellows, it was essential that they were loyal citizens. L.O.M. I)isass proposed the toast "The War- rington District." He said they bad 12 lodges with over 3,000 members, and such figures should give them cause to think how bard the management work must be. In the past the Warrington District had been well officered, and the present officers were no exception to the rule, and he thought that during 1914 they would be able to overcome all obstacles. (Hear, hear.) _ _</p>
        <p>A GOOD AUGURY. P.G.M. LAWRENCE replied and said he than the members for the honour they conferred 'Ton him when they elected him to the import- ant position lie now held. He extended hearty welcome to the Mayor, and said it would not be a surprise to those present to know that his Worship had been made an Odd&amp;llow, knowing as they did how deeply interested be was in the Friendly Society movement in War- rington. (Hear, hear.) He thought it was a good augury for the year that the civic head of the borough had become a member of that lodge. .</p>
        <p>tuditor, and since that time he had been in thee in one direction and another in that noble )rder. having referred to his long association vith Friendly Societies and spoken of the good 'eeling which existed, his Worship said that vithout any disrespect to any of the other trders to which lie belonged, he could say that he ceremony they had had that night was the most impressive and beautifully laid out he had heard. (Applause.) Bro. Massey was deserv- ing of credit for his lecture, which he had learned off by heart. He would always remem- ber that night, for it would bring back pleas- ant memories. Whilst he had not been an Odd- fellow, it had been his pleasant duty in times of distress to give a hand of friendship and help to some brother requiring assistance—(ap- plause)—for he had felt all Friendly Society members were his brothers.</p>
        <p>"That Memorable Day." Referring to "that memorable day, Novem- ber 16th," he looked back with pride to the way Friendly Society workers supported him. The memory of that Sunday would never be effaced , from his mind. His thanks were specially due to Chief Marshal Bradshaw for the way he had conducted things on that day. He also must bave had that outlook Of things—that all Friendly Society members were his brothers. He had kept records and photographs of that day, which he would be able to hand down to his children—(applause)—to treasure. He had been to seven high councils in connection with the Foresters, and he was proud to be the first benefit member of a Friendly Society to hare that distinction in Warrington. (Applause.) He blamed the Friendly Societies for making urn Mayor, for they were the ones who had en- muraged him in his speaking and his public. life.</p>
        <p>To Deal With the Aldermen. With regard to the aldermen Bro. Bradshaw ! wanted locking up, there were ways and means -A dealing with those men—(laughter)and also with tvgard to Buttermarket-street he had his remedy. Ills motto had always been "Do it now," and Councillor Osborne would bear him out in that principle. They were on a committee of seven or eight who were going to make things "go," and they had gone on very well so far. Although they had only been in existence a week they had had two meetings. On Friday they met Mr. Web- ster and Mr. Cobb, who stated that, in their opinion, the bridge would be completed in 1914. Personally, he did not believe it. (Laughter). That was quite candid, and he told them so. When the committee got into proper working ! order they would make the bridge go as quick itS possible, and so far as he was concerned there were to be no bones about it.' It had got to go, or he went off.</p>
        <p>Mayor " Rootin' Round." In regard to Buttermarket-street, he might say that being on all the committees, he had: been " rootin'" round the corners, and told those concerned that if they did not get on the move, he would move for them. Buttermarket- Street was no good for the town, and to have those buildings toppling over was not good for the inhabitants. He looked at the rates, and if they kept vacant land there, who was going to open shops. No one was going to buy land to build shops on when there were such dilapi- dated toppling buildings higher up. Better buildings were undoubtedly needed, and they would have to move in that direction. In one of his speeches he had said quite in- tentionally that he would see the bridge com- pleted in his term of office. He could please himself over it. He hoped it would be com- pleted in his term, of office, but they would have to wait and see What was offered to him. (Ap- plause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>FORESTER FOR 24 YEARS. . _</head>
        <p>A FORESTER FOR 24 YEARS. . _</p>
        <p>-.• • -••- Mayor's Record as Friendly Society Member. Replying to the toast, the MAYOR said he fully appreciated the cordial welcome they had given him as a member of that noble Order. He had been thinking of joining that lodge for some time, and he fully appreciated being con- nected with that Order, of which he had been jealous many times. Ile had been is member of the Hearts of Oak, Foresters and Shepherds since he was 16 years of age. It was 24 years since he first took oMee in the Foresters as</p>
        <p>Insurance Act Malingering. In regard to the question of malingering under the Insurance Act, he would say that all the societies ought to join and make some com- mon action. They must be united, and he was sure that if they all put their heads together they would come to some proper conclusion, which •would be of benefit to members and em- PloYers. Ile hoped, in conclusion, that he would some time be, not an honorary member, but a subscriber year alter year. (Loud ap- plause.)</p>
        <p>THE COUNCIL "LODGE." The toast of "The Visitors" was submitted by the Chairman, and -Councillor OSBORNE, in reply, said he was sure they had in the Mayor a member who would do credit to the lodge. He was a gentleman whom they in the Council tried to obey. (Laughter.) He said "try," because Ile was afraid some of them got altogether out of hand sometimes, in another little lodge not far from there—(laughter)—and where they were not so happily associated as they were that night. Sometimes they said things they ought: not to say and they were called to order in a manner which made them wish they had not got up to speak. (Laughter.) In regard to Friendly Society work, he could ,not conceive what the country would be if it were not for Friendly Societies. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>PORTRAIT OF THE MAYOR.</head>
        <p>PORTRAIT OF THE MAYOR.</p>
        <p>A hearty vote of thanks was tieCC;ded the pianists and artistes, on the proposition of P.G. COAKLEY, P.G. WEBSTER replying. , I.P.P.G.M._ HIGRAM proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman, who, in reply, intimated that they would like the Mayor to present them with a framed photograph of himself. (Applause.)' The MAYOR said he would be pleased to do that. (Cheers.) ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>PROPHECY FULFILLED.</head>
        <p>A PROPHECY FULFILLED.</p>
        <p>As far back as last September, experts de- clared the present Currant crop to be the finest received within living memory. Their opinion has turned out literally correct. It is rich in quality, and exceptionally savoury. Added to those desirable attributes there is another ele- ment which must unfailingly bring joy to the heart of the housewife. The price of Currants is decidedly moderate—nay, as compared with the wonderfully high food value of the fruit, Currants are remarkably cheap. It is impos- sible to get better value for money in the way of foodstuffs than by buying Currants. If, at all times Currants benefit both health and pocket, then the advantages of the fruit should be emphasised whole-heartedly at the present time. The existing stocks of Currants are not only beautiful, but inherently perfect. They have a richness of flavour that is indescribable. Try them this way to-day.</p>
        <p>CURRANT MARMALADE PUDDING. 4 ozs. Currants, 4 ozs. breadcrumbs, 4 ozs. suet, 4 ozs. sugar. 4 ozs. marmalade, 2 ozs. flour, 2 eggs. teacupful milk. Method: Mix all the ingredients together; grease a pudding basin or mould with butter, and dust with sugar. Put in the mixture, cover with greased paper, and steam 3 hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>– ' RUNCORN YOUTHS' RIDE. ‘.</head>
        <p>- ' RUNCORN YOUTHS' RIDE. ‘.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn- two youths, Alexander Steele, Peel-street, and Harry Woodward, Canal-street, were charged with unlawfully riding horses at a furious rate and on the wrong side of the road. The charge of furiously riding was dis- missed, but in the other case a fine of 2s. 6d. and costs (17s. each) was imposed. Steele and Woodward were further charged with assault- ing John Thomas Lea, a labourer. The Bench ordered both defendants to pay costs of Bs. 6d. each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>. . ••••• SUCCE:se.</head>
        <p>. . ••••• SUCCE:se.</p>
        <p>BY BEADING</p>
        <p>r TH , !</p>
        <p>an introductory journal full of GENUINE adver- In to all classes of ladies and ,-..-f,ntlemen desirous of marriage.</p>
        <p>6d. Pont Free in Sealed Envelope. Ed:tor, 13, Ilogrzth Road, Earl' Court.</p>
        <p>Labour Exchange, Control. The scheme was calculated not to interfere with the rights of parents, and they desired to appoint a committee, who would be a link with the schools and watch the child from the time it left school. ' They would have a representa- tive of the teachers on the Advisory Committee to find, if possible, the position for which each child was best fitted, and to look after the inter- ests of the child until 17 years of age. They would also have an official of their own to watch cases.</p>
        <p>_ Now they were told they must have an official from the Labour Exchange in control, and thus those most closely in touch with the children would not be allowed to deal with the appoint- ments. If the Committee agreed. to the altered scheme, the Board proposed to pull them up at the end of two years and to revise the scheme. If they did not then like it, they must stop, and they would be saddled with an official. He asked the Committee to tand by the School Attendance Committee in their attitude that unless the scheme as sanctioned by them two or three months ago was accepted. they s...culd cease to consider it at all. A similar Sc,E.MO to theirs was submitted by Liverpool and ac- cepted in itis entirety, while the Warrington scheme had been interfered with completely. Warrington did not like different treatment from that given to a neighbouring city. (hear,</p>
        <p>hear.) Canon STEVENSON : From what body does the objection emanate? Mr. PLINSTON : The Board of Education.</p>
        <p>"TURN OUT A DUFPER." they did Committee. Councillor PEMBERTON thought they should sup- port always Theays difficulty port the Swcall that oo s tit Attendance w was made raore difficult a boy wanted to he, and thi icult by the attitude of trade unionists. Trade unions would not allow boys to be put to certain trades. They could not fix up a trade for a child. _</p>
        <p>_ Councillor RODERTS, who presided, said that did not come within their purview. The sub- ject was, the Committee approved of a scheme in which it would have an officer who would be an officer of the Education Committee. Now the Board of Education ,aid: No. we want you to allow this work to be done through an official of the Labour Exchange." Several members take strong objection to the children being dealt with through an official of the Labour Exchange. The question was—should they support the School Attendance Committee in their objection to that departure from the terms and conditions which were at first recom- mended by the Board of Education? Councillor PEMBERTON If they do not agreethe scheme will fall through. I say let it fallt through. It sounds all very well to suppor a scheme under which a boy might finish mil- lionaire, but on. the other hand you flirtry to make a boy an engineer, and he wenturn out a duffer and finally become an engineer's labourer. Mr. 11. KINGDON : Surely this is a case for co- operation. Our Committee, without the assist- ance of the Labour Exchange, cannot go on. Councillor ROBRDTS : Co-operation was in. tended, but we were to have the controlling influence over the scheme. This would make us subordinate, to the Labour Exchange. FIGHT TO THE LAST DITCH. Mr. J. Enos thought it was evident there had been some pressure brought to hear upon the Board of Education by Board of Trade officials, and had ended in compromise. It seemed that was an attempt to remove opposition by stupid compromise, and the Warrington scheme had been spoilt completely. While they asked for co-operation, and the scheme provided for co- operation with Board of Trade officials, the alterations which the Board of Education now asked to be inserted would make the Board of Trade official the predominant power—(hear, hear)—so an official of the Committee would be in a very subordinate position. The Board of Trade official, as the scheme was altered, had the supreme control, and could act independ- ently of the Committee's official. , That was one of the weakest compromises he had ever seen. (fleet, .hear.) After all, the Education Authority must have supreme control over the child up to the age of 17 years. If they had other authorities coming in with.divided inter- ests and divided control very much of the Education Committee's work would be rendered ineffective.</p>
        <p>Tf we are to work out the salvation of the child, added Mr. Upson, the one authority to do it must be the Education Authority. Any-</p>
        <p>TRADES COUNCIL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <p>"Invaluable for Coughs and Colds." Badgeworth, Vicarage, Cheltenham. a= Dear Sirs,—l have used Angier's Emulsion for many years and have found it an invaluable remedy for colds, coughs, an Bronchial troubles. The members of my family have receir =— so much benefit from its use that lam only too glad to ha \ zr= an opportunity of bearing my testimony to its efficacy as mark of my gratitude. I constantly supply it, when needed, to the poor of my parish. (Signed) (Rev.) J. H. HODGEQ• Free Sample Coupon. ,==</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>DUAL CONTRA EVIL.</head>
        <p>DUAL CONTRA EVIL.</p>
        <p>Education Scheme Blighted by Board of Trade Interference. BLIND-ALLEY OCCUPATIONS.</p>
        <p>When the Warrington Education Committee promulgated a scheme for a Juvenile Employ- ment Bureau, calculated to prevent children entering blind-alley occupations, it was thought the matter would be plain sailing, but the Com- mittee reckoned without the prospect of inter- ference from Government officials Through such interference a most beneficent proposal for the future welfare of the children has been knocked upon the head. A clue to what had happened was found in the minutes of the School Attendance Sub-com- mittee, which contained this: "A letter was submitted from the Board of Education with reference to the establishment of a Juvenile Employment Bureau, and it was decided that, in view of the conditions laid down in the Board's letter and of their intention to approve of the scheme only for a period of two rew.s the' Board be informed that this committee 'AD- not proceed further with the scheme unless some conditions are withdrawn."</p>
        <p>AUTHORITIES CLASH. Mr. PLINSTON said some time ago the Com- mittee decided to deal with the question, and an official visited them. That official led them to understand that the desire of the authorities was that they should appoint a committee for the purpose of dealing with the work, and if that was not done the Labour Exchange would make the appointment. Mr. Winston Churchill, who was then President of the Board of Trade, in dealing with the Bill, in a speech in the House of Commons, said that there was no intention to put upon the Labour Exchanges the prime work of placing young people in employment. That, he said, must be regarded much more from the educational than from the purely industrial point of view. The Education Committee went on with their scheme and submitted it to the Board of Education. The result was that the Board had altered the scheme in such a way as would compel the Education Committee to agree that the Labour Exchange official, entirely apart from their own official, should deal with the appointments of children to vacant places.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
