      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>Working Class ReDresentation on</head>
        <p>Working Class ReDresentation on</p>
        <p>Public lwaies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>LABOUR M.P. QUESTION.</head>
        <p>LABOUR M.P. QUESTION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Traues and Labour Council was Lehi luA week.</p>
        <p>THE NEW CIiAIRMAIg.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY intimated that Mr. S. H. Smith, who was re-elected as chairman of the Council in November last, had not been re- turned as a delegate of the Life Insurance Agents' Society, of whicn he is. a member, and the Vice-Chairman (Mr. E. liandles) accord: ingly became chairman for the year. In taking up hie new position, Mr. RANntiss wished the Council and its members much pros- perity during the coming year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>EDUCATION COMMITTEE REPRESENTATION.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE REPRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>The SEcarrAav stated that he had received a letter .with reference to Mr. Plinston's position as representative on the Education Committee. The members would remember that at the lag</p>
        <p>meeting they had, after a short discussion, elected him (Mr. Dukes) to represent them on that committee. It was quite apparent that a misunderstanding had arisen with regard to the question.</p>
        <p>Mr. PL./NINON then explained Ms position. which showed that he was elected fry the Council many years ago as their representltive; and also gave a short resume of the work he had done on behalf of trade unionism fiznn time to</p>
        <p>time in that potion, especially in Te;-a-i:d" to the recent question of the extra pay to the day' school caretakers. After some further discussion, the Council accepted the explanation, which thus made Mr. Plinston their representative.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>WOMAN SUFFRAGE. ,</head>
        <p>WOMAN SUFFRAGE. ,</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Poplar Trad Society asking for support to be given to steps which would be taken to secure votes for womefl. in order that the women might be able without further delay to work side by side with men in bringing about better Social and economic cou- ditious.</p>
        <p>After several questions had been answerel the Council decided to accede to the request. In connection with the annual meeting of ti Lancashire and Cheshire Federation of Tras Unions, the Chairman and Secretary were al pointed to represent the Council;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>LABOUR PARTY'S ANNUAL MEETING, The SECRIMRIF stated that he had received tb° wencia far the annual meetina-e the T ahnUT</head>
        <p>LABOUR PARTY'S ANNUAL MEETING, The SECRIMRIF stated that he had received tb° wencia far the annual meetina-e the T ahnUT</p>
        <p>p-artY to be held at Glasgow oni Ttfeesday, the 27th January. After reviewing the business to be done, the Council endorsed the education, Policy of the party, which is contained in-the following reso- lution: "In view of the fact that the education of the mass of working class, children to-day begins and ends at the elementary school, this conference is of opinion that there i urgent need for a generous measure of educational re- 'form irt the direction of providing facilitigs for ,liberal, as distinct from technical, education, thus laying the basis of the national life in an educated democracy. This conference therefore directs the Labour party in Parliament to aP' point a small committee to consider the general question of educational reform and draw up a report thereon. In this connection, the com- mittee appointed is specially directed to con- sider: (1) A modification of the curriculum in</p>
        <p>Primary; :schools, in order that in the later years of school life more time may be even to instruction in the duties of citizenship; (2) the raising of the school-leaving age to 16 years, and the right of children in primary and seconder! schools to maintenance allowances; (3) the limit- ing of the hours of boy and girl labour up to the age of 18 to 30 per week, so as to provide 20 or more hours per Week for physical, technological and general training; (4) the establishment of medical treatment centres in connection with each primary school or groups of schools. Fur- ther, this conference urges that the Govern- ment be pressesi to appoint a Royal IF:ommis- sion to consider the matter of university endow- I ments, with a view to their adaptation to the educational requirements of the people." Mr. Duets said that if the policy contained the resolution was ultimately adopted and brought into effect, it would,be of material bene- fit to the citizens of the future.</p>
        <p>WORKING GLASS REPRESENTATION. A wide diversity of opinion ranged round the postponed resolution of the Gasvrorkere and General Labourers' Union inviting the affilia- tion of the local branches of the Independent Labour party and the British Socialist partl to the Trades Council. Much fear was ex- pressed as to the ultimate issue of such an affiliation.</p>
        <p>Many delegates were of the opinion that trade unionism was not prepared for this course: while Mr. Haley stated that he thought sox" misconception of the resolution had arisen. Ile contended it would be for the general well-being of the Council to invite such application. Mr. Duirms said the Council had many schemes in hand to benefit the working men of the town. schemes which had the sympathy of the parties in question. They wanted direct representstion on the Board of Guardians, Town Council. and other public bodies, in order to further such efforts. In consequence of this fact he thought it was essential to invite the co-opera- tion of these parties to assist them. As re- garded the question of direct Parliamentsll representation, he felt sure that the Council wero fully prepared to enter into it whenever the occasion cure.</p>
        <p>Ultimately it was decided to refer the matter to various local branches of the Council for further consideration.</p>
        <p>THE VICE-CHAIRMAN.</p>
        <p>As a vacancy had arisen on the Executive Committee, it was resolved that Mr. J. Lord bP elected to that position. It was also carried that Mr. Lord be appointed vice-chairman of the Council. On the proposition of Mr. BASTON, seconded by Mr. HEMET, it was resolved that the beet thanks of the Council be tendered to Mr. S. 14' Smith for his services as chairman, and also for his untiring efforts on behalf of Vie Court- cil's work in general.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>DUAL CONTROL EVlL—continvecL</head>
        <p>DUAL CONTROL EVlL—continvecL</p>
        <p>thing which interferes with or weakcns the co trol of ale Education Authority is to render 01 work in the child's interest abortive al ineffective, and we ought to resist it to the ye; last ditch. (Applause.) Mr. Pmerox argued in favour of rejectil the scheme. The minute was unanimously adopted.</p>
        <p>;</p>
      </div>
