      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>FIELD</head>
        <p>THE FIELD</p>
        <p>Warrington First Lose by 51 8 Points at Hull; Reserves Defeated by 20-2 Points at Home.</p>
        <p>COMIC INCIDENT AT THE "A" MATCH.</p>
        <p>A 8 was only to be experfek owing to weak- ened sides through the strike, both the War- rill, Oat teams met with defeat. The first side. *II" was practically the usual "A" team, were beaten by 51 points to eight at Hull, whither wer'e accompanied by abut 400 followers. -2" e second side, winch was more or less a Beratch one, were defeated by Huddersfield "A" at Wilderspool by 20 points to 2, not such a bad i!'erfarinance when all circumstances are taken 41,.st°, enashieration. „ a match away was not such a runaway t-iffln• for Hull as the score might lead one to Warrington played a surprisingly 'fine de:-lisive gime, and this undoubtedly kept the ;eel's' down. In comparison with the match aryBt Year, Saturday's score is not an extraordin- u;" °lle. With a full team last season War- Who were later good enough to reach Cup,. final, were defeated by 31 points to 5: Vnis time the reserve side were losers only tfi Innate to 8. That fact alone speaks for .odecr",lind defensive game the reserves played day. Th. THE HULL CALIBRE. scra;„Diay calls for little comment. It was a und"Y game, as was only natural to expect feater the circumstances, but one pleasing Guisos b asthe Welcome given to the Warring- t sY the 6,000 home supporters. Warring- tZ- re-arranged the side somewhat, and Green, y_e forward, went full-back. Waywell, Shaw, tirti_ting and Jordan were the threequarters, —auwn and Douglas played at half, and the for- , rds were : Naylor, Harmer, Bradshaw, Bevan, 'aeardman and Critchley: • iiivlarrington, from the kick-off, took the game or 'ilitncl, and the Hull line was for a minute di.;l;cv° danger. This initial attack was ,t 'Yet back, and Hull took the play in hand. !ave by Jordan Checked what looked like ertitill try. It came later, however, but was -converted. Then Boardman broke away t‘‘l`ll Et-telling bit of footwork, only for the ball "e gathered, and the Bull threequarters sent O a their way with a'passing bout. After this it ,Iras evident that Warrington were not of the '4ltill calibre.</p>
        <p>BROWN•FORCED TO RETIRE. brown got his wrist badly injured, and was all absentee during the rest of the match. Still tWarrington were able to come away with a few ne bursts. Shaw and Waywell got on the run, off-side by Hull pulled them up, and no goal lted from the kick. The best bit of work by Warrington soon inae along. Douglas got the ball and handed °n promptly to Shaw, who after gaining 4i,,°llnd Passed on to Waywell, who ran over a ka try well out. Bradshaw had to take the fie!rnm a difficult angle and landed a beauti- WX°I. After this Hull swarmed all over th,E,-;.n,gton, and tries were quickly run up. At trie,'lltrval Hull were leading by 3 goals 7 (27 points) to 1 goal 1 try (5 points).</p>
        <p>The- "tit. AND GREEN ALL THERE. INII, %tt'e".(l half was still more in favour of stay the ,Warrington men not being able t: i'ith his 'ace• Waywell was most conspicueeoti ,consider. Bi/laq tackling methods, and Gr: noven Dositio irig that lie was in a somewhat l 11(4 so 11 at full-back, was all there. If worker. wai nu_ . reliable as Jolley, he was a hard ." ' s;rker.gtOn i ,a4:1'18 ou by arrie of the rare bursts hie 14v,r succeeded in scoring res")th,,_ er try. 4 ega wtanB not converted, and soon after Hull ,w . tt,thedrl i: Pile up their score. All interest van.- iwar i„.2l's the game when it was seen that N Bpi' °n were easily beaten, and to the relief the eclators and players the whistle blew with 13 trsi c°re Rtanding in favour of Hull by 6 .goals Zlieu-114.1.4Y cu he hes (,51 Points) to 1 goal 2 tries (8 points). I) thus remained in the possession</p>
        <p>BY " Scrum."</p>
        <p>RUNCORN TEAMS AGAINST WARRINGTON:</p>
        <p>promptly tackled by the police, amidst the laughter of the spectators. The ball was re- turned, and the ingenious try-getter vanished from the field.</p>
        <p>Runcorn have selected the following teams to meet Warrington at Wilderspool and Runcorn to-day. The first team is: Hughes; Jolley, Grace, Johnson, Basnett; Lewis, Watton ; Jones, Peacock, Donoghue, Prince, Massey, Padbury. The "A" team to play at Runcorn is excep- tionally strong, every back having played in the first team this season: Shaw; Jackson, Youd, Tilley, Deakin; Thornton, Richardson; Shaw, Williams, Runt, Henshall, Burke, Fyles, Raw- lin son. Two Warrington teams were selected on Wed- nesday night, but as there was a possibility of a re-arrangement last night, the Warrington teams for to-day will be found on page 7.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' TEAMS FOR TO-DAY. In the Manchester and District League Rylands' Recs. meet Cadishead, at Cadishead, with: F. Horrocks; T. Laws, S. Carter, A. Hor- rocks and P. Morris; G Warren and J. Gallop; A. Stretford. I. Whittaker, J. Brandwood, F. Mason, H. Morris and D. Slater. In. the St. Helens and District League Rylands' Recs. "A" play Baton Recs., at Gor- sey-lane, with: F. Potter; J. Griffiths, J. Hard- man, J. Warren and T. Scarisbrick; J. Britton and G. Cummings; J. Peers. H. Welsby, A. Myles, S. Cooper, R. Bibby and J. Jackson.</p>
        <p>PICKS OUT OF THE SCRUM. Pithy Pars About N.U. Games. It was not so bad at Hull. The Hull-ites, however, took it easy. Still the Warrington scratch men at first Gave them a bit of a surprise. When the homesters got the measure Of the "A's" they piled on a score. Waywell, for Warrington, did very well. Critchley earned fame as a goalkicker. Who offered to bet his wage that The Warringtonians would win at Hull? They did not, and it was as Well that the bet was not taken on. e e ,43e e The Seniors were more intent on Speaking than playing on Saturday. George Thomas was orator-in-chief For the team on strike at Warrington. George spoke very well indeed, And he had an audience of 2,000. Many politicians would be delighted if They could get such a big crowd listening. But, there, a football strike is a More interest than mere national politics. Bradshaw also spoke very fluently Chesters tried gallantly also to score as a speaker. ‘) The Seconds—or were they Thirds?— Found Huddersfield "A" men too big A handful to hold preperly. Wright's dropped goal waa a beauty. In fact, it was quite all right. Who is the new threequarter-back Discovered during the Wilderspool match? .He seemed good enough- for a position In the Seniors; certainly on the "A" team. • The spectator nearly scored a try! His was the most humorous feat ever Seen on the pitch at Wilderspool.</p>
      </div>
