      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>OF SPORT.</head>
        <p>OF SPORT.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALLER ORATORS. 'LATCHFORD REAC)i THE SEMI-FIJIAL</p>
        <p>Thomas, Bradshaw, and Chesters Address a Large Audience.</p>
        <p>PLAYERS' VERSION OF THE STRIKE.</p>
        <p>The unique spectacle of the Warrington foot- ballers on strike haranguing a crowd of 2,000 was witnessed at the close of the "A" team match at Wilderspool on Saturday afternoon. The crowd gathered round the grand-stand, from the balcony of which three of the team addressed the audience, who were inclined to be flippant over the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>THOMAS AS CHIEF ORATOR.</head>
        <p>THOMAS AS CHIEF ORATOR.</p>
        <p>The orator-in-chief was George Thomas, who said: I speak on behalf of the players on the strike, which has arisen this season. We were driven to it. In the first place it had been suggested that we should receive 3s. instead of 6s. for broken time. (Voices: "Shame.") We always have to play. The players had a meet- ing over this matter and decided we were entitled to 6s. (Cheers.) Broken time, you know! The committee's idea was that men in good employment should receive 69. and labour- ers only 38. ("Boo" and "Shame.") Listen! lam telling you the truth. We who have the least chance of being in regular work think we should have the same pay as a tradesman.</p>
        <p>Helped the Club Through. With regard to training money, we used to receive 3s. a week. This had been stopped. We did not complain owing to the position of the club. (Cheers.) At that time we acted as sportsmen and did not ask for any pay at League matches. Last season we never asked them for anything in League engagements, be- Cause we were on the down grade, and we tried our level best as sportfimen to pull the club through. (Cheers.) Listen to this explanation! In the final against Huddersfield everyone was riromised per man, but we lost and did not receive any- thing. (Voices: " Shame" and " Rotten.") They could have done nothing less than given us winning money of 10s. per man, and winning money is not a bonus. There you have got it!</p>
        <p>I, as the oldest playing member of theTteam, said that the local lads, to encourage them in the future, deaerve some consideration, as they are the best I have ever played with in the whole of my career. I can tell you that they are. You can tell them what you like and they will listen. There is not a bit of ill-feeling amongst the team—the ill-feeling is only with the committee. (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>The Christmas Matches.</p>
        <p>If you can tell me where we are in the wrong we will give way. In the Christmas holiday matches—five engagements—we beat Wigan, with Leigh we won, Oldham we lost to—(A Voice: "Won ")--if we cannot win by fair play we must lose 'something—with Salford we drew. (A Voice: "Hard lines!") We' played the game with twelve men and they gave us win- ning money for that. The Huddersfield match we also ' won—(A Voice: "Good, old man !")—and I think a feat like that is worthy of mention—l never felt a prouder lad in my life than when we beat Huddersfield.</p>
        <p>After gaining the match, some of the players asked what we were to receive for beating Wigan. The reply was: "You will get noth- ing." (Voices: "Shame.") That is the truth! We replied that we wanted a fair and proper answer. We players have to bang our brains out, and if we ask for an allowance they treat us like that. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>Christmas Box—£l.</head>
        <p>Christmas Box—£l.</p>
        <p>- -- We players had a meeting and selected four out of the team to meet the committee, which they did. The committee stated that we should not receive anything extra for winning, but the committee decided we should receive a handsome present for beating Wigan, and if we would look after ourselves during the holiday matches they would make it worth our while. That handsome Christmas box—i. (Laughter and " Shame.")</p>
        <p>If we don't deserve more than .21 in these holiday matches then it is time we chucked down tools. I have brought in X 3, .24 and .25 in the holiday matches. (Applause.) If they think because they have local lads they can do anything with them, I say "down tools !" (Cheers and a Voice:" "Huddersfield got quite as much.")</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>£2 Gift Asked For.</head>
        <p>£2 Gift Asked For.</p>
        <p>The players had a meeting on the Wednesday and asked the committee to meet them, and they refused to do anything. The captain told them unless they met the players on Friday evening the players would not meet them and go to Hull on the Saturday. On the Friday only six committee members turned up. We asked for the Christmas box, which we only think was fair. We drew „t 1 and we asked for £2. Ido not think that was anything excessive. (Voices: "Little enough," and "You want more.")</p>
        <p>"Want Fair Play." They said they would cancel the Hull match or play the "A" team. We having done our best in the holidays were disappointed, and decided to try to get it in a proper manner. If anyone can take anything out of that they are welcome. (Applause.) WA, as players, have done our level best. I have played for 12 years and have done my level best, and I have not been on the club for more than eight weeks during all my career. I do not think we have cost you too Much. (Voice: "No, George.") I say we, as players, want fair play. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>THE. CAPTAIN'S STATEMENT.</head>
        <p>THE. CAPTAIN'S STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>" Bert" Bradshaw, the captain, said: I would rather be on the field than here. (Laughter.) We are heartily sorry for what has happened. We are sorry this affair has got so serious, as wherever you could go you could not find a more happy or jolly team. It is not our fault this grievance came on; all we are sticking out for is what we think is justice. (Voice: "Stick out and you will get it.") As we said last night, "We don't want to do it."</p>
        <p>Hardworking Men. George has told you all our grievances. The committee says " Thou wilt take' all of it and give nowt." We want you to say whether we are in the wrong or the right. (A Voice: "In the right.") We ask for fair play! We are hard-working men like yourselves. If you have a grievance you have got the remedy in your own hands. You can go to your employer and give in your notice. Can we do that? ("No.") No; we're fixed. They have us, we're bound hand and foot for ever. They have us and ope r) o n mean to keel) us there. The players request Tyohilurtsodaayllonwightth.em(Vtooicegso: t?' Onitiely meeting p r</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>ALL OF ONE MIND.</head>
        <p>ALL OF ONE MIND.</p>
        <p>• _ George Thomas, in the course of another speech, said: I am not the instigator of the strike; we are all of one mind. • Chesters, in answer th cries for him, mounted the stand and said: As to, all Thomas has said I can add nowt to it. (A Voice: "What did you get up for?" and laughter.) I ask you to pass a resolution to allow the players to come to the meeting on Thursday. (A Voice: "We'll see you there," and applause.) Thomas: Thank you all for giving us this hearing. •The most remarkable gathering ever held in connection with football then canie, to an end, with cheers for the players on strike.</p>
        <p>A full report of the meeting of the club members will be found on another Page.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>They Play a Great Game at New Brighton and Revenue Last Year's Defeat.</head>
        <p>They Play a Great Game at New Brighton and Revenue Last Year's Defeat.</p>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY NOW LIKELY LEAGUE CHAMPIONS</p>
        <p>The most important match to our local Soccer enthusiast was the Cheshire Senior Cup-tie at New Brighton, where the Latchford team were pitted against Hb arrowb ered that these two teams met last season on the same enclosure, when, on that occasion, the locals were defeated. Y and it will be remem- bered When the draw for .this round was made known it gave great satisfac- tion to the Latchford team to know they had the opportunity to avenge themselves _for their previous defeat. It was with a fixed determina- tion to win or die that they entered upon their task. That they attained their object is now a well-known fact; and for the first time in their existence Latchford have won the right to take part in the seini-final for the Cheshire Senior Cup. At the close of last Saturday's match the home captain very generously congratulated the Latchford team on their success, and wishei them good luck in their efforts to win the cup.</p>
        <p>he raced away and made a grand centre, when Poulter headed the ball past Simcock, and added a second point. Excitement was keen amongst the spectators, and from the centre Stephens sent in a long shot, which Simcock managed to tip over the bar, thus conceding a corner-kick, but this was not improved upon. The visitors now made strenuous efforts to reduce the lead, but Gardiner proved too good for them. An injury to Glover caused a tem- porary stoppage, and the home team -again pressed towards the, interval, but no further score was made. Half-time Great -Sankey, two goals; Stockton Heath, nil. On resuming both teams played with fresh to end, and chances were missell on both sides.' energy, and the ball travelled quickly from end Off-side against Shakeshaft sent the visitors back, but another effort was made, the visitors' centre passing out to Stubbs, and he sent in a splendid shot, the ball being a shade' too high. The home team now gained ground and Darling- ton being prominent on the right, passed inside, but J. Ditchfield shot past the post, and Midge tested Simcock, who cleared his lines. The ' game continued for a brief spell in midfield, when a free-kick against Gardiner placed the home goal in danger. The ball was well placed in the goalmouth, and Bond only partially cleared. The visitors' forwards rushed in and the ball was sent between the uprights, and thus the Heathens obtained their only goal. The home team now played with more deter- mination, and advanced on the visitors' goal. A. fine shot from Gardiner missed the goal by inches. The homesters came again, Houghton and Poulter playing well together. The former centred and J. Ditchfield missed an open goal. From the goal kick the Heathens transferred ply to the opposite end, where Dunn missed a fine opportunity of scoring, and good play by Gardiner set the home left going again. Poulter hit the post with a fine drive. Play was now even for a while, and a free kick against Gardiner was of no advantage. A piece of a third splendid play placed the visitors' goal in danger, and before the ball could be got away gGoaarld. nRe re s uwlat s: Greatsucces ssf aunl kieny rtehgri es te ge r ionagl ; Stock- ton Heath, one goal.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Second Division. Wilderspool Brewery V. Excelsior. In the Brewery Park. The opening exchanges were very even, the visitors being the first to become aggressive, and the home goalie was called upon to save several times, while a fine shot from Cox just skimmed the bar. The visit- ors raced to the other end, and lost sever-al good openings by getting off-side, but the homesters could not do much against the visitors' half- backs, who returned the ball to their forwards, and the home defence were hard pressed and kicked into touch for relief. From a throw-in Walker got possession, beat several opponents, and passed to Cox, who scored with a grand drive. From the centre kick the home forwards by good passing got near the visitors' goal area, when Rigby cleared with ,a timely kick, and shortly after the interval was called, the visit- ors leading by one goal to none. On resuming the Brewery lads went off with a dash for the visitors' goal, where Rigby and Boyd defended splendidly, and drove them back. From this stage the Excelsiors threat- ened danger, their passing and shooting being good. Pritchard scored the second goal. It was only on rare occasions that the home for- wards got into their opponents' half, and they were easily held in check by the visitors' backs, and from a good passing bout Cox added the third goal. From now the home team crumpled up against the attacks of the visitors, and the ensuing play was confined to the home half, and the visitors indulged in shooting practice. Cox, Droving himself to be the best marksman, added four more goals to his credit, and Morris and Walker also scored goals. The game ended as follows: Excelsior, nine goals; Wilderspool Brewery, none. St. Barnabas' V. Fairfield United. These teams met in their return fixture at Lovely-lane, when the ground was in a wretchea condition. The visitors won the toss, and from the ,early movements the home team were the more dangerous, the visitors not being able to withstand the onslaught on their goal. The score began to rise rapidly, and at the interval the home side were credited with six goals. The second half was merely a repetition of the first, the visitors only playing in a half- hearted manner, and the homesters experienced no difficulty in piling on the agony. The game ended with the score of 12 goals to none in favnur of St. Barnabas'. The scorers were Shuttleworth (4), Woods (2), Vernon (2), Warham (2), Maddock and Glover.</p>
        <p>WIDNES AND DISTRICT ALLIANCE. Stockton Heath Reserve V. Muspratt's Works. On the West-avenue ground. The "heathens" had nee best of the play during the first ten minutes, and the visitors' goalkeeper had 'to save. The game continued even, but no scor- ing took Place and the interval arrived with a clean sheet. The homesters began to force the visitors back to their own half, and it was only by good work by the visitors' goalkeeper that prevented a score. The home forwards did not combine at all well, and many grand opportunities were wasted. The game ended in a pointless draw.</p>
        <p>WIDNES MINOR MEDAL COMPETITION. Semi-final Tie. Orford St. John's V. Widnes Juniors. On Muspratt's Works ground, Ditton. Teams: Orford St. John's—Forber ; W. Smith and W. Millward ; J. Denny, W. Stretford„and L. Ather- ton; J. Smith, T. Tickle, A. Meachin, S. Rankin- on and W. Smalley. Widnes Juniors—C. Mc- Manns; J. McManns and J. Allen; Edgerton, Murray and McKeegan: Jones, Kelly, Devonald, Hollins and Gandy. The opening exchanges were erratic, both teams being too excited to play their usual game. However, St. John's were the first to settle down, and Tickle passed out to Smith on the right, and, shooting well into goal, McManns put the ball over the bar. The corner-kick proved of no gain to St. John's, and the ball was sent well out of danger. The St. John's were now playing much better than their opponents, and Smalley broke away and sent in a rattling shot, which McManns saved, and from the clearance Devonald set his for- wards going, and the St. John's defence had a busy time, until .Millward cleared with a big kick, which was received by Smith, and from his centre Meachin beat the opposing backs and sent in a shot which McManns failed to reach, and thus opened the score for St. John's. This success put new life into the game, and the Juniors could not do much against the St. John's half-backs. Meachin accepted a centre from Smith and had not much difficulty in registering a second goal. The interval was called with the score: .St. John's, two goals; Juniors, nil. The Juniors came along in very promising</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY DEFEAT THE HEATHENS."</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY DEFEAT THE HEATHENS."</p>
        <p>- —..—. The next important match to all local fol- lowers of the game was decided on the Penketh Recreation Ground, where Great Sankey de- feated the lads from Stockton Heath. There was a record crowd of spectators to witness the game, which was fast and keenly contested, the feature being that at the nsittionrgs had only six players on the field, affintFe7tviiwas while they were thus short- handed that the home team scored their first goal. The visitors were at fault in their late arrival, but after the first goal the full com- plement turned out and the game was keenly contested throughout. The home team played a splendid game all round, their players having a very good understanding with each other, and of the peculiarities of the ground; while the visitors, having recogoisen—' the difficult task they had to face, had strengthened their ranks by the inclusion of H. Sankey, a veteran Heathen player, who did well at full-back, and Saltihaeknlaft and Durin , of Latchford, Great Sankey thus retain their unbeaten record and have greatly enhanced their chances of beconi- ing champions of the League. eh" , The match arranged between Divex and Daresbury, to be played on the ground of the former, did not take place. and when the official appointed to take /at the dressing-rooms there were no players to be seen. he officials f of the game arrived to notify him that the match was cancelled. T charge o the Dives Club failed Juniors in the Widnes Minor Medal Competi ~ tioTnh see mOir Orford St. tSi te., oJh:f no :le tearn met Widnes s pratt's enclosure. The lads from Orford proved thems?lves superior to their opponents, and in the first half gained a lead of two goals, and in the second half, after the Juniors had reduced the lead, St. John's penned them in their own quarters until they had regained a better lead, and at the close of play the score was three goals to one in favour of St. John's. Orford St. John's are now in the final and are anxiously awaiting the result of the semifinal tie between Bank Quay Rees. and St. Barnabas', which will be played in Warrington on February , 7th, if a suitable ground can be obtained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>CHESHIRE SENIOR CUP-TIE. Harrowby. y. Latchford..</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE SENIOR CUP-TIE. Harrowby. y. Latchford..</p>
        <p>• .-- - - , __..._. At New Brighton. The Latchford team Were: Kite: Haspell and Robinson; Downs, E. Webb and Carter; Smith, P. Webb, Lees, Fogg and Stubbs. Play at first was in favour of the homesters, and as the visitors got warm to their work play became more even. The home right wing met with strong resistance from Carter and Robinson. After 15 minutes good play by the visitors' left ended in Fogg scoring an offside goal. From the goal-kick Myers got was called whe upon possession and forced Kite to handle, and the ball going out to Carter, he transferred to Lees, who sent out to Stubbs on the left, who raced away along the touch-line, centred finely, and Fogg fastened on the ball and scored the first goal for the visitors after 25 minutes' play. ' From the centre-kick the game was hotly con- te hvisitors' ball ,tested, and the homesters gradually worked to- wards out l .K it: Davis, in a grand centre from near the corner-flag, to save, vet,h e Carter heading clear. The ball was again re- turned towards the visitors' goal, when Ackerley, with a fine effort, beat Kite and made the scores equal. The home team, encouraged by this success, now made strenuous efforts to gain the lead, and Kite distinguished himself by making several splendid saves. With play in mid-field half-time arrived with the scores one goal each. The game was resumed at a fast pace, and every inch of ground was contested. When the homesters' right wing became very dangerous, Robinson cleared finely at the critical moment. Lees obtained the leather from the clearance, made .a splendid solo effort, and in his final attempt he pushed the ball a shade too far, and it went outside the posts. The home forwards transferred the scene of play and the visitors' defence was severely tested, but they eventually cleared their lines. The ball was returned to Myers, who transferred to Davies, who sent in a terrific drive and only missed the goal by inches. This brought relief to the visitors' defence, and at the other end Smith was pro- minent with a fine centre, which P. Webb was, 1 unlucky in placing over the bar. At this stage the visitors were having the best 0- f the ex- changes, and Smith forced another corner. The ball, which was well placed in front of goal, was ' headed out, and Carter got' possession and shot straight for goal. Sutcliffe falling, only par- tially cleared, and P. Webb rushed in and notched the visitors' second goal. Excitement was now very high, and the Players on both sides worked very hard. The home team made strenuous efforts to get on equal terms with their opponents, while the visitors' defence was very sound and would not give way. At the other end the visitors could not break thronoh, the half-backs on both sides playing a '' very determined game, and when the final whistle was blown Latchford ran out winners, the result being: Latchford, two goals; Harrowby, one goal. On the visitors' side Kite, Haspell, Robinson Carter, P. Webb and Fogg were the pick of a, good all-round team. ' --_</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Great Sankey V. Stockton Heath. The first meeting of these clubs in the above competition took place on the Penketh Recrea. tion Ground, when both clubs had their strong- est teams out. There was a large crowd of spectators, a good number of whom had followed the "Heathens," in order to cheer their favour- ites on. Mr. H. Smith, of Latchford, had charge of the following players: Great Sankey— Bond; E. Glover and R. Stephens; IL. Ditchfield, Gardiner and Ir. Payne; Darlington, Illidge, J. Ditchfield, Poulter and Houghton. Stockton Heath—Simeock; H. Sankey and Gosling; Glover, Denny and Gifford; Antrobus, Jones, H. Shakeshaft, Dunn and Stubbs. At the commencement of the game there were only seven of the "Heathens' " players out. The home captain won the toss and decided to Play clown the slope, and Shakeshaft set the ball rolling. For the first few minutes the visitors held their own, when, from a long shot; Gosling handled the ball in the Penalty area, and H. Ditchfield being entrusted with the kick, made no mistake in placing the ball beyond the reach of Simcock. At this juncture the visitors' team was completed, and the home players, en- eouralzed by their early success, again advanced into the visitors' half, Wher a fine movement was intercepted by Denny, who transferred to krotrobus on the right. and the latter centred well across the field, where Stephens cleared, -Ind then Bond had not much difficulty with a long, dropping shot. The players had now got warm to their' work and were playing a hard game, and the ball.being sent out to Darlington</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
