      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>ASSOCIATION ITEMS.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION ITEMS.</p>
        <p>The Latchford team have brought honour to the club by defeating Harrowby in the Cheshire Senior Cup. They have,won the right tt take part in the semi-final round for the first time in the history of the club.</p>
        <p>E. Webb played a very hard game at centre- half, and the visitors' half-back line was §trengthened by the .inclusien of Downs. The Great Sankey team still remain uncle feated and they gall:led a grand victoty over the "Heathens" by 3-1. Stockton Heath had greatly strengthened their .ranks by including H. Sankey, H. Shakeshaft, Dunn and Stubbs, but they failed to put a cog in the wheel of Great Sankey's line of successes. The Dives Club again failed to fulfil their fixture last week with Daresbury, this time on their own ground.</p>
        <p>St. Barnabas' piled up 12 goals against Fair- field United.</p>
        <p>Lovely-lane Field was not a Fairfield for the United. They say they will not play the Recs. there on Saturday. It is declared to be the worst playing pitch in Warrington and district.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. North Cheshire League. Latchford v. Altrincham St. Vincent's. Warrington and District League. Dareshury v. Stockton Heath. Orford Barracks v. Great Sankey. Excelsior v. St. Barnabas'. Bank Quay Recs. v. Fairfield United.</p>
        <p>Widnes and District Alliance. Widnes County Reserve v. Stockton Heath Reservls.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES. Elmwood-avenue Congregational V. St. George's</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES. Elmwood-avenue Congregational V. St. George's</p>
        <p>Mission.</p>
        <p>These teams met in the return fixture on the Elmwood ground. The Avenue made one or two changes in the team, but the visitors were fully represented. Thomson set the ball rolling and the Avenue besieged the visitors goal, but the shot went outside. After this the "Saints" improved and transferred the leather to the home quar- ters, but the Avenue drove them back, and shortly after the home team were awarded a free-kick three yards from the goal. This, how- ever, was well cleared, and most of the remain- ing play in the first half was in mid-field, until H. Tully got possession and centred, Owing to the centre-forward being attacked imme- diately, he was forced to pass, and the left wing, getting the ball, centred finely, and H. Tully headed the leather through, but the goal was not allowed, and half-time arrived with no score.</p>
        <p>On resuming, the Avenue played well, and the visitors tried hard, to score, but could not break down the home defence. Up to three- quarter time no score had been registered. The "Saints' got going and good play in the home half ended in Thomson scoring for St. George's; and three minutes later Marsden, the "Saint?" latest capture, scored their second goal. A third goal was scored by the Avenue's full-back for St. George's, and when the final whistle blew the score was: St. George's Mission, three goals: Elmwood-avenue, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>TEAMS FOR SATURDAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Padgate United play Emmanuel Old Boys, at Padgate, with: Nork; Warburton and Sankey; Hodkinson, Wadsworth and Williams; John Houghton, Whitlow, Critchley, Hardman and James Houghton. Orford Albion meet Emmanuel Old Boys' Re- serve, at Latchford, with: Griffiths; Foster and A. Humphries; Higham,'Howard and R. Hum- phries; Yates, Smart, Taylor, Robinson and Lager. Orford St. John's play Wilderspool Brewery, at Long-lane, Orford, with: Forber; W. Smith and Millward; Denny, Stretford and Redford; J. Smith, Tickle, Meachin, Sudell and Hankin- son. _ - .</p>
        <p>Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's meet Army Service Corps at Longford with: Shakeshaft; A. Evans and Booth; B. Evans, Brazendale and Parr; Watson, Ford, Richards, Dunn and Burns. St. Wilfrid's Reserves play Stratton at Strat- ton with: Barber; Peters and Carey; Howells, Fairhurst and Hough; Woodcock, Woodward, Ravenscroft, Parr and Hardy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>The following will represent the "Heathens" against Daresbury : Simcock; H. Sankey and.P. Gosling; Marsh, Denny and Gifford; Antrobus, Jones, Shakeshaft, Dunn and Stubbs. Stockton Heath meet Widnes County Reserve with: Harrison; Jefferson and Houghton; Magill, Woodward and Welsby; Woodhead, Isherwood, J. Sankey, Rigby and Vernon. Orford Juniors play Longford, at Orford, with: Pickering; C. Miller and Jackson; Berry, S. Miller and Maugham; Riley, Eaton, O'Day, Houghton and Redford. Emmanuel Old Boys' Reserve meet Orford Albion, at Victoria Park, with a team selected from: Trainor, Brimelow, Day, Robinson, Archer, Ashton, Carroll, Balshaw, Green, Edel- ston, Hankinson. Rigby (captain) and Jones. Emmanuel Old Boys play Padgate United, at Padgate, with: Knight; Eccles and Roberts: Brooks, Hatton and Booth; Wainwright, Mil- lington, Isherwood, Caldwell and Gregory. LATCHFORD NOTES. Latchford first eleven had no match on Sat- urday. The second eleven were at hozae to Southport Canton, and the game resulted in a 10-0 win for Latchford, who are playing well at present, as will be seen by Ihe fact that in the last eight matches they ' awe s-ore.l izl goals against 8. The scorers were Knight (4). Minards (3), Furness, and Wilkinson.</p>
        <p>Teams for To-day. The first team are at home to-day to South Manchester with the following: J. Bruhl; \V Fletcher and C. Billington; J. Albinsen, J. B Wilkinson and T. Banks; F. W. Isherwood, R. A. Dale, J. E. Pearson, D. B. Travis and A. Knight. 'Umpire, Mr. F. Groanls. Buily.ofi 2.30. The second eleven travel to South Manchertor with the following team: G. Mynekyme; Eccles and P. Allanson; 11. Eccles. F. C. New. hold and A. B. Starkey; J. J. McCauley, w. s. Furness, W. IL Minards, A. G. Mars4len and C. Newbold. Train leaves Centrai Statitin 1.55.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH i.WINNINGTON PARKI Stockton Beath were at home to Winn:ngton Park, and after a keencame won t v twa goals against one. At liVinnington Stoektn Heath were defeated by five goals to two. The Lome tern were conOderably strengthene,l in the for- ward line, Bainbridge hirning 0.. t fur the tiist time this season.</p>
        <p>_ Prom the bully-off Winnington got away to the,. home circle and pressed strongly, Dobell opening the scoring after ten minutes. On resuming, play was fairly even and very fast,..llainbridge equalising. Just on half-time Stockton Heath pressed ,strongly, the visiting defence conculies a number of corners in quick stwcession, were, however, .fruitless. Half-time: Stockton Heath, one goal; Winnington Park, one goal. With only a few minutes, to play in the second half; Bainbridge secured possession in the some circle, • and, running .the length Of the held, finished with a splendid shot, which gave the goalkeeper no chance. ,</p>
        <p>Northern are the visitors to the Crieliel Ground to-day.</p>
      </div>
