      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>STAR KINEMA. Opened By the Mayor.</p>
        <p>The inhabitants of Church-street and the sur- rounding districts have flocked to see the beau- tiful and steady pictures at the handsome, luxurious and well-appointed New Star Kinema House, which was formally opened by the Mayor on Monday in the presence of a large assembly. Prior to entering the new building, Mr. E. Con- nor presented the Mayor with a gold key with which he opened the door. Miss Phyllis Light- burn handed a beautiful bouquet to the Mayoress. Several interesting pictures were thrown on the screen for the edification of a select audience, who had been invited. At an interval the Mayor said that when Mr. Connor asked him to open that magnificent building he gave his consent quite readily when he had made inquiries as to what sort of pic- tures were going to be shown. Mr. Connor was an old resident in Warrington, and he was assured that all the pictures would be of an educational character and such that anyone quid send their children to see, and from which they would receive instruction as well as amuse- ment. Having that assurance they could quite understand that he, as the Mayor of the bor- ough, should have no hesitation in opening it, and further to encourage any enterprise which increased the rateable value of Warrington. The building was very commodious and its beauty reflected credit on the architect, Mr. Kay, and the builders, Messrs. Broadhurst and Sons. Under the management, of the fatally of Connor's they would never reeet having opened a picture hall in that neighbourhood, which had not been catered for before. Ile wished the enterprise every success.. (Applause.) Some beautiful scenery was seen in the pie - turd "In the Purka Valley." There were shimmering glaciers and torrential waterfalls, as well as numerous scenic glades and moun- tains. "Old Coupons" was a dramatic film, dealing with an old man who made a livinz, by collecting coupons from cigars; his rescue irom a gang of thieves by an iron-worker and his practical gift as a token of thanks when priva- tion entered his rescuer's home. A masterpiece of the cinematographist's art was "Keepers of the Flock," in which was depicted "The Vil- lage Wedding," after the painting by Sir Luke Fildes. The story told of a young married man's greed for money, his infidelity and sub- sequent repentance. The acting in all the pic- tures was highly commendable. "Bloomer loses his hat" was a very humorous film.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>STAR KINEMA ROUSF. FEATURES FOR NEXT WEEK.</head>
        <p>STAR KINEMA ROUSF. FEATURES FOR NEXT WEEK.</p>
        <p>The second week will stait with a fascinating programme. "The White Lie," an adaptation from the book by William Le Queux, will be shown on Monday, Tuesday and Wedilesday, whilst for the remainder of the week "The Earth Trembles" will be the piece de resist- ance. A new feature is the Auxeto-gramophone, as used at the Free Trade Hall, Manchester, and the Manchester Corporation in the parks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>ROBINSON CRUSOR'S ANTICS. Panto at Royal Court Theatre.</head>
        <p>ROBINSON CRUSOR'S ANTICS. Panto at Royal Court Theatre.</p>
        <p>Great success has waited upon Miss Edith St. Clare's production of "Robinson Crusoe" at the Royal Court Theatre this week. Comedy stands on a very high pedestal, but further pleasure is afforded by the latest songs and the Pretty dancing. As the principal boy, Miss St. Clare is very attractive, and sings her songs merrily. That inimitable comedian, J. W. -Hall, in the role of Will Atkins,. does not detract from Ids previous good reputation, and his local skits are very keenly appreciated. The remainder of the company are first class. - •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>"CINDERELLA" DUE TO ARRIVE,</head>
        <p>"CINDERELLA" DUE TO ARRIVE,</p>
        <p>There is every probability that "Cinderella,"' SS presented by Mr. Redfern's company, will meet with a well-merited reception when it appears at the Royal Court Theatre next week. The piece abounds with humour, the scenic effects are gorgeous, as can be gathered when it is noted that there are nine scenes in this two- houses-a-night production.- The dressing has received special attention, and the fairy coach of our childish dreams will be practically a blaze of fairy lights, for it has been equipped with 1,000 electric lamps, and will be drawn by four Shetland ponies. The best of this season's Pantomime songs will be sung; and, together with the dancing, the show should be most acceptable. Miss Winnie Victoria will be the principal boy, and with her songs and dancing she should make a hit in Warrington, as she has already done in Manchester, Oldham, Birken- head &amp;c. - .</p>
        <p>Harry Firth's eight dainty maids will accom- pany the production.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>TIP-TOP VARIETY AT HIPPODROME. Skilful Gymnasts and Witty Comedians.</head>
        <p>TIP-TOP VARIETY AT HIPPODROME. Skilful Gymnasts and Witty Comedians.</p>
        <p>An -All star" - - - company of variety artistes formed by the Five are pleasing large "houses at the Hippodrome Wartons, who are daring this week. Wonders in acrobatics are Per- spring bar acrobats. The Brothers Webb ap- pear in a merry ange of melody and mirth. Harry Linden and Ida Berridge give a pleasing entertainment with songs, mel Witty bits, and piano- forte selections, all presented in the best man- ner. Adam Tomlinson is a dialect comedian with an abundance of clever Patter. Arthur in Ferris pays a welcome return visit, and is as popular as over. The Three Sisters Eston appear with success a graceful and daring triple trapeze act, and the other turns are I excellent.</p>
        <p>"THE BAILIFF 'I TAKES POSSESSION AT _ THE HIPPODROME NEXT WEEK.</p>
        <p>Fred Karno's name is associated with bur- lesques of character, and it is stated that ahevras af Yculinineved his greatest success in "The Bailiff"—a production he will bring to Warrington Hippodrome next week. The com- pany will include Albert Bruno, who is no stranger to Warrington audiences. Be "caught on" some time ago with his excruciatingly funny representations of "Perkins"—that hero of the "The lleid o' the noose." Other artistes ' burlesque. The programme also includes Mrs. will be: Sinclair's Three Diamonds, Violet Jay. Graerne Goring and Co. in their celebrated Scotch comedy sketch, Jules Kiaro, J. A. Wil- son, and Harris Coonie.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>PIOTUREDROME.</head>
        <p>PIOTUREDROME.</p>
        <p>- - - The feature of next week's programme at the Picturedrome will be the biosoopie presentation of "Moths," adapted from the work of "Ouida.' This picture will be shown on Thursday, Friday and Saturday, whilst the premier film for the first part of the week will be "The Way of the World," a realistic drama of modern life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>NEW PICTURE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>NEW PICTURE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>The m - - auagement of the New Picture House deserve congratulations for procuring such an attraction for next week as "London by Night," showing how London lives. This sensational drama will be shown here for the whole a next week. A film descriptive of the "Life of 'David Copporfield" will he shown here shortly.</p>
        <p>PARK PICTURE PALACE. There is an enchanting programme booked for next week at the Park Picture Palace, when the two principal pictures will be "The Woman Thou Gayest Me" and "The Vicar of Wake- field." "T Lost Chord" will be shown on Monday. Tuesday and Wednesday, and is an he adaptation of the lyrics introducing Sir Arthur Sullivan's world-famous music.</p>
        <p>Not If He Could Arrange It. "Indeed,' declared Mrs. Henpeck, " f guess I have just as good a chance of going to heaven as you have." " Not if I get there first," replied her husband.</p>
        <p>Conclusive. The story is told of an old Ayrshire farmer who had a habit of feigning deafness when he wanted to avoid answering an awkward ques- tion. One day a neighbour said to him: "I'd like to borrovv yOur cart this morning; mine is having a spring mended!' "You'll have to speak louder," the old farmer answered. "I don't hear very well—and I don't like to fend my cart, anyhow."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <p>"ROUGHING IT IN CANADA.</p>
        <p>Rejoinder by a Warringtonian Who</p>
        <p>_ "Came Through."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>COLD BUT HEALTHY.</head>
        <p>COLD BUT HEALTHY.</p>
        <p>On the 10th inst. we published a letter from Canada, in which the writer drew a rather doleful picture of the conditions in which he had lived since he went to British Columbia, a short time ago. That letter has elicited the following reply from a Warrington-Canad ian now on a visit home:—</p>
        <p>TO TUE EDITOR OF "THE EXAMINER:" Sir,—Being a consistent reader of your paper and a Warringtonian, I have great pleasure in writing to you concerning the letter which was in your paper of January .10th, sent by an old Warringtonian from Western 'Canada. I was very much struck by the extraordinary way he put Canada down. I have just come over after two years' experience in the West. and I don't think he should have called it in the way he did. Of course, as you know. Canada has good and bad points, just the same as this country, or any other country. I don't know what we ahould do without it, not only for wheat, but for other things. The top and bottom about it is he has lost heart. I have been like that myielf, but I gave ray mind to it, and I came through all right.</p>
        <p>By writing that letter he will no doubt dis- hearten a lot who had intentions of going out there in the coming spring; they will be seared to go lest they get in the same position se he is in. But as regards getting back if they (buil like it, I always found that a very easy matter indeed. Of course, some people go out i with the idea that they can pick money up n the street, but that is not so. It is just the same as here; you have to work for it. But you get more out there and you gain a lot of knowledge about the country. I will never regret the day I set sail from Liverpool, as it has done me the world of good. I ata looking forward to the day I start on my retnrn The weather we hays here is very unhealthy, damp and cold. The one fault about Canada is the long winter. We have on an average about five months of very cold weather, but I think it is a lot healthier than this, as it is • very dry cold, every day being the same, whereas here we have spring, summer, autumn and winter all in a week. I think the readers of this very popular paper will agree with me. Last winter we had a week steady of 56 below zero, but on an average we get about 35 or 4C but I don't feel it as much as I do this here. I think that some day, when your correspond- ent gets on his feet, he will regret ever writing such a letter. Hoping this short letter will be a little interesting to all. G.A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>Caustic, Reply to the Co-operative Store Yarn.</head>
        <p>A Caustic, Reply to the Co-operative Store Yarn.</p>
        <p>LARGE LIBERAL AUDIENC DELIGHTED.</p>
        <p>Quite a variety of political topics were dealt with in a lucitl manner at a well-attended meet- ing held under the auspices of the Warrington Junior Liberal Association in the Co-operative Hall on Monday evening. The question of re- ducing the national expenditure on armaments was dealt with by Mr. A. G. C. Harvey, M.P. for Rochdale, who suggested that the Govern- ment should pause for a year or two in expend- ing money in this direction. Mr. F. M. Oliver, who is a favourite political orator at Warring- ton, and Mr. E. Wynne Jones roused the enthu- siasm of the audience in denouncing local Con- servatives, Mr. Jones giving a caustio criticism of the deputation sent by Lady Greenall to Ulster. This part of the speech will be found under the special article on the Ulster deputa- tion.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Griffiths presided and he was sup- ported by the speakers named and Mrs. Osborne, Messrs. E. Lloyd Jones, E. A. Sanders, G. Toulmin, R. Jones, B. Williams, E. T. frughes, and R. Turner. _</p>
        <p>Miss Mary Sutherland, of llrmston, pleased the audience with her tasteful rendering •4 well-chosen songs. The CHAIRMAN said there was one question at the present time which should give alarm to all good Liberals. He was referring to the question of armaments. As a member of the rank and file of Liberalism, and speaking for himself, he was somewhat alarmed at the enormous expenditure the country was involved in at the present time so far as armaments were concerned, lie was glad to know that there was a gathering of a strong opinion on the part of Liberals against this expenditure. Radical ,as he was, he did not for one moment want it ' to bp understood that the defence of the coun- try, `so far as the first line of defence was con- cerned, should in any way be imperilled. He did not think there was anyone present who would jeopardise the safety of the country for one moment, but they thought, when the ex- penditure on the defences was amounting to .R 75,000,000 this year it was time for all good Liberals to be alarmed, and consider the ques- tion as far as they were able. They must also remember that with £24,000,000 interest in an- nual charge on the National Debt, there was an expenditure of -09,000,000—which was more than half of the revenue of the country—on war material. The European countries would find, if they went on spending money at the rate they were, that they would not have sufficient to meet the growing expenditure. •</p>
        <p>THE ROCHDALE M.P.'s VIEWS. Mr. iiARVIST said that the expenditure on armaments could not give the same gratification or delight to those who found the taxes, as the spending of the money on commerce or social reforms. They were agitating in the country to endeavour to rouse public opinion so that if possible some way out of the state of things to which we and other nations had arrived at, could be found, although he believed we bore our burdens more easily than other countries, thanks to Free Trade. (Hear, hear, and ap- plause.) Those who were trying to formulate public opinion would not allow themselves to be called disloyal Liberals. They were nothing of the kind. (Hear, hear.) They wanted to find some way out of the waste of money that was going on at the present time; they used no arguments to endeavour to induce the people of the country to reduce tho means of the national safety. (Hear, hear.) That must be the first consideration, but what they said was that .the increase of late years, founded as it had been on mistaken data—he referred to the scare of 1909, when, according to Mr. Balfour, Germany had 23 Dreadnoughts, when she had only six—had given them a huge supremacy, and they made this moderate demand, "That our Government should stand still for a year or two where they are at present." (Hear, hear.) They were not asking them to reduce the power of the fleet, they were asking them to pause and consider, in the hope that when a year or two of deliberation had gone, there might be a great council of nations, which would decide to do away with the oppression of the people as far as was possible. It was oppression. They were told the rich man paid, but it was the working classes who suffered. He was an Englishman like the Tories, he loved his country, respected its power, and honoured its history. He looked to the future being greater than ever before, and to England to guide the other nations of the earth in the ways of sanity. prosperity and general friendship. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>"THE SMITH CO-OPERATIVE STORES." Mr. P. M. Oliver Trounces Mr. Harold Smith. Metaphorically speaking, Mr. Harold Smith was given a very severe trouncing by the elo- quent Mr. P. M. Oliver. The member was dragged through his Government Co-operative Stores and was not allowed to gaze on his so- called shop-soiled Liberal goods, but was taken behind into the musty storerooms, where the results of Tory ingenuity lay rotting away. Some of the Tory goods had become so dilapi- dated that they had been thrown away with the rubbish. Mr. Oliver explained to this creator of the G.C.S. that the reason some of the Liberal goods were " shop soiled" was because of the "dog in the manger" policy of the Tory party:._</p>
        <p>Mr. OLIVER said: I am going to deal with a few remarkable speeches delivered by your Member, Mr. Harold Smith—(laughter)—on the .7th of January. and I hope,.in doing so, I shall be able to exercise somewhat more restraint and dis- play somewhat more the courtesy of debate than we received from him.</p>
        <p>The Simile. First of all he commenced with • simile, for which I thank him. It is a simile which I in- tend to carry out myself. He likened the nation to a great co-operative stores, in which all the people were shareholders. I venture to say that is a true simile, and again I thank him for it. We are a great co-operative stores, in which all are shareholders. We are ruled and governed by a board of directors, and we, being shareholders, want to see what our directors are doing. But in this we are a co-operative stores and not a limited liability company. And why do I mention that? In a well-known work on co-operation I read, No bona-fide co-eperative society allows any of its members more than one rote, no matter what his shareholding may be." If we are -- ce-operative society, I hope Mr. Harold Smithwill remember his speech of lanuary 7th when the Plural Voting Bill comes up. (Ai") lau-.e)n He goes on to say we have turned th-econce-t:n into a penny bazaar. Well, so long as you get a pennyworth at a penny bazaar, it is an admir- able institution. (Hear, hear.) "Filthy Rubbish."</p>
        <p>-;-••• At another meeting he said we were dispens- ing filthy rubbish. It was not the only time that evening he used the word "filthy." It is not a nice adjective, and I hope he will not use it so frequently as he does. It does not show much imagination, and when it is used more than once it shows much less taste. (Hear, hear.) As regards the rubbish, I should like Mr. Smith to go round the schools when the feeding of necessitous school children is taking place, and it will be quite easy for him to find a school, for every year there are 16,0000 meals provided. It will be quite easy for Mr. Smith to chance on one, and I would like him, away from the glamour of a political platform, to go and tell them that what they Sr. receiving is filthy rub- bish. Then I would like him to go to one of the workshops where women carry on the mak- ing of paper boxes, and go to Bradley Heath, where women make chains, and any of the other works which come under the Trades Boards Act, and tell them that the better conditions they worked under and the better wages they received were filthy rubbish. Let Mr. Smith go and stand—in the rain pre- ferably—outside the Post Office and tell the old age pensioners that what they were receiving was lthy rubbish. Mr. Harold Smith is the member for Warrington, and presumably he knows something about the condition of War- , ringtea.</p>
        <p>were 348 old people over 70 years of age in re- ceipt of out-door relief. On January Ist last year that 348 had gone down to nine. (Ap- plause.) In this town alone we had saved 339 old people from the stain of pauperism. If Mr. Harold Smith has the time to spare from Wal- ton Hall--(laughter)--and if he will go to those people and tell them they have been in receipt of filthy rubbish, well! the door might be shut in his face. (Laughter and applause.) When he talks about filthy rubbish his mind is still whirling round the Insurance Act. He had a good deal to say about that. He said the Gov- ernment had deluded a certain and even dimin- ishing section of the British public by the rare and refreshing fruit and promises of 9d. for 4d. Needless to say, this was greeted with laughter. It is really remarkable how old jokes go down at Conservative meetings. One would have thought they would have got tired of that. He said it had already opened its branches, but no member of the British public had touched it. I am amazed Mr. Smith should make a state- ment like that anywhere, and even more amazed that he should make it in Warrington, where the National Insurance Act is appreciated. (Hear, hear.) Warrington's Appreciation.</p>
        <p>It appreciates the Act more than any other town in England, except, perhaps, Wigan. Warrington and Wigan may nut be beautiful places, yet they have appreciated the Insurance Act, and why? The National Insurance Act is a great measure of co-operation, such as .11r. Smith says the whole of our national life is, and if that Act is appreciated you will find out where by the numbers joining approved socie- ties. (hear, hear.) If you find in any town that large numbers have joined approved societies and few people have joined the Post Office, you will understand that the people there have understood the bene- fits of brotherhood, mutual help and co-opera- tion. (Applause.) In Warrington there are , 24,942 members of approved societies, and there are only 339 in the Post Office. That means to say that out of every 1,000 people coming under the National Insurance Act, 986 are in approved societies, and only 14 have drifted into the Post Office. There is only Wigan that exceeds that record. We in Lancashire understand co-opera- tion. (Applause.) A Pearson and Knowles Example.</p>
        <p>I will now refer to a large well-known firm in Lancashire, the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., which is presided over by Sir liar- mood Banner, a Unionist member of Parlia- ment, who, at the annual meeting, told the shareholders that the National Insurance Act had cost that company .26,180. Now that .26,180 which has gone from the company has gone somewhere, it has not vanished into thin air. It has been transferred from the company into the coffers of the approved societies for the bene- fit of the workers. (Hear, hear.) Where would that money have gone if the Act had not come into operation? Would it have come to the workers. ("No.") If the workers had wanted it they would have had to get it, the company would not have given it with a free hand. The Chairman said that if they had not had to pay that money under the Act it would have meant an increase of one per cent. in their dividend. That is where the money would have gone, and therefore, in this case, one fact is worth a deal of theory. You have a specific instance, for the Chairman admits that the money\ has been transferred from the dividends to the coffers of the workers. (Hear, hear.) "Shop Soiled Goods."</p>
        <p>I want to pass on from some of this " filthy rubbish," to consider what Mr. Harold Smith calls our shop-soiled goods, which they (the Liberals) have .attempted to thrust on the people</p>
        <p>for many years past. Disestablishment am admit they are shop</p>
      </div>
