        <p>_ the window for a long time; but in the window the Conservatives have not allowed their goods time to get shop soiled. You may remember a little article called food taxes. They did not</p>
        <p>let that get shop soiled, because when they found the people did not like it they took it out of the window. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) They had also Imperial Preference, which was a kind of court sticking plaster, which you bought in a little packet of red, white and blue, and it was for sticking the Empire together. (Laughter.) We walked down the streets of Warrington and saw this Tariff Reform Im- perial Preference sticking plaster, and as we knew the Empire did not need sticking together. we did not buy it, and so Imperial Preference was not allowed to get shop soiled, it was la ken out of the window. (Laughter.) We walked down a little bit later and saw thei had Pro- tection in the window, which was a kind of hot- house.</p>
        <p>" A Hot House." It is a hot-house fox forcing British industry, and was made in Germ, and having no need for that we passed on the other side. The next time we came down the street the blind was don't know what is in the down. (Laughter.) I window now, because they won't allow us to see it. They have got something in. no doubt They are still sending round their. c( oLmaumgehrteei g (Laughter.) travellers, are instructed to about their w theilli.o chief constructive say lank but to do nothing ht abuse an mis- p. nothin represent the other side. Now uct you know that if a commercial traveller tries to push his mer- chandise by abusing, libelling and misrepresent- ing other people, be very the dock—(hear, , liear soon finds himself in a aerierai Election and )—and when we come to the Conservatives find themselves before the tribunal of public opinion they will be sent to another five years' hard labour on the Opposition bench. (Hear, hear, and applause.) So much for their goods, which' they have net all owed to get shop soiled. I don't think it lie, in their month to abuse us for keeping_e. our goods in the window so long, beeens, th House of Lords have not allowed us to pass them.</p>
        <p>..atistics of the Sunday Sens. these are counted, and sometimes the officials omit them, according to their individual opinion. There are 3.135 ministers connected with Con- gregational churches in Great Britain and Ire- land. Of this Number 204 are temporarily withoour other ro I work, 37 are occupied ia accretarial work, and 429, by reason of age or iii_h„ith, have retired from pastoral duty. pastoral feessi charge, 91 are engaged in tutorial Seventy-one ministers died during the past year at the average age of sixty-eight years, to that there is no reason to doubt that the minis- terial office in the Congregational churches pro- vides a healthy occupation. During the year thirteen ministers left far other denominations, and three ministero entered the Cangregational churches from other churches.</p>
        <p>Twelve colleges and institutes for ministe: training belong to Congregationalists in Gx</p>
