      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-01-24_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>NOT CHRISTIAN ENOUGH.</head>
        <p>NOT CHRISTIAN ENOUGH.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan President's New Year Message. In the course of his New Year massage to the members of the Wesleyan Methodist Church, the Rev. Samuel F. Collier, the president, refers to "the surging unrest and ominous. discon- tent" of the last year. Tie thinks this ought not to be met with suspicidn or resentment, but with an earnest and sympathetic attempt to understand the lives of the people, the causes of complaint, and the reasonableness or unreason- ableness of their demands, with a view to find- ing a true and permanent settlement. he says, is the higher standard of have through education and civil- and the fact that this standard has life ente h a t cause, we irsiasetin°llo.ut of all proportion to the corresponding rise in wages. It is not that the people are ' worse off, but that their ideas of what life ought to be have improved, and their expectations accordingly enlarged. There is some satisfac- tion that never, OA now. were men so willing to acknowledge that wrongs existed, and never wore so many leaders of the churchea, the State, the working classes, philanthropists and sociolo- gists all busily engaged in trying to understand and remedy the evils. These social problems are with us simply becauso we have not been Christian enough as • nation; and government by the principles of Jesus Christ would have prevented their verY existence. Christians must take their share in the work of social reform, and see to it that the improvement shall be moral and spiritual as well as material. A much larger number of the Methodist Church and congregation arer engaged in the service of humanity outside the programme of their Church than is generally known.</p>
        <p>Mr. Collier proceeds to ask whether these workers are looked upon as fellow workers by their co-religionists, or are they looked upon with suspicion, as if unspiritual. For want of proper recognitioa many of those earnest workers become estranged from the inner life of the Cituroh. Wiwi' worker. should be reeog- aised, enrolled, arid linked ion ta the Churok organisation.</p>
      </div>
