        <p>FOOTBALL STRIKE AT Mi END</p>
        <p>The romantic footballers' strike at Warring- ton is settled. So ran the welcome message last night. Mr. llciughton, the well-known N.U. official, met the players and the committee, and the result was a happy settlement. It was officially stated that the players had waived their claim for an extra .R 1 in the Christmas box. George Thomas, interviewed last night on behalf of the players, said: "The whole trouble is now at an end. The men have lost, and we accept our defeat in a true sporting spirit, and I hope there will be no more friction." TEAMS FOR TO-DAY. First Team, at Wilderspool—Jolley; Waywell, Dickenson, Tranter, Ruddick; Nicholas, Young; Cox, Harmer, Skelhorne, Clare, Thomas, Fearu- ley. Bradshaw and Daintith, reserves. Ches- ters indisposed. Second Team—Gardner; Baker, Daintith, Shaw, Shakeshaft; Douglas, Brookes; Naylor, Boardman, Bevan; Critchley,- Green, Arnold. COMMITTEE'S STATEMENT CON- VINCES MEMBERS. With Practical Unanimity the Meeting Approves of the Officials' Action. PLAYERS SAID TO BE GENEROUSLY TREATED. The strike, the causes which led up to it, and the bonus question, were vigorously thrashed out at a 1.000 strong meeting of the Warrington Football Club members in the Co-operative Hall</p>
